"use strict";
/* Technique clinic content: proper technique with the reason for every step, errors, rescues, evidence and error-spotting scenarios. */
const TECH=[{"id":"anterior-rhinoscopy","name":"Anterior rhinoscopy","group":"Ear, nose and eye","signs":[],"purpose":"Answers what is in the front half of the nasal cavity: the bleeding point in epistaxis, a septal haematoma after trauma, a polyp versus a swollen turbinate in obstruction, a foreign body, or a septal perforation. Reach for it in every epistaxis, nasal trauma, unilateral blockage or discharge, and before prescribing a nasal steroid.","kit":["Head light (or head mirror and lamp) so both hands are free and the light is coaxial with your eyes","Thudichum speculum (adult) or the largest otoscope speculum that sits in the vestibule as an alternative","Otoscope with a large speculum for children and when no Thudichum is available","Jobson Horne probe or a cotton-tipped applicator for the sensitivity and mobility test","Topical decongestant with or without local anaesthetic (e.g. xylometazoline or co-phenylcaine spray) to shrink mucosa when the view is poor","Gloves and a mask or eye protection if there is active bleeding"],"setup":[{"do":"Seat the patient upright on a chair with the back of the head supported, facing you, knees to one side of yours, at your eye level.","why":"The nasal floor runs straight back, so your line of sight must be horizontal to look along it; head support stops the patient pulling away when the speculum touches the vestibule."},{"do":"Position the head light so the beam is centred where you are looking, 25 to 35 cm from the nose.","why":"The vestibule is a narrow tunnel; a light that is off-axis from your eyes throws the ala's shadow over exactly the area you want to see. A head light keeps one hand for the speculum and one for the head or a probe."},{"do":"Ask about anticoagulants, recent trauma, nasal surgery and cocaine use, and tell the patient it will feel odd but should not hurt.","why":"These change what you are looking for (haematoma, perforation, bleeding risk with probing), and a relaxed patient does not flinch into the speculum."}],"steps":[{"do":"Before any instrument, inspect the external nose from the front and from above, then lift the tip of the nose with your thumb on the nasal tip, pushing it gently upwards and backwards.","why":"Tip elevation opens the vestibule and shows the anterior septum and Little's area (Kiesselbach's plexus, anteroinferior septum, about 1 cm behind the columella) without any instrument. It is often the whole examination in a child and the best view of the commonest bleeding site.","cue":"You can see the vestibular skin, the columella, and the pale pink anterior septum on both sides; prominent vessels or a crust on the anterior septum are visible."},{"do":"Hold the Thudichum speculum in your non-dominant hand, with your index finger resting on the patient's nose or cheek and the speculum's spring in the palm. Use the dominant hand to steady the head or hold a probe.","why":"Bracing a finger on the patient's face means the speculum moves with the head, so a sudden movement does not drive the blades into the septum. The non-dominant hand holds it so the skilled hand is free for the probe or suction.","cue":"The speculum feels like an extension of your hand resting on the face, and you can let go with the other hand without the blades shifting."},{"do":"Insert the closed blades about 1 cm into the vestibule only, angled slightly upwards along the line of the nostril, then open them gently so the lateral blade lifts the ala outwards and upwards while the medial blade only rests near the septum.","why":"The vestibule is hair-bearing skin and is the only part that tolerates pressure; going deeper presses on mucosa and the septum, which is painful and makes Little's area bleed. The opening you need comes from moving the mobile ala, not from pushing the rigid septum.","cue":"The nostril opens into an oval, the patient is comfortable, and there is no blanching or bleeding on the septum."},{"do":"With the head upright, look along the floor of the nose: identify the septum medially and the inferior turbinate laterally, then follow the inferior meatus backwards as far as the view allows.","why":"With the head upright your view is parallel to the hard palate and you see the lower half of the cavity: the inferior turbinate, which is the commonest swelling mistaken for a polyp, and the floor where foreign bodies lodge.","cue":"You see a pink, smooth, rounded inferior turbinate arising from the lateral wall and running backwards, with a dark air space between it and the septum."},{"do":"Tilt the patient's head back about 30 degrees (support it with your free hand) and look again to see the middle turbinate, the middle meatus and the upper septum.","why":"The nasal cavity is tall; extending the head brings its upper part into the line of sight. The middle meatus is where most nasal polyps arise (from the ethmoid sinuses) and where purulent sinus discharge appears.","cue":"You see a second, smaller turbinate above and behind the inferior one, and the slit of the middle meatus under it."},{"do":"Assess the septum systematically: is it in the midline, is there a spur or deviation, is there a swelling on one or both sides, is there a hole.","why":"Deviation explains unilateral obstruction; a bilateral soft swelling after trauma is a septal haematoma until proved otherwise; a perforation explains crusting, whistling and recurrent bleeding and has a differential that matters (granulomatosis with polyangiitis, cocaine, previous surgery, digital trauma).","cue":"A straight septum lies parallel to the light; a perforation shows as a defect whose edge is often crusted and through which light from the other side may be seen."},{"do":"If there is a swelling, test it: touch it gently with the probe and ask whether the patient can feel it; note whether it moves on its stalk, whether it is firm or soft, and what colour it is. If unsure, spray decongestant, wait 5 minutes and look again.","why":"A turbinate is covered with sensate, vascular mucosa: it is tender, pink to red, firm and fixed, and shrinks with a vasoconstrictor. A polyp is oedematous mucosa with few nerve endings and little vascular erectile tissue: it is insensitive, grey and glistening, soft, mobile, and does not shrink.","cue":"A turbinate flinches, blanches and indents slightly; a polyp is painless to touch and swings a little on its pedicle."},{"do":"Press a septal swelling gently with the probe or a cotton bud and compare both sides of the septum.","why":"A septal haematoma is blood between perichondrium and cartilage; it is soft and compressible and usually bilateral. Cartilage gets its nutrition from the perichondrium, so untreated haematoma leads to necrosis, abscess and saddle nose within days.","cue":"Haematoma is fluctuant, dusky red or purple, and the probe sinks in; a deviated septum or spur is firm and covered by normal-coloured mucosa."},{"do":"Repeat on the other side, then check airflow: hold a cold metal tongue depressor or mirror under the nostrils and look at the misting, or occlude each nostril in turn while the patient sniffs gently.","why":"The other side may hold the cause (a contralateral deviation, a second polyp), and an airflow check tells you whether the anatomy you saw is actually obstructing.","cue":"Two roughly equal misting patches on the metal; unequal or absent misting on one side matches the obstruction you saw."}],"normal":"Vestibule with intact skin and fine hairs. Septum near midline, smooth, pale pink mucosa, no prominent vessels or crusts on Little's area. Inferior turbinate pink, smooth, moist, sensitive to touch, with a clear air space between it and the septum. Middle turbinate and meatus visible with head extended, no pus or polyps. Equal misting bilaterally.","abnormal":[{"finding":"Prominent vessels, a crust or a bleeding point on the anteroinferior septum","means":"Little's area bleed, the usual source of anterior epistaxis; suitable for silver nitrate cautery (one side at a time) or a nasal cream."},{"finding":"Bilateral, soft, fluctuant, dusky septal swelling after nasal trauma","means":"Septal haematoma: same-day ENT for incision and drainage to prevent septal abscess and cartilage necrosis (saddle nose)."},{"finding":"Grey, glistening, translucent, insensitive, mobile swelling arising from the middle meatus, bilateral","means":"Nasal polyps (chronic rhinosinusitis with polyps); consider aspirin-exacerbated respiratory disease if asthmatic. In a child, consider cystic fibrosis."},{"finding":"Unilateral polyp or mass, especially with bleeding, crusting, pain or a fleshy or friable appearance","means":"Not a simple polyp until proved otherwise: inverted papilloma or malignancy in adults; in a child, encephalocele or glioma, so do not probe or biopsy; refer urgently."},{"finding":"Septal perforation","means":"Previous surgery, digital trauma, cocaine or other intranasal drug use, granulomatosis with polyangiitis, sarcoidosis, syphilis or tuberculosis; check urinalysis and ANCA if no clear cause."},{"finding":"Unilateral foul or purulent discharge in a child, with a visible object or very oedematous mucosa on one side","means":"Nasal foreign body; if a button battery or magnets are possible, treat as an emergency because of rapid septal necrosis."}],"errors":[{"error":"Pushing the speculum deep and opening the blades against the septum.","result":"Pain, a flinch that pushes the blades further, and fresh bleeding from Little's area that obscures the view.","fix":"Keep the blades in the vestibule (about 1 cm), and open them so the movement lifts the ala laterally; brace your index finger on the face."},{"error":"Looking only with the head upright.","result":"Missed middle meatus polyps, pus and upper septal pathology.","fix":"Always look twice: head upright for the floor and inferior turbinate, then head extended about 30 degrees for the middle turbinate and meatus."},{"error":"Calling a swollen inferior turbinate a polyp.","result":"Wrong diagnosis, unnecessary referral, or a probe test that never happens.","fix":"Probe it (sensate and firm = turbinate), note it arises from the lateral wall low down, and decongest and re-look if still unsure: a turbinate shrinks, a polyp does not."},{"error":"Using a hand-held pen torch held away from your line of sight.","result":"The ala's shadow covers the view; you see the vestibule and nothing beyond.","fix":"Use a head light, or an otoscope with a large speculum, so the light is coaxial with your eye."},{"error":"Skipping the septum in a trauma patient because the external nose looks fine.","result":"Missed septal haematoma, which can occur without an external deformity.","fix":"In every nasal injury, specifically inspect both sides of the septum and press any swelling with a cotton bud."},{"error":"Probing or biopsying a unilateral mass in a child.","result":"Possible CSF leak or meningitis if it is an encephalocele.","fix":"Look only, note pulsation or enlargement with crying (Furstenberg sign), and refer for imaging first."}],"trouble":[{"problem":"I can't see past the vestibule.","fix":"Check the light is on your line of sight; lift the nasal tip; insert the speculum a little further but still within the vestibule and open it laterally; if the mucosa is swollen, spray decongestant, wait 5 minutes and look again."},{"problem":"It is bleeding so much I can't see anything.","fix":"Sit the patient forward, ask them to blow out clots, apply firm pressure on the soft part of the nose for 10 to 15 minutes, then apply a vasoconstrictor and local anaesthetic on cotton wool for a few minutes before looking again with suction if available. If there is no anterior source and bleeding continues, think posterior epistaxis."},{"problem":"I can't tell if the swelling is a polyp or a turbinate.","fix":"Probe it for sensation, note its origin (inferior turbinate arises low on the lateral wall, polyps mostly from the middle meatus), its colour and mobility, then decongest and reassess; if still unsure or unilateral, refer for nasendoscopy."},{"problem":"The child won't tolerate an instrument.","fix":"Use tip-of-nose elevation and an otoscope with the largest speculum while the child sits on a parent's lap with the parent holding the forehead against their chest; most anterior foreign bodies and bleeding points are visible this way."}],"pearls":["Tip-of-nose elevation alone shows Little's area, the source of most nosebleeds, so do it first and you may not need a speculum.","Conventions for holding the Thudichum vary between textbooks (some teach the dominant hand); what matters is a braced finger on the face and opening against the ala, not the septum.","Bilateral polyps in an adult are common and benign; a unilateral polyp is a red flag that needs nasendoscopy.","Cottle's test (pulling the cheek laterally) that relieves obstruction points to the nasal valve rather than the septum or turbinates.","Cauterise only one side of the septum at a session to avoid perforation from bilateral ischaemia."],"evidence":[{"stat":"The large majority (around 90%) of nosebleeds arise from Little's area on the anterior septum, which is why anterior rhinoscopy with tip elevation finds most bleeding points.","src":"NICE CKS: Epistaxis"},{"stat":"Anterior rhinoscopy shows only the front of the nose; middle meatus and posterior disease, including posterior polyps and tumours, need nasendoscopy.","src":"Scott-Brown's Otorhinolaryngology 8e"}],"document":"Normal: Anterior rhinoscopy (head light, Thudichum): septum midline, no haematoma or perforation, Little's area clean, inferior turbinates pink and not hypertrophied, no polyps or discharge, equal misting. Abnormal: Anterior rhinoscopy R: bleeding point with prominent vessels on R Little's area; no septal haematoma. L: grey, insensitive, mobile polyp in middle meatus. Silver nitrate cautery to R Little's area, haemostasis achieved.","hook":"Lift the tip, stay in the vestibule, open against the ala, look low then high, and probe anything that bulges.","mcq":[{"q":"After a punch to the nose, a doctor sees bilateral smooth septal swellings, decides they are a deviated septum because there is no external deformity, and arranges routine follow-up. What should have been done?","opts":["Press the swellings with a cotton bud; soft fluctuant swelling is a septal haematoma needing same-day drainage","Order nasal bone X-rays before deciding","Prescribe a decongestant and review in a week","Reassure, as bilateral swelling excludes haematoma"],"a":0,"why":"Septal haematoma is usually bilateral, soft and fluctuant and can occur without external deformity. Untreated, the cartilage loses its perichondrial blood supply and necroses within days. X-rays do not change management."},{"q":"A doctor sees a pink, firm swelling low on the lateral nasal wall that the patient feels when touched with a probe. What is it most likely to be?","opts":["A nasal polyp","A septal spur","The inferior turbinate","An inverted papilloma"],"a":2,"why":"The inferior turbinate arises low on the lateral wall, is covered by sensate, vascular mucosa and is firm. Polyps are grey, glistening, insensitive and mobile and usually come from the middle meatus."},{"q":"A doctor inserts the Thudichum speculum 2 to 3 cm into the nostril and opens it towards the septum; the patient winces and the septum starts bleeding. What is the correct technique?","opts":["Use a smaller speculum and insert it further","Keep the blades in the vestibule about 1 cm, brace a finger on the face and open by lifting the ala laterally","Apply local anaesthetic and then open fully against the septum","Abandon rhinoscopy and refer for nasendoscopy"],"a":1,"why":"Only the vestibular skin tolerates pressure. Opening against the ala rather than the septum gives the view without trauma to Little's area."}],"src":["Scott-Brown's Otorhinolaryngology Head and Neck Surgery 8e","Macleod's Clinical Examination 15e","NICE CKS: Epistaxis","Talley and O'Connor's Clinical Examination 9e"]},{"id":"otoscopy","name":"Otoscopy","group":"Ear, nose and eye","signs":["otoscopy"],"purpose":"Answers what the ear canal and tympanic membrane look like and whether the membrane moves: otitis media with or without effusion, otitis externa, perforation, cholesteatoma, wax, foreign body or haemotympanum. Reach for it in ear pain, discharge, hearing loss, vertigo, facial palsy, fever in a child and head injury.","kit":["Otoscope with a fully charged battery (a yellow light makes a normal drum look red)","The largest speculum that fits comfortably in the outer canal, fresh disposable tip for each patient","Pneumatic bulb attachment and a speculum that seals the canal (or a soft-tipped speculum) for mobility testing","Magnifying lens closed and sealed on the otoscope head for pneumatic otoscopy"],"setup":[{"do":"Adult seated, head tilted slightly towards the opposite shoulder; young child sitting sideways on a parent's lap, one of the parent's arms around the child's arms and body, the other hand holding the child's head against the parent's chest.","why":"Tilting the head away aligns the canal with your line of sight; a child held firmly cannot jerk their head onto the speculum, which is the main cause of canal injury in children."},{"do":"Ask which ear is affected and examine the other ear first, using a fresh speculum or at least wiping it between ears.","why":"The unaffected ear gives you the patient's own normal for comparison and avoids carrying infection from a discharging ear."},{"do":"Inspect the pinna, the skin behind it, the mastoid and the canal entrance with the naked eye first; then gently pull the pinna and press the tragus.","why":"Pain on moving the pinna or tragus is a sign of otitis externa (the canal skin is stretched), which warns you to use a smaller speculum and to be gentle. Mastoid swelling, tenderness or a protruding ear points to mastoiditis, a postauricular scar to previous surgery."}],"steps":[{"do":"Hold the otoscope like a pen, between thumb and index finger close to the speculum, handle pointing down or forwards, and rest the ulnar side of your hand or your little finger on the patient's head or cheek.","why":"With your hand anchored on the head, any sudden movement of the patient moves the otoscope with them, so the speculum cannot be driven into the bony canal or the drum. A power grip on the handle with an unbraced arm is the classic cause of canal trauma.","cue":"Your hand is in contact with the patient's head the whole time the speculum is in the canal; if they turn, your hand moves too."},{"do":"Use the hand on the same side as the ear for the otoscope (right hand, right ear) and the other hand for the pinna; in an adult pull the pinna up and back (superiorly and posteriorly).","why":"The adult canal is S-shaped, about 2.5 cm long; its outer third is cartilage that runs upwards, backwards and inwards, the inner two-thirds bony running forwards and downwards. Pulling up and back straightens the cartilaginous part over the bony part so you see the drum down a straight tube.","cue":"The canal opens into a straight tunnel and the drum comes into view without having to angle the otoscope steeply."},{"do":"In an infant or young child pull the pinna back and slightly down (posteriorly and inferiorly); from early school age move to the adult up-and-back pull.","why":"In young children the bony canal is not yet developed, the canal is short and mostly cartilaginous, and it runs upwards from the meatus, so the straightening pull is back and down rather than up.","cue":"The drum comes into view at the end of a short canal; if you are looking at canal wall, change the direction of traction rather than pushing in."},{"do":"Choose the largest speculum that fits comfortably, and insert it only into the outer, cartilaginous third, under direct vision through the lens, angled slightly forwards and downwards towards the drum.","why":"A larger speculum gives a brighter, wider view and better seal for pneumatic testing. The bony canal is lined with thin skin directly on periosteum and is very sensitive; touching it hurts and can trigger a cough or vasovagal reflex through the auricular branch of the vagus (Arnold's nerve).","cue":"The patient does not wince or cough, and you can see canal skin moving past as you advance, never a blank wall."},{"do":"Inspect the canal on the way in: wax, discharge, debris, swelling, redness, exostoses, foreign body, vesicles.","why":"The canal tells you as much as the drum: debris and oedema mean otitis externa, vesicles mean herpes zoster (look for facial weakness), discharge may come from the middle ear through a perforation.","cue":"You can describe the canal skin colour and calibre and whether there is anything between you and the drum."},{"do":"Find the handle of the malleus first, then survey the drum systematically: lateral process at its upper end, umbo at its lower end, cone of light running anteroinferiorly from the umbo, pars tensa in four quadrants, and finally the pars flaccida (attic) above the lateral process.","why":"The malleus is the one fixed landmark and orients you: its handle points down and back towards the umbo. The drum sits tilted, facing down and forwards, so the light reflects back only from the anteroinferior quadrant: at about 5 o'clock in the right ear and 7 o'clock in the left. The attic is where retraction pockets and cholesteatoma begin and is the part most often missed.","cue":"You can name the lateral process, handle, umbo, light reflex and pars flaccida in that ear; a scan that misses the pars flaccida is incomplete."},{"do":"Describe the drum in four terms: position (neutral, retracted or bulging), colour (pearly grey, red, yellow, amber, blue), translucency (can you see middle-ear structures through it, or is it opaque or cloudy) and integrity (intact or perforated, with site).","why":"Position and translucency discriminate acute otitis media far better than colour: a bulging, cloudy drum is the strongest sign of acute otitis media, while redness alone occurs with crying, fever and attempts to remove wax. Retraction (prominent lateral process, foreshortened handle) points to Eustachian tube dysfunction.","cue":"You can state, for example: neutral, pearly grey, translucent, intact."},{"do":"Test mobility with pneumatic otoscopy: with a speculum that seals the canal, squeeze the bulb gently, then release, while watching the pars tensa.","why":"Positive pressure pushes a normal drum inwards and release lets it move outwards. Fluid or pus in the middle ear or a perforation stiffens or vents the system, so the drum moves little or not at all. This is the best bedside test for middle-ear effusion.","cue":"A normal drum flickers in and then out with a small squeeze; an effusion gives a sluggish or absent movement. If air escapes around the speculum (no seal), you will see nothing move and must change speculum or angle."},{"do":"Withdraw the speculum slowly under vision, then examine the other ear and compare.","why":"Pathology on the way out may be seen from a different angle, and comparison between ears is your best reference for subtle colour or position change.","cue":"You can state whether the two drums look the same, and if not, how they differ."}],"normal":"Pinna and mastoid normal, no tragal tenderness. Canal skin pink, small amount of wax, no discharge or swelling. Drum neutral position, pearly grey, translucent, intact; handle of malleus and lateral process clearly visible, light reflex anteroinferior; pars flaccida intact with no retraction or debris. Drum moves briskly in and out with pneumatic otoscopy.","abnormal":[{"finding":"Bulging, opaque or cloudy drum, often yellow or red, with loss of the malleus landmarks and reduced mobility","means":"Acute otitis media; bulging is the most specific sign."},{"finding":"Retracted or neutral, dull drum with amber or grey colour, fluid level or air bubbles, reduced mobility, no acute signs","means":"Otitis media with effusion (glue ear); check hearing, particularly in children."},{"finding":"Swollen, tender canal with debris or discharge, drum hard to see","means":"Otitis externa; check for fungal hyphae or spores (otomycosis), and in diabetics with severe pain and granulation tissue on the canal floor think necrotising otitis externa."},{"finding":"Retraction pocket or white keratin debris in the pars flaccida or posterosuperior quadrant, sometimes with foul discharge","means":"Cholesteatoma until proved otherwise; refer to ENT."},{"finding":"Hole in the pars tensa, through which the middle ear may be seen","means":"Perforation, from infection or trauma; keep the ear dry, avoid drops that are ototoxic if you cannot see a seal, and arrange follow-up to confirm healing."},{"finding":"Blue or dark red drum behind an intact membrane after head injury","means":"Haemotympanum, a sign of base-of-skull fracture; look for CSF otorrhoea or rhinorrhoea and Battle's sign."}],"errors":[{"error":"Holding the otoscope by the handle like a hammer with the hand not touching the patient.","result":"Canal trauma or drum injury if the patient, especially a child, moves suddenly.","fix":"Use a pen grip near the speculum and rest your little finger or the ulnar edge of your hand on the patient's head."},{"error":"Pulling the pinna up and back in an infant.","result":"The canal is not straightened; you see canal wall and push harder, causing pain.","fix":"In infants and young children pull back and slightly down; switch to up and back as the child grows."},{"error":"Using a speculum that is too small and pushing it deep into the bony canal.","result":"Pain, cough, bleeding, a tiny dim view, and no seal for pneumatic testing.","fix":"Use the largest speculum that fits in the outer third and stay in the cartilaginous canal."},{"error":"Diagnosing acute otitis media on redness alone.","result":"Overdiagnosis and antibiotic overuse: crying, fever and canal manipulation all redden the drum.","fix":"Base the diagnosis on bulging and opacity, with reduced mobility, plus acute symptoms."},{"error":"Not looking at the pars flaccida.","result":"Missed attic retraction pockets and cholesteatoma.","fix":"Always finish the survey above the lateral process of the malleus; angle the otoscope slightly upwards to see the attic."},{"error":"Examining with a weak battery.","result":"A yellow light makes the drum look red and dull, mimicking otitis media.","fix":"Check the light is bright white before you start; charge or replace batteries routinely."},{"error":"Pneumatic otoscopy with a speculum that does not seal.","result":"No movement seen, so a normal drum is wrongly called immobile.","fix":"Use a larger or soft-tipped speculum, angle to seal, and check that the closed lens is in place."}],"trouble":[{"problem":"I can only see the canal wall, not the drum.","fix":"Withdraw slightly, change the direction of traction (up and back in adults, back and down in children), tilt the patient's head further towards the opposite shoulder, and angle the speculum forwards and downwards; do not push deeper."},{"problem":"The canal is full of wax.","fix":"Do not force past it. If symptoms need a diagnosis now, try a different angle round the wax; otherwise use olive oil or sodium bicarbonate drops for a few days and re-examine, or remove with microsuction or a curette under direct vision if trained. Avoid irrigation if there is a known or possible perforation, previous ear surgery, or acute otitis externa."},{"problem":"I can't tell if there is a perforation or just a thin retracted segment.","fix":"Use pneumatic otoscopy (a perforation does not move and air escapes), look for middle-ear mucosa or discharge through the defect, and compare with the other ear; if still unsure, refer for microscopy or tympanometry."},{"problem":"The child is screaming and the drum looks red.","fix":"Redness is unreliable in a crying child. Focus on position (bulging or not) and translucency, and on mobility if you can obtain a seal; if you cannot judge, re-examine once the child is settled."}],"pearls":["The light reflex is not a sign of health: it can be present with effusion and absent in a normal drum with an unusual angle; position, translucency and mobility matter more.","Right ear light reflex at 5 o'clock, left at 7 o'clock: both point down and forwards towards the nose.","Do not insert anything, including drops or irrigation, into an ear with possible CSF leak after head injury; look only.","Some texts teach holding the otoscope in the same hand as the ear examined and some always in the dominant hand; either is correct if the hand is braced on the head.","If a facial palsy is present, look for vesicles in the canal and on the pinna (Ramsay Hunt syndrome) before calling it Bell's palsy."],"evidence":[{"stat":"For acute otitis media, a bulging drum has a positive LR of about 51, a cloudy drum about 34 and a distinctly immobile drum about 31; a distinctly red drum only about 8.","src":"Rothman et al 2003, JAMA (Rational Clinical Examination)"},{"stat":"Pneumatic otoscopy for middle-ear effusion: sensitivity about 94%, specificity about 80%.","src":"Takata et al 2003, Pediatrics"},{"stat":"The AAP guideline advises against diagnosing acute otitis media without middle-ear effusion confirmed by pneumatic otoscopy and/or tympanometry.","src":"Lieberthal et al 2013, Pediatrics (AAP guideline for acute otitis media)"}],"document":"Normal: Otoscopy: pinna and mastoid normal, no tragal tenderness. Canals clear. Both TMs neutral, pearly grey, translucent, intact, landmarks and pars flaccida normal, mobile on pneumatic otoscopy. Abnormal: R otoscopy: canal normal; TM bulging, opaque yellow, malleus landmarks lost, immobile on pneumatic testing; no perforation. L TM normal. Impression R acute otitis media.","hook":"Pen grip, braced hand, straighten the canal, find the malleus, and judge position and movement, not redness.","mcq":[{"q":"A doctor examines a 9-month-old, pulling the pinna up and back, sees only canal wall, and pushes the speculum further in. The baby screams. What should they have done?","opts":["Use a smaller speculum and push in more slowly","Pull the pinna back and slightly down, keep the speculum in the outer canal and brace the hand on the head","Abandon otoscopy and treat empirically","Tilt the head towards the examined ear"],"a":1,"why":"In infants the canal is short, mostly cartilaginous and runs upwards, so it straightens with a back-and-down pull. Pushing deeper only touches the sensitive canal wall."},{"q":"A crying 3-year-old with fever has red, neutral, translucent drums that move normally on pneumatic otoscopy. The doctor diagnoses bilateral acute otitis media. What is wrong?","opts":["Nothing, red drums in a febrile child are diagnostic","The doctor should have used a smaller speculum","Redness alone is unreliable; without bulging, opacity or reduced mobility, acute otitis media is unlikely","Pneumatic otoscopy cannot be used in children"],"a":2,"why":"Crying and fever redden the drum. Bulging, cloudiness and immobility carry the diagnostic weight; a neutral, translucent, mobile drum argues against acute otitis media."},{"q":"A doctor performs pneumatic otoscopy and sees no drum movement, then notices the speculum is loose in the canal. What is the best next step?","opts":["Change to a larger or soft-tipped speculum that seals and repeat","Record the drum as immobile, indicating effusion","Squeeze the bulb harder","Record a perforation"],"a":0,"why":"Without a seal, pressure escapes around the speculum and no drum will move. A false 'immobile' result leads to overdiagnosis of effusion."}],"src":["Rothman et al 2003, JAMA: Does this child have acute otitis media?","Macleod's Clinical Examination 15e","Lieberthal et al 2013, Pediatrics: The diagnosis and management of acute otitis media","Scott-Brown's Otorhinolaryngology 8e"]},{"id":"direct-ophthalmoscopy","name":"Direct ophthalmoscopy (fundoscopy)","group":"Ear, nose and eye","signs":["fundoscopy"],"purpose":"Answers what the optic disc, retinal vessels, retina and macula look like: papilloedema, optic atrophy, glaucomatous cupping, hypertensive and diabetic retinopathy, central retinal artery or vein occlusion. Reach for it in headache with red flags, raised ICP concern, sudden visual loss, hypertension or diabetes review, and unexplained neurological signs.","kit":["Direct ophthalmoscope with a fully charged battery, large white aperture for dilated pupils and small aperture for undilated pupils","Red-free (green) filter for haemorrhages, microaneurysms and nerve fibre layer","Tropicamide 0.5% or 1% drops if dilation is appropriate (short-acting, minimal cycloplegia)","Darkened room and a fixation target on the far wall"],"setup":[{"do":"Darken the room and seat the patient upright, ask them to remove glasses (contact lenses can stay in), and to fix on a distant target at their eye level over your shoulder.","why":"Darkness dilates the pupil, which widens your field of view through the keyhole. Distant fixation relaxes accommodation (no near-reflex miosis) and keeps the eye still, and their own lenses can be left out because the ophthalmoscope dial corrects spherical error."},{"do":"Decide about mydriasis: dilate with tropicamide when you need to see the macula or periphery or the pupils are small, unless pupil observation matters (head injury, decreasing consciousness, post-neurosurgery) or there is a known narrow-angle risk or iris-clipped lens implant. Warn about blurred vision, glare and not driving for several hours, and document the drug, eye(s) and time.","why":"Through a small pupil you see only about one disc at a time; dilation multiplies the view. But dilated pupils remove a key neurological sign, and in an eye with a shallow anterior chamber dilation can rarely precipitate acute angle closure. Documenting prevents a later observer misreading a dilated pupil as a neurological sign."},{"do":"Remove your own glasses, set the dial to your own correction (or 0 if you are emmetropic), and stand on the side of the eye you will examine: patient's right eye from their right side with the ophthalmoscope in your right hand at your right eye; left eye, left hand, left eye.","why":"Same hand and same eye means your head is beside the patient's head rather than face to face, so your noses do not collide and you do not block the fixation target with your head. Your own glasses keep you too far from the eye."}],"steps":[{"do":"Hold the ophthalmoscope vertically with the handle down and the head against your brow or cheek just beside your nose, index finger on the dial; put your other hand on the patient's forehead with the thumb gently lifting the upper lid.","why":"Fixing the instrument to your face means your eye, the aperture and the light move as one unit, so you keep the view while moving closer. The thumb on the forehead acts as a distance guide and keeps a drooping lid out of the way.","cue":"You can turn the dial with your index finger without the instrument shifting on your face."},{"do":"From arm's length (about 50 cm), with the dial at your own correction, shine the light into the pupil and look for the red reflex in both eyes.","why":"The red reflex is light reflected from the fundus; an opacity in the cornea, lens or vitreous casts a dark shadow on it. Checking at distance screens the media and tells you whether a poor fundal view later is due to opacity or to your technique.","cue":"A uniform orange-red glow fills the pupil; dark spots or spokes mean media opacity."},{"do":"Keep the red reflex in view and move in along a line about 15 degrees temporal to the patient's line of fixation, in the horizontal plane, until you are about 2 to 3 cm from the eye, your forehead almost touching your thumb.","why":"The optic disc lies about 15 degrees nasal to the fovea. Approaching from 15 degrees temporal brings the disc into your path first, and avoids shining into the macula, which causes miosis and glare. Because the field of view is only a few degrees (roughly one disc diameter or two through an undilated pupil), moving close is essential: the pupil is the keyhole and you must put your eye against it.","cue":"The red glow fills your view as you approach; retinal detail appears as you get to 2 to 3 cm."},{"do":"Focus by turning the dial: towards red (minus) numbers if the retina is blurred and the patient (or you) are myopic, towards green or black (plus) if hypermetropic. The correct setting is the combined refractive error of you and the patient.","why":"The ophthalmoscope lens neutralises the refractive error of both eyes in the light path; a short-sighted patient needs minus lenses, a long-sighted or aphakic patient plus lenses. Start near zero and dial slowly; the sharpest vessels mark the right setting.","cue":"Vessel walls look crisp with a central light reflex stripe on arterioles."},{"do":"If you land on retina but not the disc, find a vessel and follow it in the direction its branches point back to their parent: the Y-shaped forks open away from the disc, so travel towards the stem of the Y, against the arrowheads.","why":"All retinal vessels radiate from the disc, and the bifurcations always point away from it. Following vessels against the branching is a guaranteed route back to the disc wherever you land.","cue":"Vessels get thicker as you follow them, and the disc appears as a pale yellow-pink circle."},{"do":"Assess the disc systematically: colour, cup (vertical cup-to-disc ratio), margins, and the presence of spontaneous venous pulsation where the central veins cross the disc edge or dip into the cup; watch for 10 to 15 seconds.","why":"Colour: pallor means optic atrophy. Cup: enlargement, notching or asymmetry means glaucoma. Margins: blurring and elevation mean disc swelling. Spontaneous venous pulsation occurs when intraocular pressure briefly exceeds venous pressure in systole; when ICP is raised, the venous pressure rises and the pulsation disappears. Its presence makes raised ICP at that moment unlikely; its absence is common in normal people and proves nothing alone.","cue":"Normal: orange-pink disc, slightly paler temporally, cup central and small, sharp margins (nasal edge slightly less sharp), veins visibly pulsating at the disc edge in many people."},{"do":"Follow each of the four main vascular arcades outwards from the disc: superotemporal, inferotemporal, inferonasal, superonasal, asking the patient to look slightly in the direction of the quadrant you are examining for the periphery.","why":"A systematic route ensures you see arterioles (calibre, light reflex, arteriovenous nipping), veins (dilatation, beading), and the retina between them (haemorrhages, exudates, cotton wool spots, new vessels). Moving the patient's gaze rotates more peripheral retina into the keyhole.","cue":"You can describe arterioles as narrower and brighter than veins (about 2:3), with crossings and the retina in each quadrant."},{"do":"Examine the macula last: move about 2 disc diameters temporal to the disc, or ask the patient to look straight at the light.","why":"Looking at the light brings the fovea into view instantly but causes discomfort and pupillary constriction, which would ruin the rest of the examination, so it comes last. The macula is darker, avascular centrally, with a bright foveal reflex in young people.","cue":"A darker, vessel-free area with a pinpoint foveal light reflex."},{"do":"Repeat for the other eye using the other hand and your other eye, and compare discs.","why":"Asymmetry is the most sensitive indicator: unilateral disc swelling, pallor or cup enlargement is more easily recognised against the patient's other eye than against a textbook image.","cue":"You can say whether the two discs, cups and vessels are similar."}],"normal":"Red reflex bright and uniform bilaterally. Discs orange-pink, round or slightly vertically oval, with sharp margins (nasal margin slightly less distinct), central cup with vertical cup-to-disc ratio around 0.3 and symmetrical, spontaneous venous pulsation often visible. Arterioles narrower than veins, about 2:3, with a light reflex and no nipping. No haemorrhages, exudates, cotton wool spots or new vessels. Macula with foveal reflex.","abnormal":[{"finding":"Swollen disc with blurred margins, elevation, obscured vessels at the disc edge, venous engorgement, loss of spontaneous venous pulsation, bilateral","means":"Papilloedema (raised intracranial pressure) until proved otherwise; check vision, fields and blood pressure, urgent imaging. Malignant hypertension can mimic it."},{"finding":"Pale, flat disc with sharp margins","means":"Optic atrophy: previous optic neuritis, compression, ischaemia, glaucoma end stage, toxic or hereditary causes."},{"finding":"Enlarged vertical cup, notching of the neuroretinal rim, cup-to-disc asymmetry over about 0.2","means":"Glaucomatous optic neuropathy; refer for pressure and field testing."},{"finding":"Dot and blot haemorrhages, microaneurysms, hard exudates; new vessels at the disc or elsewhere","means":"Diabetic retinopathy; new vessels mean proliferative disease and urgent referral; exudates near the fovea suggest maculopathy."},{"finding":"Arteriolar narrowing, AV nipping, flame haemorrhages, cotton wool spots, with or without disc swelling","means":"Hypertensive retinopathy; disc swelling with these changes means hypertensive emergency."},{"finding":"Pale, oedematous retina with a cherry-red spot at the macula and sudden painless loss of vision","means":"Central retinal artery occlusion: stroke equivalent, immediate referral; also consider giant cell arteritis."}],"errors":[{"error":"Examining from too far away (more than about 5 cm).","result":"The tiny field of view means you see only a red glow and never find the disc.","fix":"Move in until your forehead almost touches your thumb on the patient's brow, keeping the red reflex in view all the way."},{"error":"Using the right eye to examine the patient's left eye face to face.","result":"Your noses collide, your head blocks their fixation target, the eye wanders and the pupil constricts.","fix":"Right hand, right eye for the patient's right eye; left, left, left for their left."},{"error":"Letting the patient look at the light or at you.","result":"Pupil constricts (near and light reflex), eye moves and you lose the disc.","fix":"Give them a clear distant target over your shoulder and remind them to keep looking at it even if your head blocks it."},{"error":"Approaching straight along the visual axis.","result":"You land on the macula, causing glare, miosis and discomfort, and the disc is off to one side.","fix":"Approach from about 15 degrees temporal along the horizontal."},{"error":"Calling papilloedema absent because spontaneous venous pulsation is absent, or present because it is absent.","result":"Over- or under-calling raised ICP; absence is common in normal people.","fix":"Use SVP only as a reassuring sign when present; judge disc swelling by margins, elevation and vessel obscuration, and compare eyes."},{"error":"Dilating the pupils in a patient whose pupils are being monitored for neurological change.","result":"Loss of a vital sign and possible misinterpretation of a 'blown pupil'.","fix":"Discuss with the team first, and if you dilate, document the drug, eye and time clearly in the notes and at the bedside."}],"trouble":[{"problem":"I can't find the disc.","fix":"Go back to the red reflex at arm's length, re-approach exactly 15 degrees temporal along the horizontal, get close, then find any vessel and follow it against its branching (towards the stem of the Y) until it widens and leads you to the disc."},{"problem":"Everything is blurred.","fix":"Dial slowly through the lenses: towards red (minus) for myopia, towards plus for hypermetropia. If no setting gives crisp vessels, check the red reflex for cataract or other media opacity, and consider dilation or a different aperture to reduce glare."},{"problem":"The pupil is too small.","fix":"Darken the room further, ask the patient to fix in the distance, use the small aperture to reduce reflections, and approach from the temporal side; if it is safe and a good view matters, dilate with tropicamide."},{"problem":"The patient keeps blinking or moving the eye.","fix":"Hold the upper lid gently against the brow with your thumb, reduce brightness, give a definite distant target, and work in short bursts with breaks to blink."}],"pearls":["The disc is the size of a small coin on your view; if you see a pale circle much bigger than your field, you are looking at the right thing but too close or too far to focus.","Use the red-free (green) filter: haemorrhages and microaneurysms look black and stand out from background.","Papilloedema rarely affects vision early except for an enlarged blind spot, whereas optic neuritis causes early visual loss and an RAPD: vision helps distinguish the two.","Darker fundal pigmentation, common in Middle Eastern and Asian patients, makes the fundus look darker and the disc look relatively pinker; compare with the other eye rather than the textbook.","Smartphone fundus photography or nonmydriatic cameras are better than direct ophthalmoscopy for detecting and recording disease; use them when available."],"evidence":[{"stat":"In an ED study, emergency physicians using direct ophthalmoscopy detected none of the relevant fundal findings later seen on nonmydriatic photography in the patients they examined.","src":"Bruce et al 2011, NEJM (FOTO-ED study)"},{"stat":"Spontaneous venous pulsation is absent in a minority of normal people, so its absence is non-specific; its presence makes raised ICP unlikely at that time.","src":"Jacks and Miller 2003, J Neurol Neurosurg Psychiatry"}],"document":"Normal: Fundoscopy (undilated, direct): red reflexes present bilaterally. Discs pink, sharp margins, CDR 0.3 both, SVP present. Vessels normal calibre, no AV nipping. No haemorrhages, exudates or new vessels; maculae normal. Abnormal: Fundoscopy (dilated tropicamide 1% both eyes 14:10): bilateral disc swelling with blurred margins, vessel obscuration at disc edges and venous engorgement; SVP absent. Peripapillary flame haemorrhages R. Maculae normal. Impression: papilloedema; urgent CT and neurology.","hook":"Same side, same hand, same eye; 15 degrees temporal; get close; follow the vessels home; macula last.","mcq":[{"q":"A doctor examines a patient's right eye, holding the ophthalmoscope in the right hand at the right eye, and approaches straight along the patient's visual axis. The patient squints, the pupil constricts and the doctor sees only a glare. What should be changed?","opts":["Use the left eye","Approach about 15 degrees temporal to the line of fixation along the horizontal","Ask the patient to look at the light","Increase the brightness"],"a":1,"why":"Approaching along the visual axis lands on the macula, causing glare and miosis. The disc is about 15 degrees nasal to the fovea, so approaching from 15 degrees temporal brings it straight into view."},{"q":"A doctor finds a branching vessel but no disc. Which way should they follow the vessel?","opts":["In the direction the branches open (with the arrowheads)","Towards the macula","Towards the stem of each Y, against the direction of branching","Outwards to the periphery"],"a":2,"why":"All retinal vessels branch as they leave the disc, so the forks open away from it. Following them towards the parent trunk always leads to the disc."},{"q":"A doctor sees sharp disc margins but no spontaneous venous pulsation in a patient with headache and concludes the patient has raised ICP. What is the error?","opts":["SVP is absent in some normal people; its absence alone does not indicate raised ICP","SVP is only seen in the macula","SVP is only present when ICP is raised","There is no error"],"a":0,"why":"Spontaneous venous pulsation is absent in a minority of healthy people. Its presence is reassuring, but its absence alone does not diagnose raised ICP; look for disc swelling and use clinical context."}],"src":["Macleod's Clinical Examination 15e","Talley and O'Connor's Clinical Examination 9e","Bruce et al 2011, NEJM: Nonmydriatic ocular fundus photography in the emergency department","Jacks and Miller 2003, J Neurol Neurosurg Psychiatry: Spontaneous retinal venous pulsation"]},{"id":"red-reflex","name":"Red reflex and Brückner test","group":"Ear, nose and eye","signs":[],"purpose":"Answers whether the optical media (cornea, aqueous, lens, vitreous) are clear and whether the two eyes reflect light equally. In infants and children it screens for retinoblastoma, congenital cataract, strabismus and significant refractive asymmetry; in adults it detects cataract, vitreous haemorrhage and other media opacities and explains a poor fundal view.","kit":["Direct ophthalmoscope with a fully charged battery, set at 0 dioptres (or your own correction), large round aperture","Darkened room","A toy or interesting target behind you for a child to look at"],"setup":[{"do":"Darken the room; the patient looks at a distant target (adult) or at your light or face (infant, where cooperation is limited).","why":"Darkness dilates the pupils so more of the reflex is visible and asymmetry is easier to see; in infants the light itself becomes the fixation target, which is acceptable for the reflex."},{"do":"Sit or stand at the patient's eye level and hold the ophthalmoscope against your own brow so you look through the aperture.","why":"If you are above or below eye level, the angle into each eye differs and can create artificial asymmetry; looking through the aperture keeps the light coaxial with your line of sight, which is what produces the red reflex."}],"steps":[{"do":"Set the dial to 0 (or your own refractive correction) and hold the ophthalmoscope against your brow, looking through the aperture.","why":"The red reflex is light reflected from the fundus back along the same axis; you can only see it when your eye is in the path of the returning light, so the light and your view must be coaxial.","cue":"When you look at a pupil, it glows orange-red rather than looking black."},{"do":"For the single-eye red reflex, stand about 30 to 50 cm from each eye in turn and look at the pupil through the ophthalmoscope.","why":"At this distance the whole pupil is in view, and opacities in the cornea, lens or vitreous show as dark shadows against the glow. Close-up you lose the overall view.","cue":"A uniform orange-red glow filling the pupil, without dark spots."},{"do":"For the Brückner test, move back to about 50 cm to 1 m so the beam covers both eyes at once, and compare the colour, brightness and size of the two reflexes simultaneously.","why":"Comparing the two eyes at the same instant under identical light is far more sensitive to asymmetry than looking at each eye separately. A deviated eye, an eye with a different refractive error, or an eye with an opacity reflects light differently.","cue":"Both pupils glow with the same colour and brightness, and the corneal light reflexes sit symmetrically in each eye."},{"do":"While doing the Brückner, look at the corneal light reflexes as well: they should sit in the same position (just nasal to the pupil centre) in both eyes.","why":"This is the Hirschberg test: a displaced reflex in one eye means that eye is deviated (temporal reflex = esotropia, nasal = exotropia). It confirms whether a brighter reflex is due to strabismus.","cue":"The two pinpoint reflections are in matching positions relative to each pupil."},{"do":"If you see an opacity, ask the patient to look up and then straight ahead and watch which way the opacity moves.","why":"Parallax against the pupil margin localises the opacity: corneal opacities move in the same direction as the eye, vitreous opacities move in the opposite direction, and lens opacities near the pupil plane move little; vitreous opacities also keep drifting after the eye stops.","cue":"You can say whether an opacity is corneal, lenticular or vitreous."},{"do":"Record the reflex as present and symmetrical, or describe exactly what is abnormal (absent, white, dull, dark spots, asymmetric) in each eye.","why":"An abnormal reflex in a child is a screening finding that triggers referral; a vague description delays action.","cue":"You can write a clear description that another doctor could check."}],"normal":"Both pupils show a bright, uniform orange-red reflex of equal colour, brightness and size, without dark spots, and the corneal light reflexes sit symmetrically. In darker-pigmented individuals the reflex can be darker red or orange-brown, but it is equal between the eyes.","abnormal":[{"finding":"Absent reflex or white reflex (leukocoria) in a child","means":"Retinoblastoma, congenital cataract, persistent fetal vasculature, retinopathy of prematurity, Coats disease, toxocara; refer urgently."},{"finding":"Dark spots or spokes within the reflex","means":"Media opacity: cataract (often radial spokes), corneal scar, vitreous opacities; parallax localises it."},{"finding":"Asymmetric reflex, one brighter, with a displaced corneal light reflex","means":"Strabismus: the deviated eye usually gives the brighter reflex; refer to orthoptics or ophthalmology."},{"finding":"Asymmetric reflex with symmetrical corneal reflexes","means":"Anisometropia (unequal refractive error) or a partial opacity; risk of amblyopia in a child."},{"finding":"Dull or absent reflex in an adult after trauma or in diabetes","means":"Vitreous haemorrhage, dense cataract, or retinal detachment; urgent ophthalmology if acute visual loss."}],"errors":[{"error":"Not looking through the ophthalmoscope aperture (light held beside the eye).","result":"No red reflex because your view is not coaxial with the light, wrongly called absent.","fix":"Keep the ophthalmoscope against your brow and look through the aperture."},{"error":"Examining in a bright room.","result":"Small pupils make the reflex small and asymmetry is hard to judge, especially in infants.","fix":"Darken the room and let the pupils dilate before testing."},{"error":"Checking each eye separately and calling it normal.","result":"Subtle asymmetry from strabismus or anisometropia is missed.","fix":"Always finish with the simultaneous Brückner comparison from 50 cm to 1 m."},{"error":"Dismissing an intermittent white reflex reported by parents because the reflex looks normal today.","result":"Missed retinoblastoma, which may show only at certain angles or in photographs.","fix":"Take parental reports and photographs seriously; refer for dilated examination."},{"error":"Standing at an angle or not at eye level.","result":"Artificial asymmetry and false referrals.","fix":"Position yourself directly in front, at the same height as the patient's eyes."}],"trouble":[{"problem":"I can't see any red reflex.","fix":"Check that you are looking through the aperture with the dial near zero, the room is dark and the battery is strong. If still absent, compare with the other eye; an absent reflex in one eye only is a true finding until proved otherwise."},{"problem":"The infant won't keep the eyes open or keeps turning away.","fix":"Try during feeding or when drowsy, use a dim light, have a parent hold the baby upright and talk to it; if you cannot see both reflexes, document this and arrange to re-examine or refer rather than recording normal."},{"problem":"The reflexes look slightly different in colour.","fix":"Recheck from directly in front at exact eye level in a dark room, compare corneal light reflexes, and if a difference persists, refer: subtle asymmetry can be the only sign of strabismus or anisometropia."}],"pearls":["The distance for the Brückner test varies between sources, from about 45 cm to 1 m; any distance at which the beam covers both eyes evenly and you are exactly in front works.","In strabismus, the deviated eye usually gives a brighter reflex in the Brückner test.","Parents' photographs showing a white pupil (leukocoria) in one eye are an important sign; ask to see them.","A red reflex can be present with a small retinoblastoma that does not involve the posterior pole; persistent concern still needs a dilated examination.","In adults, check the red reflex before a fundal view; a poor reflex explains a poor view."],"evidence":[{"stat":"NICE recommends considering urgent referral (appointment within 2 weeks) for ophthalmological assessment for retinoblastoma in a child with an absent red reflex.","src":"NICE NG12: Suspected cancer: recognition and referral"},{"stat":"The AAP recommends red reflex examination in all neonates and at all routine health supervision visits in infancy and childhood.","src":"American Academy of Pediatrics 2008, Pediatrics: Red reflex examination in neonates, infants, and children"}],"document":"Normal: Red reflexes present and symmetrical bilaterally (Brückner), corneal light reflexes central. Abnormal: R red reflex absent with white pupillary reflex (leukocoria); L normal. Corneal light reflexes symmetrical. Urgent ophthalmology referral for suspected retinoblastoma.","hook":"Dark room, light coaxial, both eyes at once: equal red is good, white is an emergency.","mcq":[{"q":"A doctor checks a baby's red reflex holding the ophthalmoscope 10 cm to the side of their own face and sees black pupils in both eyes. What is the most likely explanation?","opts":["Bilateral congenital cataract","Retinoblastoma","Normal finding in infants","The doctor was not looking through the aperture coaxially with the light"],"a":3,"why":"The red reflex is only seen when the examiner's view is coaxial with the light. Holding the light away from your eye gives black pupils in any eye."},{"q":"At the 6-week check, both red reflexes are seen separately as present. The doctor stops there. What has been missed?","opts":["The simultaneous Brückner comparison for asymmetry","Fundoscopy of the macula","Visual acuity testing","Nothing, the examination is complete"],"a":0,"why":"Separate checks can miss subtle asymmetry from strabismus or anisometropia; the simultaneous Brückner comparison at 50 cm to 1 m is more sensitive."},{"q":"A 2-year-old's parents show a photo with one white pupil. Today the red reflex looks normal. What should the doctor do?","opts":["Reassure and repeat at the next routine check","Refer for urgent ophthalmology assessment","Arrange glasses","Repeat the red reflex in 6 months"],"a":1,"why":"Leukocoria in photographs is a red flag for retinoblastoma; a normal reflex on one examination does not exclude it. Refer for a dilated examination."}],"src":["American Academy of Pediatrics 2008, Pediatrics: Red reflex examination in neonates, infants, and children","NICE NG12: Suspected cancer: recognition and referral","Macleod's Clinical Examination 15e"]},{"id":"swinging-torch","name":"Swinging flashlight test (RAPD)","group":"Ear, nose and eye","signs":["rapd"],"purpose":"Answers whether one eye's afferent pathway (retina or optic nerve) transmits less light than the other: a relative afferent pupillary defect. Reach for it in any unilateral visual loss, suspected optic neuritis, ischaemic optic neuropathy, retinal detachment, orbital trauma or compressive lesion; it is objective and works even in uncooperative patients.","kit":["Bright, even light source: indirect ophthalmoscope, bright LED pen torch or the ophthalmoscope on full brightness with large aperture","Dim room","Distant fixation target","Neutral density filters (0.3, 0.6, 0.9, 1.2 log units) if you want to grade the defect"],"setup":[{"do":"Dim the room and ask the patient to look at a distant target on the far wall throughout.","why":"Dimming dilates the pupils so changes in size are larger and easier to see. Distant fixation prevents accommodation and the near response, which constricts both pupils and would mask a dilation."},{"do":"Look at the pupils in dim light first, then in bright light, and note any anisocoria.","why":"Anisocoria is an efferent problem and does not by itself cause an RAPD; recording it first stops you mistaking a larger pupil for a defect."},{"do":"Position yourself slightly to one side, and hold the light about 10 to 15 cm from the eye, aimed from just below the visual axis.","why":"Light from slightly below avoids the patient looking at it (which would provoke accommodation) and lets you see both pupils."}],"steps":[{"do":"Check the direct and consensual responses in each eye first.","why":"If one pupil does not react at all (for example a third nerve palsy or a pharmacologically dilated pupil), you will need to use the other pupil to read the test, the reverse method.","cue":"Both pupils constrict briskly to direct light in either eye."},{"do":"Shine the light into the right eye for 2 to 3 seconds, keeping the same distance and angle.","why":"Both pupils constrict and then begin to re-dilate slightly (pupillary escape) as the retina adapts. Holding for a consistent 2 to 3 seconds lets the pupils reach a steady state before you switch, so the comparison is fair.","cue":"Both pupils constrict and stabilise."},{"do":"Swing the light briskly across the bridge of the nose to the left eye (in about 1 second), keeping the same distance and angle, hold 2 to 3 seconds.","why":"A quick swing means the pupil does not have time to dilate in the dark between eyes. The same distance and angle mean equal light reaches each retina; only then does a difference in pupil response reflect a difference in the afferent pathway.","cue":"At the moment the light arrives, the newly illuminated pupil either stays the same or constricts slightly (normal), or dilates (abnormal)."},{"do":"Watch only the initial movement of the newly illuminated pupil in the first second after the light arrives.","why":"Because both pupils are linked through the consensual response, they are always the same size. What changes is the strength of the afferent signal: if the light on a damaged eye is perceived as dimmer, both pupils dilate as the light moves onto it.","cue":"Normal: the pupil remains the same size or constricts slightly. RAPD: the pupil dilates as the light arrives."},{"do":"Repeat the swing several times (3 to 4 cycles), with the same rhythm.","why":"A single swing can be misleading because of hippus (normal pupil oscillation); a true RAPD is reproducible every time.","cue":"The same eye dilates every time it is illuminated."},{"do":"If one pupil is unreactive or cannot be seen, watch the reactive pupil throughout the swinging test (reverse method).","why":"The consensual response gives the same information: if the reactive pupil dilates when the light moves to the other eye, the other eye has the RAPD.","cue":"The reactive pupil constricts when its own eye is lit and dilates when the light moves to the eye with the defect."},{"do":"Grade the RAPD clinically or with neutral density filters.","why":"Grading allows comparison over time: clinical grades 1 to 4 (from weak initial constriction followed by greater redilation, to immediate dilation, to an amaurotic pupil) or the log unit of filter needed over the good eye to balance responses.","cue":"You can record the grade, for example a 2+ RAPD left or 0.6 log unit RAPD left."}],"normal":"Both pupils constrict equally to light in either eye. When the light swings from one eye to the other, the newly illuminated pupil remains the same size or constricts slightly before slight symmetrical escape. No relative afferent pupillary defect.","abnormal":[{"finding":"Newly illuminated pupil dilates each time the light reaches one eye","means":"RAPD in that eye: optic neuropathy (optic neuritis, ischaemic optic neuropathy, compression, glaucoma if asymmetric, trauma) or extensive retinal disease (central retinal artery or vein occlusion, large retinal detachment)."},{"finding":"RAPD with severe visual loss in one eye and pain on eye movement","means":"Optic neuritis; check colour vision and fields; consider MS work-up."},{"finding":"RAPD with sudden painless visual loss in an older patient","means":"Ischaemic optic neuropathy (consider giant cell arteritis) or central retinal artery occlusion; urgent assessment."},{"finding":"Pupil does not constrict to direct light but constricts normally to consensual light (amaurotic pupil)","means":"Severe or complete afferent loss in that eye: grade 4 RAPD."},{"finding":"Small RAPD in the eye on the side of a complete homonymous hemianopia (the eye contralateral to the lesion) with normal acuity","means":"Optic tract lesion: that eye has lost its temporal field, and the nasal retina serving it carries slightly more pupillary fibres than the temporal retina."}],"errors":[{"error":"Holding the light longer on one eye or at a different distance or angle.","result":"Artificial asymmetry creating a false RAPD or hiding a real one.","fix":"Use the same distance, angle and a consistent 2 to 3 seconds per eye."},{"error":"Letting the patient look at the light.","result":"Accommodation and near-reflex miosis mask the dilation, causing a false negative.","fix":"Give a distant target and hold the light slightly below the visual axis."},{"error":"Swinging slowly with a pause between eyes.","result":"Both pupils dilate in the dark interval, making both look like they constrict on illumination and masking a defect.","fix":"Swing briskly across the nose in about 1 second."},{"error":"Interpreting anisocoria as an RAPD.","result":"A false positive; the larger pupil is an efferent issue.","fix":"Compare the movement (constriction or dilation) at the moment of illumination, not the absolute pupil size."},{"error":"Attributing an RAPD to cataract.","result":"Missed optic nerve or retinal pathology.","fix":"Cataract and refractive error do not cause an RAPD; dense cataract can even cause a small RAPD in the other eye. Look for an afferent cause."},{"error":"Using a weak or uneven light.","result":"Small responses that are hard to judge, causing false negatives.","fix":"Use a bright, even light such as an indirect ophthalmoscope or a strong LED torch."}],"trouble":[{"problem":"I can't see the pupils well, the irises are dark.","fix":"Dim the room further, use a side light at low intensity to show the pupil margin, or use the ophthalmoscope at +10 dioptres from a short distance to magnify, or look at the red reflex pupil size from 30 cm."},{"problem":"Both pupils look like they are escaping (dilating) after constriction.","fix":"Normal pupillary escape is symmetrical and gradual. Focus only on the first second after the light arrives on each eye, and compare: in an RAPD the dilation is immediate and occurs only in one eye."},{"problem":"One pupil is fixed and dilated.","fix":"Use the reverse method: watch the reactive pupil while swinging. If it dilates when the light moves to the fixed-pupil eye, that eye has an RAPD."},{"problem":"The result is equivocal.","fix":"Use neutral density filters over the better eye to see if the response balances, repeat several cycles, and check visual acuity, colour vision (red desaturation) and fields to support the finding."}],"pearls":["Clinical grading scales vary between sources; record the description and the filter value in log units if available.","A positive RAPD with normal fundoscopy points behind the eye: retrobulbar optic neuritis or compression.","Bilateral symmetrical optic neuropathy gives no RAPD because the test is relative.","Red desaturation (red looks washed out in one eye) supports an optic nerve cause.","The test is objective and works in children and in patients who cannot cooperate with visual acuity."],"evidence":[{"stat":"An RAPD is expected in acute unilateral optic neuritis when the fellow eye is healthy; its absence should make you reconsider the diagnosis.","src":"Toosy, Mason and Miller 2014, Lancet Neurology (Optic neuritis review)"},{"stat":"A unilateral cataract does not cause an RAPD in that eye; a dense cataract can produce a small RAPD in the contralateral eye.","src":"Lam and Thompson 1990, Ophthalmology"}],"document":"Normal: Pupils equal and reactive to light, no RAPD on swinging torch. Abnormal: Pupils 4 mm in dim light, equal. Swinging torch: L pupil dilates on illumination, reproducible over 4 cycles; 2+ RAPD L. L red desaturation. Impression: L optic neuropathy.","hook":"Same light, same distance, same time, brisk swing, distant gaze: watch the first movement of the pupil you just lit.","mcq":[{"q":"During the swinging test, the doctor holds the light 5 seconds on the right eye and 2 seconds on the left, and finds the left pupil dilates when lit. What is the error?","opts":["Unequal time on each eye creates artificial asymmetry","The doctor should hold for 10 seconds","The light should be held closer to the left eye","There is no error; this is an RAPD"],"a":0,"why":"Unequal duration (or distance or angle) gives different retinal adaptation and pupil sizes, causing a false RAPD. Use the same 2 to 3 seconds on each eye."},{"q":"A patient has a dense right cataract and a left RAPD. What is the most likely interpretation?","opts":["The cataract causes the RAPD","The cataract causes an RAPD in the left eye","The test is invalid in cataract","Look for an afferent cause in the left eye, such as optic neuropathy"],"a":3,"why":"Cataract does not cause an RAPD in the affected eye; a dense cataract may cause a small RAPD in the other eye but a left RAPD needs evaluation for left optic nerve or retinal disease."},{"q":"A doctor performs the swinging torch test while the patient looks at the light. Both pupils constrict at each swing and no RAPD is seen. What is the problem?","opts":["The light was too bright","The patient's fixation on the light triggers accommodation and miosis, masking an RAPD","The test should be done in bright light","The doctor should have used a red light"],"a":1,"why":"Looking at the near light causes the near response, constricting both pupils and hiding the dilation of an RAPD. Ask the patient to fixate on a distant target."}],"src":["Macleod's Clinical Examination 15e","Toosy, Mason and Miller 2014, Lancet Neurology","Lam and Thompson 1990, Ophthalmology","Talley and O'Connor's Clinical Examination 9e"]},{"id":"confrontation-fields","name":"Visual field confrontation","group":"Ear, nose and eye","signs":["visual-fields"],"purpose":"Answers whether there is a gross visual field defect and its pattern: hemianopia, quadrantanopia, bitemporal defect, central scotoma or altitudinal loss. Reach for it in visual complaints, headache with red flags, suspected stroke, pituitary or chiasmal disease, and before declaring a patient fit to drive or work.","kit":["Your hands for finger counting and hand comparison","A 5 mm red-headed hatpin (or red-topped pen cap) for central field and colour comparison","A white hatpin or wiggling finger for kinetic peripheral testing","Patient's near correction if needed for central testing"],"setup":[{"do":"Sit facing the patient about 1 m apart (arm's length), at the same eye level, with even lighting behind or above you, not behind the patient.","why":"At about 1 m, a target held in the plane midway between you subtends the same angle for both of you, so your field becomes the reference for theirs. Equal eye level aligns the horizontal meridians; light behind the patient would put you in silhouette."},{"do":"To test the patient's right eye, ask them to cover their left eye with their left palm (cupped, not pressing on the globe) and look straight at your open left eye; you close or cover your right eye. Reverse everything for their left eye.","why":"Pressing on the globe blurs vision in that eye when it is uncovered later. Each of you then has one open eye facing the other (their right with your left), with identical temporal and nasal fields mirror-imaged. Their fixation on your eye lets you see instantly if they look at the target."},{"do":"Ask about glasses and visual acuity first.","why":"Poor acuity limits central testing, and confrontation results are uninterpretable if the patient cannot see the target well."}],"steps":[{"do":"Screen with bilateral simultaneous finger movement first: with both eyes open, hold your hands in the upper left and upper right quadrants and move a finger on one, the other, or both hands; repeat in the lower quadrants.","why":"Simultaneous testing detects visual inattention (extinction) from parietal lesions, where a single stimulus is seen but one side disappears when both are presented together.","cue":"The patient correctly reports which hand moved, including both."},{"do":"Test each eye separately with static finger counting in four quadrants: hold your hand about 20 to 30 degrees from fixation (about 20 to 25 cm from the centre in the midway plane when you sit 1 m apart), in the plane midway between you, briefly showing 1, 2 or 5 fingers.","why":"Static finger counting at about 20 to 30 degrees checks each quadrant of the central field where most neurological defects show. Using 1, 2 or 5 fingers is easier than 3 or 4, which reduces false errors.","cue":"The patient counts correctly in each quadrant without moving their eye."},{"do":"Do kinetic testing for the periphery: bring a wiggling finger or white pin from beyond the edge of your own field inwards along each diagonal and along the horizontal and vertical meridians, in the plane midway between you, asking the patient to say as soon as they see it.","why":"Moving targets from unseen to seen map the outer boundary. Using the plane midway means you see it at the same time if their field is normal. Diagonals cover the quadrants where hemianopias and quadrantanopias begin.","cue":"The patient sees the target at the same point as you do."},{"do":"Compare red colour saturation across the vertical meridian: hold the red pin at the same point either side of the vertical midline in the upper temporal and upper nasal fields and ask if the colour looks the same.","why":"Red desaturation is an early sign of compressive or optic nerve damage. A pituitary tumour compressing the chiasm from below affects the crossing nasal fibres first, giving superior temporal field red desaturation earliest.","cue":"The red looks equally bright either side of the vertical midline."},{"do":"Test the central field with the red pin: move it slowly through and around fixation, asking if it disappears, changes colour or looks faded; map the blind spot about 15 degrees temporal to fixation and compare with yours.","why":"A central scotoma (optic neuritis, macular disease, toxic or nutritional optic neuropathy) is missed by finger counting. The blind spot mapping checks your technique and enlarges in papilloedema.","cue":"The red pin stays red and visible at fixation; the blind spot is the same size and position as yours."},{"do":"Do the hand comparison test: hold both palms either side of the vertical meridian in the central field and ask whether both look equally clear and bright.","why":"Subtle hemianopic defects show as one hand appearing dimmer or blurred, even when finger counting is normal.","cue":"Both hands look the same."},{"do":"Repeat for the other eye, and record defects on a chart from the patient's point of view.","why":"Comparing eyes defines the pattern: homonymous defects point behind the chiasm, bitemporal to the chiasm, unilateral to the eye or optic nerve.","cue":"You can draw each eye's field from the patient's perspective."}],"normal":"Patient detects bilateral simultaneous finger movements, counts fingers correctly in all four quadrants of each eye, sees kinetic targets at the same point as the examiner, perceives red equally across the vertical meridian, has no central scotoma, and the blind spot matches the examiner's.","abnormal":[{"finding":"Homonymous hemianopia (same side in both eyes)","means":"Lesion behind the chiasm: optic tract, radiation or occipital cortex, most commonly stroke."},{"finding":"Bitemporal hemianopia or superior temporal red desaturation","means":"Chiasmal compression: pituitary adenoma, craniopharyngioma, meningioma."},{"finding":"Homonymous superior quadrantanopia","means":"Temporal lobe lesion (Meyer's loop)."},{"finding":"Homonymous inferior quadrantanopia","means":"Parietal lobe lesion."},{"finding":"Central scotoma in one eye","means":"Optic neuritis, macular disease, or toxic or nutritional optic neuropathy."},{"finding":"Visual extinction on bilateral simultaneous testing","means":"Parietal lobe lesion causing visual inattention (neglect)."}],"errors":[{"error":"Sitting too close or at a different eye level.","result":"Your field no longer matches the patient's, so normal can look abnormal and vice versa.","fix":"Sit at about 1 m, at the same eye level, and hold targets in the plane midway between you."},{"error":"Not matching eyes (both of you have your right eyes open).","result":"The fields are not mirror-imaged and you cannot compare.","fix":"If the patient covers their left eye, you close your right eye, and vice versa."},{"error":"Letting the patient look at the target.","result":"Defects are missed because the target is brought to central vision.","fix":"Ask the patient to look at your open eye and watch it; stop and remind them if it moves."},{"error":"Holding targets too close to the patient or too close to you.","result":"Different angular sizes and inaccurate mapping.","fix":"Keep targets in the midway plane."},{"error":"Using only finger counting.","result":"Central scotomas and subtle chiasmal defects are missed.","fix":"Add red desaturation, central red pin testing and hand comparison."},{"error":"Assuming a normal confrontation means a normal field.","result":"Missed glaucoma, early chiasmal compression, and defects relevant to driving.","fix":"Refer for formal perimetry when symptoms or risk factors exist."}],"trouble":[{"problem":"The patient keeps looking at my fingers.","fix":"Remind them to look at your open eye, use a quick static presentation, and watch their eye; if they cannot maintain fixation, test with bilateral simultaneous hand movement only and record the limitation."},{"problem":"The patient has poor acuity.","fix":"Use larger targets (hand movement or light perception) and compare quadrants; for central testing use near correction; record what targets were used."},{"problem":"I'm not sure if a defect is real.","fix":"Repeat with a red pin, compare with your own field, check the other eye for a matching pattern, and confirm with formal perimetry."}],"pearls":["Textbooks vary in the distance (60 cm to 1 m); what matters is targets in the midway plane so your field is the reference.","Bitemporal defects are easiest to detect with red desaturation across the vertical meridian, especially superiorly.","Homonymous hemianopia that respects the vertical meridian is the sign of a lesion behind the chiasm.","A patient with a hemianopia may not complain; screen all stroke patients.","Confrontation cannot replace perimetry; refer for automated fields when in doubt."],"evidence":[{"stat":"Confrontation tests have high specificity but low and variable sensitivity; finger counting was the least sensitive and kinetic red target the most sensitive single test, with combinations improving sensitivity.","src":"Kerr et al 2010, Neurology"},{"stat":"Normal confrontation fields do not exclude glaucomatous or other subtle field defects, which need formal perimetry.","src":"Macleod's Clinical Examination 15e"}],"document":"Normal: Visual fields full to confrontation (finger counting, kinetic, red pin) both eyes; no central scotoma, no extinction. Abnormal: R homonymous hemianopia to finger counting and kinetic testing, respecting vertical meridian; macula spared to red pin; no extinction. Impression: L retrochiasmal lesion.","hook":"Sit 1 m, match eyes, fixate on yours, test in the midway plane, and add red to catch the chiasm.","mcq":[{"q":"A doctor sits 30 cm from the patient, both with their right eyes open, and finds a left temporal field defect. What is wrong with the technique?","opts":["Nothing, this is correct","The patient should cover their right eye","They are too close and not matching eyes, so the fields are not comparable","The doctor should use a white pin"],"a":2,"why":"Confrontation requires sitting about 1 m apart with mirror-matched eyes (patient's right with examiner's left) so the examiner's field is a reference."},{"q":"A patient has normal finger counting in all quadrants but reports red looks washed out in the upper temporal fields of both eyes. What does this suggest?","opts":["Chiasmal compression, e.g. pituitary tumour","Normal finding","Occipital stroke","Retinal detachment"],"a":0,"why":"Superior temporal red desaturation is an early sign of chiasmal compression from below, affecting the crossing nasal fibres."},{"q":"A doctor uses only finger counting and declares the fields normal in a patient with a central visual complaint. What test was missed?","opts":["Bilateral simultaneous hand movement","Kinetic white pin testing","Rinne test","Central red pin testing for a central scotoma"],"a":3,"why":"Finger counting at 20 to 30 degrees misses central scotomas. Moving a red pin through fixation detects them."}],"src":["Macleod's Clinical Examination 15e","Kerr et al 2010, Neurology: Diagnostic accuracy of confrontation visual field tests","Talley and O'Connor's Clinical Examination 9e"]},{"id":"tuning-fork","name":"Rinne and Weber tests","group":"Ear, nose and eye","signs":["rinne-weber"],"purpose":"Answers whether a hearing loss is conductive or sensorineural and which ear is affected, using air and bone conduction. Reach for it when a patient reports hearing loss, sudden deafness, tinnitus or vertigo, or after head injury, and as a bedside screen before audiometry.","kit":["512 Hz tuning fork (256 Hz acceptable if 512 is unavailable)","Quiet room","Noise source to mask the opposite ear (Bárány noise box, or rubbing the tragus or crinkling paper) for suspected severe unilateral loss"],"setup":[{"do":"Use a quiet room, explain that you will play sounds and ask them to say which is louder or when the sound stops.","why":"Background noise masks the soft fork tone; the patient's understanding prevents unreliable answers."},{"do":"Strike the fork on your knee, elbow or a rubber pad, at the junction of the upper and middle third of the tines.","why":"A soft surface produces a pure tone with fewer overtones and avoids damaging the fork. Striking on a hard surface creates high-frequency overtones and can damage the tines. Striking at the upper third maximises the fundamental tone."},{"do":"Use a 512 Hz fork.","why":"512 Hz is in the speech range and decays slowly enough to hear; lower frequencies (128 or 256 Hz) are felt as vibration and give misleading bone conduction results, higher frequencies decay too fast."}],"steps":[{"do":"Weber: strike the fork and place the base firmly in the midline of the forehead, vertex, or on the upper incisors (if the patient has their own teeth).","why":"Bone conduction transmits sound equally to both cochleas from the midline. With a conductive loss, the affected ear hears it louder because ambient noise is masked and bone vibration is better transmitted. With a sensorineural loss, the better cochlea hears it louder.","cue":"The patient reports the sound is heard in the middle or equally in both ears, or louder in one ear."},{"do":"Ask: 'Where do you hear the sound: in the middle, or more on one side?'","why":"An open question avoids suggesting an answer.","cue":"The patient answers clearly: middle, left or right."},{"do":"Rinne: strike the fork and place the base firmly on the mastoid process behind the ear, with your other hand supporting the opposite side of the head.","why":"This tests bone conduction; firm contact transmits vibration to the cochlea, and support prevents the head moving away.","cue":"The patient hears the sound."},{"do":"When the patient says they cannot hear it any longer, or immediately (loudness comparison method), move the tines about 1 to 2 cm from the external acoustic meatus, with the tines in line with the canal axis.","why":"Air conduction is normally more efficient than bone conduction because the middle ear amplifies sound. Holding the tines in line with the canal (one tine nearer than the other) directs the strongest sound into the canal; at 45 degrees there is a null in the sound field.","cue":"Normal: the patient hears the sound again (or hears it louder) at the meatus."},{"do":"Ask: 'Is it louder behind the ear or in front of the ear?' (or 'Can you hear it now?' after bone conduction stops).","why":"Comparing loudness is quick and reliable; timing methods take longer but are equally valid.","cue":"Normal: louder in front (air conduction better than bone conduction), Rinne positive."},{"do":"Repeat Rinne on the other ear.","why":"Each ear must be tested separately to identify the side and nature of loss.","cue":"You can record Rinne positive or negative for each ear."},{"do":"If Rinne is negative in an ear with severe hearing loss and Weber lateralises to the other ear, mask the opposite ear with a noise box or by rubbing the tragus and repeat Rinne.","why":"Bone conduction crosses the skull with almost no attenuation, so a severely deaf ear's 'bone conduction' may actually be heard by the opposite cochlea, giving a false negative Rinne. Masking the good ear eliminates this cross-hearing.","cue":"After masking, the deaf ear no longer hears bone conduction, revealing a sensorineural loss."}],"normal":"Weber: heard in the midline or equally in both ears. Rinne: positive in both ears (air conduction louder or longer than bone conduction).","abnormal":[{"finding":"Weber lateralises to the left; Rinne negative on the left, positive on the right","means":"Left conductive hearing loss: wax, otitis media with effusion, perforation, otosclerosis."},{"finding":"Weber lateralises to the right; Rinne positive in both ears","means":"Left sensorineural hearing loss: sudden sensorineural loss, acoustic neuroma, Meniere's disease, labyrinthitis."},{"finding":"Weber lateralises to the right; Rinne negative on the left (false negative)","means":"Severe left sensorineural loss with cross-hearing; confirm with masking."},{"finding":"Weber midline; Rinne negative in both ears","means":"Bilateral conductive loss."},{"finding":"Weber midline; Rinne positive bilaterally with reduced hearing","means":"Bilateral sensorineural loss (e.g. presbycusis), confirm with audiometry."}],"errors":[{"error":"Striking the fork on a hard surface.","result":"Overtones distort the tone and can damage the fork.","fix":"Strike on your knee, elbow or rubber pad."},{"error":"Using a 128 or 256 Hz fork.","result":"Vibration is felt, giving misleading bone conduction results.","fix":"Use a 512 Hz fork."},{"error":"Holding the tines at an angle to the canal.","result":"Weaker air conduction, possibly a false negative Rinne.","fix":"Hold the tines in line with the canal, 1 to 2 cm away."},{"error":"Not masking in severe unilateral loss.","result":"False negative Rinne due to cross-hearing, misclassified as conductive loss.","fix":"Mask the opposite ear with a noise box or tragal rubbing and repeat."},{"error":"Asking leading questions in Weber.","result":"Patients may answer to please you, giving false lateralisation.","fix":"Ask an open question: 'Where do you hear it?'"},{"error":"Relying on tuning forks for screening.","result":"Small conductive losses and mild sensorineural loss are missed.","fix":"Use the whispered voice test or audiometry for screening."}],"trouble":[{"problem":"The patient can't tell where the sound is.","fix":"Repeat with firm contact on the forehead or teeth, ensure a quiet room, and ask the patient to close their eyes; if still uncertain, record as indeterminate and use audiometry."},{"problem":"The Rinne result is confusing.","fix":"Check that the fork is 512 Hz, that you held the tines in line with the canal, and that the patient understands. In suspected severe loss, mask the opposite ear and retest."},{"problem":"The patient has wax.","fix":"Wax may cause a conductive loss; remove it if appropriate and retest, or interpret results in light of the wax."}],"pearls":["Rinne 'positive' (normal) and 'negative' (abnormal) are confusing; record 'AC > BC' or 'BC > AC' for each ear.","Weber lateralises to the side of conductive loss, or away from the side of sensorineural loss.","Sudden unilateral sensorineural hearing loss is an emergency; refer the same day for audiometry and consideration of steroids.","Textbooks vary in the distance from the meatus (1 to 2.5 cm); the key is to avoid touching the hair or ear.","Rinne is insensitive to small conductive losses; a positive Rinne does not exclude a mild conductive loss."],"evidence":[{"stat":"Tuning fork tests are poor screening tools; the whispered voice test is more sensitive for detecting hearing impairment.","src":"Bagai et al 2006, JAMA (Rational Clinical Examination)"},{"stat":"Rinne becomes reliably negative only with moderate or larger air-bone gaps, so small conductive losses are often missed.","src":"McGee, Evidence-Based Physical Diagnosis 5e"}],"document":"Normal: Weber midline; Rinne AC > BC bilaterally (512 Hz). Abnormal: Weber lateralises to L; Rinne L BC > AC, R AC > BC. Impression: L conductive hearing loss. Otoscopy: L wax impaction.","hook":"512 on the knee, midline then mastoid, tines in line with the canal; mask the good ear when the bad ear seems to hear bone.","mcq":[{"q":"A doctor strikes a 512 Hz fork on the metal desk edge and tests Rinne. What is wrong?","opts":["Nothing, it is correct","Striking on a hard surface produces overtones and may damage the fork","A 256 Hz fork should be used","The fork should be struck on the patient's head"],"a":1,"why":"Striking on a hard surface produces overtones and can damage the fork. Strike on your knee, elbow or a rubber pad."},{"q":"A patient with sudden right deafness has Weber lateralising to the left and Rinne BC > AC on the right. What should the doctor do?","opts":["Diagnose right conductive loss","Diagnose left sensorineural loss","Mask the left ear and repeat Rinne on the right","Refer for routine audiometry"],"a":2,"why":"A severe right sensorineural loss can produce a false negative Rinne via cross-hearing in the left cochlea. Mask the left ear and retest; sudden sensorineural loss needs same-day referral."},{"q":"A doctor holds the fork tines perpendicular to the canal during Rinne, and the patient hears bone conduction better than air conduction. What is the likely error?","opts":["Tines should be in line with the canal to maximise air conduction","The fork should be held on the forehead","A 128 Hz fork should be used","There is no error"],"a":0,"why":"Holding the tines in line with the canal directs the strongest sound into the canal. A perpendicular or 45-degree position reduces air conduction, risking a false negative Rinne."}],"src":["McGee, Evidence-Based Physical Diagnosis 5e","Bagai et al 2006, JAMA: Does this patient have hearing impairment?","Macleod's Clinical Examination 15e","Scott-Brown's Otorhinolaryngology 8e"]},{"id":"tinel","name":"Tinel's sign","group":"Peripheral nerve","signs":["tinel-wrist"],"purpose":"Tells you whether a peripheral nerve segment is mechanically irritable at a suspected compression site, and in a nerve injury where the leading edge of regenerating axons has reached. Reach for it in suspected carpal tunnel, cubital tunnel, fibular neck or tarsal tunnel entrapment, and serially after a nerve laceration or repair.","kit":["Your middle finger (index or middle finger pad, tapped like percussing a chest) for superficial sites such as the median nerve at the wrist","Reflex hammer (Queen Square or Taylor) for deeper or padded sites and for regeneration mapping, used with a light wrist-flick, not a reflex blow","Skin marker pen and tape measure when tracking an advancing Tinel after injury"],"setup":[{"do":"Ask about and record the patient's baseline symptoms first: where they tingle now, if at all, at rest.","why":"A positive Tinel is defined by provoked paraesthesia in the nerve's territory; if you do not know the resting baseline you cannot tell a provoked response from ongoing symptoms."},{"do":"Explain the expected sensation: 'I will tap; tell me if you feel tingling or an electric feeling, and point to where you feel it, not where I tap.'","why":"Patients often report local soreness at the tap site as 'yes'. Asking them to point to the distribution separates true nerve-trunk mechanosensitivity from local tenderness."},{"do":"Position the limb so the target nerve is superficial and lightly tensioned: wrist in slight extension (about 20 degrees) on a rolled towel for the median; elbow flexed to about 90 degrees for the ulnar; patient seated with the knee flexed for the common peroneal; ankle in neutral to slight eversion and dorsiflexion for the posterior tibial.","why":"Light tension and superficial position bring the nerve against bone or a retinaculum so a modest tap transmits enough mechanical deformation to depolarise demyelinated or regenerating axon segments, which have abnormal mechanosensitive ion channels."}],"steps":[{"do":"Median at the wrist: palpate the distal wrist crease and the palmaris longus tendon (if present). Tap with a fingertip or the hammer just ulnar to the flexor carpi radialis, over the proximal carpal tunnel, from the distal wrist crease to about 1 cm distal to it. Use 4 to 6 light, crisp taps.","why":"The median nerve runs under the flexor retinaculum just ulnar to FCR and deep to palmaris longus; tapping here compresses it against the carpal bones. Several taps are needed because a single tap may be below threshold.","cue":"A correct positive is tingling shooting into the thumb, index, middle and radial half of the ring finger, not into the little finger and not just a sore wrist."},{"do":"Set the force: enough to be felt as a definite tap but not painful, roughly the force of percussing a chest or a knee reflex at its lightest. Keep it constant between sides and between visits.","why":"Excess force will produce paraesthesia in some normal nerves (false positive) and local pain that masks the radiating sensation; inconsistent force makes serial comparison meaningless.","cue":"The patient does not wince, and the normal side gives no paraesthesia at the same force."},{"do":"Ulnar at the elbow: flex the elbow to about 90 degrees, palpate the groove between the medial epicondyle and the olecranon, and tap over the nerve in the groove, then just distal to it over the cubital tunnel (between the two heads of flexor carpi ulnaris).","why":"Flexion stretches the ulnar nerve and narrows the cubital tunnel as the arcuate (Osborne) ligament tightens, so an irritable segment is easier to provoke. Distinguishing groove from tunnel helps localise.","cue":"Positive: tingling into the little finger and ulnar half of the ring finger. A 'funny bone' feeling that stays at the elbow is not positive."},{"do":"Common peroneal at the fibular neck: palpate the fibular head, slide two fingerbreadths distally and posteriorly to the fibular neck, and tap where the nerve can be rolled under the finger as it wraps the neck.","why":"The common peroneal nerve is subcutaneous against bone here and is the commonest lower-limb compression site (crossed legs, plaster, weight loss, prolonged squatting or kneeling).","cue":"Positive: tingling down the lateral leg into the dorsum of the foot and first web space."},{"do":"Posterior tibial at the tarsal tunnel: palpate the gap between the medial malleolus and the calcaneus, and tap along the line of the flexor retinaculum, just posterior and inferior to the malleolus.","why":"The tibial nerve passes under the flexor retinaculum with the long flexor tendons and posterior tibial vessels; this is the tarsal tunnel compression site.","cue":"Positive: tingling into the sole, heel (calcaneal branches) or medial or lateral plantar territory."},{"do":"For any suspected site, start tapping distally along the nerve's course and move proximally in steps of about 1 cm until paraesthesia first appears.","why":"Starting distal avoids tapping first over the most irritable point and sensitising the area, and it is essential in regeneration because the target is the most distal point that still produces tingling.","cue":"You find a clear transition point: no tingling distal to it, tingling at and proximal to it."},{"do":"Regeneration mapping after nerve injury or repair: tap from distal to proximal along the nerve, mark the most distal point that produces tingling referred distally, measure its distance from a fixed bony landmark, and repeat every 2 to 4 weeks.","why":"Regenerating axon sprouts are unmyelinated and mechanosensitive at their growing tips; the most distal provoking point marks the advancing front. Regeneration typically proceeds at roughly 1 mm per day (commonly quoted 1 to 3 mm per day), so a front that moves distally over time is reassuring.","cue":"The marked point moves distally at a plausible rate across visits; the tingling distal to the injury becomes progressively stronger than at the injury site."},{"do":"Repeat on the opposite side at the same site and force.","why":"A minority of asymptomatic people have a positive Tinel; a unilateral, symptom-matching response is more meaningful than a bilateral one.","cue":"Asymmetry that matches the clinical side."}],"normal":"Taps feel like taps. There may be mild local pressure or tenderness at the tap site, but no tingling or electric sensation referred into the nerve's distal distribution. After nerve injury, no Tinel or a Tinel that is stationary and weak at the repair site with no distal progression early on.","abnormal":[{"finding":"Tingling into the median distribution on tapping over the carpal tunnel","means":"Supports carpal tunnel syndrome, but weakly; combine with history, Katz hand diagram, hypalgesia and thenar weakness."},{"finding":"Tingling into the little and ulnar ring finger on tapping in the ulnar groove or cubital tunnel","means":"Supports ulnar neuropathy at the elbow, particularly if unilateral and associated with intrinsic weakness."},{"finding":"Advancing Tinel that moves distally at about 1 mm per day after a nerve injury or repair","means":"Axonal regeneration is progressing; a good prognostic sign, though it does not guarantee functional reinnervation."},{"finding":"Strong Tinel that stays fixed at the injury or repair site over several weeks, with no distal progression","means":"Possible neuroma in continuity or failed regeneration; warrants surgical or neurophysiological review."},{"finding":"Tingling at the fibular neck radiating to the dorsum of the foot, with foot-drop","means":"Common peroneal neuropathy at the fibular neck rather than L5 radiculopathy (check ankle inversion, which is spared in peroneal palsy)."}],"errors":[{"error":"Counting local pain or tenderness at the tap site as positive","result":"False positives, especially in tenosynovitis or after wrist trauma","fix":"Only record positive if paraesthesia radiates into the nerve's distal territory; ask the patient to point to where they feel it."},{"error":"Hitting hard, like a reflex blow, to 'make sure'","result":"Normal nerves can tingle and the patient tenses, giving false positives and pain","fix":"Use light, crisp taps of constant force and compare with the asymptomatic side."},{"error":"Tapping only one spot","result":"Missing a positive just proximal or distal, or failing to localise the lesion","fix":"Tap a short line along the nerve, distal to proximal, in roughly 1 cm steps."},{"error":"Tapping over the distal palm or thenar eminence for carpal tunnel","result":"You are over the recurrent motor branch or palmar cutaneous territory, not the tunnel; false negatives","fix":"Tap at and just distal to the distal wrist crease, ulnar to FCR, with the wrist slightly extended."},{"error":"Starting proximal when mapping regeneration","result":"You overestimate how far regeneration has reached because the most proximal sites are always irritable","fix":"Always tap from distal to proximal and record the first point that tingles."},{"error":"Leading the patient with 'Does that tingle in your fingers?'","result":"Suggestion produces false positives","fix":"Ask the open question 'What do you feel, and where?'."}],"trouble":[{"problem":"I can't feel the ulnar nerve in the groove","fix":"Flex the elbow fully, roll a finger transversely across the groove behind the medial epicondyle; the nerve feels like a cord. If it subluxes over the epicondyle on flexion, note that, and tap at 90 degrees instead where it sits in the groove."},{"problem":"The patient says everything tingles, even on the normal side","fix":"Reduce the force, test a control site away from any nerve (for example the dorsum of the forearm), and record the result as indeterminate rather than positive; rely on other tests."},{"problem":"In a repaired nerve I get tingling at several points along the limb","fix":"Record the most distal point that tingles referred distally; that is the advancing front. Compare the strength of the response at the repair versus distally: distal stronger than proximal over time is the favourable pattern."}],"pearls":["Jules Tinel and Paul Hoffmann described the sign independently in 1915 as a marker of regeneration, not as a diagnostic test for entrapment; its value in regeneration tracking is better than its value in diagnosing carpal tunnel syndrome.","A positive Tinel is common in asymptomatic people, so a negative test does not exclude carpal tunnel syndrome and a positive test alone does not establish it.","Conventions differ on finger versus hammer and on number of taps; what matters is that you use the same tool, force and number of taps each time and on both sides.","For cubital tunnel, the elbow flexion test (full flexion with wrist extended) combined with pressure over the nerve just proximal to the cubital tunnel is often more informative than tapping alone.","Foot-drop with tingling at the fibular neck but weak ankle inversion points away from peroneal neuropathy and towards L5 or sciatic involvement."],"evidence":[{"stat":"Tinel sign for carpal tunnel syndrome: positive LR about 1.4, negative LR about 0.8 (near-useless alone)","src":"D'Arcy & McGee 2000, JAMA (Rational Clinical Examination)"},{"stat":"Axonal regeneration after injury proceeds at roughly 1 mm per day (range commonly quoted 1 to 3 mm per day), the basis for tracking an advancing Tinel","src":"Macleod's Clinical Examination 15e"}],"document":"Normal: 'Tinel negative over carpal tunnel bilaterally.' Abnormal: 'R Tinel positive over carpal tunnel (paraesthesia to thumb, index, middle), L negative.' Regeneration: 'Advancing Tinel R median: most distal positive point 9 cm distal to medial epicondyle (6 cm at review 4 weeks ago).'","hook":"Tap where it is squeezed, feel where it goes: tingling must travel, and the front of a healing nerve moves a millimetre a day.","mcq":[{"q":"A GP taps firmly over the volar wrist of a patient with wrist pain after a fall. The patient says 'yes, that hurts' and points to the tap site. The GP records 'Tinel positive'. What is wrong?","opts":["Local pain is not a positive Tinel; paraesthesia must radiate into the nerve territory","The test should be done with the wrist fully flexed","Tinel cannot be done after trauma","Nothing; pain at the site is a positive Tinel"],"a":0,"why":"A positive Tinel is paraesthesia referred distally into the nerve's distribution. Local pain or tenderness, especially after trauma, is not a positive sign, and the force used should be light."},{"q":"Six weeks after a median nerve repair at the forearm, a doctor tracks recovery by tapping from the elbow downwards and records the first point that tingles. What should they change?","opts":["Use a tuning fork instead","Tap from distal to proximal and record the most distal point that tingles","Tap only at the repair site","Wait until 6 months before testing"],"a":1,"why":"The advancing front is the most distal provoking point. Starting proximally finds the always-irritable proximal segment and overestimates or misplaces the front."},{"q":"A patient with foot-drop has tingling into the dorsum of the foot when the fibular neck is tapped. Which examination finding best supports a common peroneal lesion over L5 radiculopathy?","opts":["Absent ankle jerk","Weak hip abduction","Preserved ankle inversion","Weak ankle eversion"],"a":2,"why":"Inversion is by tibialis posterior (tibial nerve, L5). It is spared in common peroneal palsy but weak in L5 radiculopathy. Eversion is weak in both."}],"src":["D'Arcy CA, McGee S. JAMA 2000;283:3110-3117","Macleod's Clinical Examination 15e","McGee, Evidence-Based Physical Diagnosis 5e","Talley & O'Connor, Clinical Examination"]},{"id":"phalen-durkan","name":"Phalen's, reverse Phalen's and carpal compression (Durkan) tests","group":"Peripheral nerve","signs":["phalen"],"purpose":"Provocation tests that raise carpal tunnel pressure to reproduce median-territory paraesthesia, used when a patient reports hand numbness or tingling and you need bedside support for or against carpal tunnel syndrome before deciding on splinting, injection or nerve conduction studies.","kit":["A watch or phone timer with a seconds display","Your thumbs (for Durkan); optionally a blood pressure cuff bulb or a dedicated compression device if you want standardised pressure","A Katz hand diagram (printed outline of both hands, palm and dorsum) for the patient to shade symptoms","A neurotip or disposable pin for testing hypalgesia in the index finger pulp"],"setup":[{"do":"Seat the patient with forearms free, shoulders relaxed, and ask them to shade their symptoms on a Katz hand diagram before you do any provocation.","why":"The hand diagram is more informative than the provocation tests; doing it first avoids the provocation contaminating the patient's description."},{"do":"Ask whether they have tingling right now and where, and establish the timer is ready before positioning.","why":"Phalen and Durkan are timed tests; resting paraesthesia must be recorded so you can distinguish provoked from ongoing symptoms."}],"steps":[{"do":"Phalen's: ask the patient to place the dorsal surfaces of both hands together, fingers pointing down, elbows out to the sides and forearms horizontal, so both wrists are passively flexed to about 90 degrees. Do not let them press hard.","why":"Full wrist flexion raises pressure within the carpal tunnel and moves the flexor tendons and proximal edge of the retinaculum against the median nerve, provoking ischaemic paraesthesia in a compromised nerve. Gravity-assisted flexion without forcing standardises the stimulus.","cue":"Both wrists at about 90 degrees flexion, fingers relaxed, elbows not resting on the thighs or table."},{"do":"Start the timer and hold for 60 seconds. Ask the patient to tell you as soon as they feel tingling or numbness and where.","why":"Phalen's original description used up to 60 seconds; most positive responses appear within this time and longer holds increase false positives in normal people.","cue":"You record the time to onset and the distribution."},{"do":"Record positive only if paraesthesia or numbness appears in the median territory (thumb, index, middle, radial ring finger) within 60 seconds.","why":"Diffuse whole-hand tingling or pain at the wrist dorsum from forced flexion is not specific to median compression.","cue":"Patient points to the radial digits; little finger is spared."},{"do":"Reverse Phalen's (prayer position): palms together in front of the chest, fingers pointing up, wrists extended to about 90 degrees, held for 60 seconds.","why":"Wrist extension also raises carpal tunnel pressure (more than flexion in some studies). It is useful when flexion is limited or painful, and adds information if Phalen is negative.","cue":"Forearms horizontal, wrists fully extended, symptoms recorded with time and distribution."},{"do":"Durkan carpal compression test: with the patient's forearm supinated and wrist in neutral, place both thumbs one on top of the other over the carpal tunnel (just distal to the distal wrist crease, between thenar and hypothenar eminences) and press firmly and steadily for 30 seconds.","why":"Direct pressure over the transverse carpal ligament raises local pressure on the median nerve without moving the wrist; Durkan's original description used about 150 mmHg for 30 seconds via a device. Neutral wrist avoids adding a flexion stimulus.","cue":"Steady, moderate pressure that blanches your thumbnails slightly; the patient should not report pain as the main sensation."},{"do":"Record positive if median-territory paraesthesia appears within 30 seconds of compression.","why":"Matching Durkan's protocol keeps your result comparable to published accuracy figures.","cue":"Time to onset noted; distribution median."},{"do":"Complete the assessment: test pinprick in the index finger pulp versus the ipsilateral little finger, inspect for thenar wasting, and test thumb abduction (abductor pollicis brevis) against resistance.","why":"Hypalgesia and weak thumb abduction carry more diagnostic weight than the provocation tests, and wasting indicates axonal loss that changes urgency.","cue":"You have a symptom map, provocation results, sensory and motor findings to combine."}],"normal":"No paraesthesia during 60 seconds of Phalen's or reverse Phalen's or 30 seconds of carpal compression. Mild wrist discomfort or a vague heaviness may occur but no tingling in the median digits.","abnormal":[{"finding":"Median-territory paraesthesia within 60 seconds of Phalen's","means":"Supports carpal tunnel syndrome, but only weakly; many people without CTS are positive and many with CTS are negative."},{"finding":"Paraesthesia within 30 seconds of carpal compression","means":"Supports CTS; original reports showed high accuracy that later studies have not consistently reproduced."},{"finding":"Katz diagram 'classic' or 'probable' pattern","means":"Moderately increases the likelihood of CTS; an 'unlikely' pattern reduces it."},{"finding":"Reduced pinprick in the index pulp compared with the little finger","means":"Moderately increases likelihood of CTS."},{"finding":"Thenar wasting and weak thumb abduction","means":"Advanced CTS with axonal loss; needs prompt nerve conduction studies and surgical referral."},{"finding":"Tingling into the little finger on Phalen's","means":"Not median; consider ulnar neuropathy, C8 radiculopathy, or a non-specific response."}],"errors":[{"error":"Patient pushing the backs of the hands hard together, or the examiner forcing flexion","result":"Wrist pain and non-specific paraesthesia; false positives","fix":"Let gravity flex the wrists with the dorsal hands touching lightly; no active pressing."},{"error":"Holding Phalen's for 2 minutes or until something happens","result":"False positives in normal people","fix":"Stop at 60 seconds for Phalen and 30 seconds for Durkan, and record the time to onset."},{"error":"Pressing over the thenar eminence or proximal forearm for Durkan","result":"Not over the tunnel; false negatives","fix":"Thumbs just distal to the distal wrist crease, centred between the thenar and hypothenar eminences."},{"error":"Accepting 'tingling' without asking where","result":"Whole-hand or ulnar symptoms recorded as positive","fix":"Ask the patient to point to the digits involved; positive requires median distribution."},{"error":"Relying on Phalen and Tinel to diagnose or exclude CTS","result":"Missed or over-diagnosed CTS","fix":"Weight the Katz diagram, hypalgesia and thumb abduction weakness more heavily, and request nerve conduction studies when management depends on it."}],"trouble":[{"problem":"The patient can't flex the wrists because of arthritis or a fracture","fix":"Use the carpal compression test with the wrist in neutral, or reverse Phalen's if extension is comfortable; document that Phalen's was not possible."},{"problem":"The patient already has tingling at rest","fix":"Record the baseline, then ask whether the tingling worsens or spreads during the test; a clear increase in median-territory symptoms counts, an unchanged baseline does not."},{"problem":"Results are equivocal on both sides","fix":"Fall back on the Katz diagram, pinprick comparison of index versus little finger, and APB strength; if management depends on it, refer for nerve conduction studies."}],"pearls":["In D'Arcy and McGee's review, the findings that moved probability most were hypalgesia, a classic or probable Katz diagram and weak thumb abduction; Phalen's and Tinel's barely changed it.","Timing conventions vary: Phalen's is usually 60 seconds, some sources use 30 to 60 seconds; Durkan's is 30 seconds. State the time you used.","Many patients describe nocturnal symptoms relieved by shaking the hand (flick sign); this history item is often more useful than any provocation manoeuvre.","Thenar wasting spares adductor pollicis and the first dorsal interosseous (ulnar); if the first web space is wasted too, think ulnar nerve, C8 or T1, or motor neurone disease."],"evidence":[{"stat":"Phalen sign: positive LR about 1.3, negative LR about 0.7","src":"D'Arcy & McGee 2000, JAMA (Rational Clinical Examination)"},{"stat":"Hypalgesia in the median territory: positive LR about 3.1","src":"D'Arcy & McGee 2000, JAMA (Rational Clinical Examination)"},{"stat":"Katz hand diagram classic or probable: positive LR about 2.4; unlikely pattern: negative LR about 0.5","src":"D'Arcy & McGee 2000, JAMA (Rational Clinical Examination)"},{"stat":"Carpal compression test in the original series: sensitivity 87%, specificity 90%, not consistently reproduced by later studies","src":"Durkan 1991, J Bone Joint Surg Am"}],"document":"Normal: 'Phalen's and carpal compression negative bilaterally at 60 s and 30 s. Pinprick symmetrical, no thenar wasting, APB 5/5.' Abnormal: 'R Phalen's positive at 25 s (thumb, index, middle), Durkan positive at 15 s. Katz classic pattern R. Reduced pinprick R index pulp vs R little finger. APB 4/5 R, mild thenar wasting.'","hook":"Flex for sixty, press for thirty, but let the hand diagram and the pin do the real talking.","mcq":[{"q":"A doctor asks a patient to press the backs of their hands firmly together for 2 minutes. At 100 seconds the whole hand tingles. The doctor records 'Phalen's positive'. What is the main problem?","opts":["The test should be done with palms together","The test must be done with the elbow extended","Excess force and duration with non-median distribution make this a false positive","Phalen's must be done one hand at a time"],"a":2,"why":"Phalen's uses gravity-assisted flexion without pressing, for 60 seconds, and requires median-territory paraesthesia. Prolonged forced flexion and whole-hand tingling are non-specific."},{"q":"Which bedside finding most increases the likelihood of carpal tunnel syndrome according to the JAMA Rational Clinical Examination review?","opts":["Positive Tinel's sign","Positive Phalen's sign","Wrist pain on flexion","Hypalgesia in the median territory"],"a":3,"why":"Hypalgesia had a positive LR around 3.1, whereas Tinel and Phalen had positive LRs around 1.3 to 1.4."},{"q":"A patient with a stiff post-fracture wrist cannot flex it. Which provocation test is most appropriate?","opts":["Carpal compression with the wrist in neutral for 30 seconds","Forced passive flexion by the examiner","Phalen's held for 3 minutes in partial flexion","Froment's sign"],"a":0,"why":"The Durkan test applies direct pressure over the carpal tunnel without wrist movement, so it can be done when flexion is limited."}],"src":["D'Arcy CA, McGee S. JAMA 2000;283:3110-3117","Durkan JA. J Bone Joint Surg Am 1991;73:535-538","McGee, Evidence-Based Physical Diagnosis 5e","Macleod's Clinical Examination 15e"]},{"id":"froment","name":"Froment's sign, card test and crossed-finger test","group":"Peripheral nerve","signs":["froment"],"purpose":"Detects weakness of the ulnar-innervated intrinsic hand muscles (adductor pollicis and the interossei), used when a patient has little-finger numbness, clumsiness or wasting and you need to confirm ulnar motor involvement and separate it from median or C8/T1 pathology.","kit":["A sheet of paper or thin card (A4 folded once is ideal), about 10 cm wide","A second sheet for the card test, or a playing card","Good light to see thumb interphalangeal joint posture"],"setup":[{"do":"Seat the patient facing you with both forearms pronated on a table or held in front of them, hands relaxed.","why":"Comparing both hands simultaneously exposes asymmetry in thumb posture, which is the whole sign; pronation lets you see the thumb IP joint side-on."},{"do":"Inspect first: first dorsal interosseous (first web space dorsally), hypothenar eminence, interosseous guttering between metacarpals, and clawing of the ring and little fingers.","why":"Wasting tells you the lesion is chronic and axonal; the tests then quantify which muscles are weak."}],"steps":[{"do":"Froment's: ask the patient to grip a sheet of paper between the pulp of each thumb and the radial side of the proximal phalanx of the index finger (a key pinch), thumbs uppermost, with both hands at once.","why":"Key pinch is powered mainly by adductor pollicis (deep branch of the ulnar nerve). With the thumb IP joint held extended, pinch tests adductor pollicis in relative isolation.","cue":"Both thumbs start straight, with the IP joint extended and the paper held between thumb and side of index."},{"do":"Grasp the far end of the paper and pull steadily away from the patient, telling them 'Don't let me take it'.","why":"As adductor pollicis fails, the patient recruits flexor pollicis longus (anterior interosseous branch of the median nerve) to clamp the paper, which flexes the thumb IP joint.","cue":"On the normal side the thumb stays straight; on the weak side the thumb IP joint flexes. That flexion is a positive Froment's sign."},{"do":"Watch the thumb metacarpophalangeal joint at the same time.","why":"Hyperextension of the thumb MCP during key pinch (Jeanne's sign) reflects loss of the stabilising action of adductor pollicis and flexor pollicis brevis deep head.","cue":"MCP hyperextension plus IP flexion is a zig-zag thumb: strongly ulnar."},{"do":"Card test: ask the patient to hold a card between two extended, adducted fingers (for example ring and little, or index and middle) while you try to pull it out. Compare sides.","why":"Finger adduction is by the palmar interossei (ulnar). Fingers must be extended at the MCP joints so long flexors cannot substitute by clamping.","cue":"On the weak side the card slides out with little resistance or the fingers flex to grip it."},{"do":"Crossed-finger test: ask the patient to cross the middle finger over the dorsum of the index finger with the hand flat on the table, palm down, then uncross and repeat on the other hand.","why":"Crossing requires coordinated abduction of the index (first dorsal interosseous) and adduction of the middle finger (second palmar interosseous), both ulnar-innervated; the flat hand prevents long flexor or extensor tricks.","cue":"Normal: crosses smoothly. Positive: cannot cross, or crosses only by lifting and flexing the fingers."},{"do":"Test first dorsal interosseous directly: hand flat, palm down, ask the patient to spread the index finger radially against your finger at the radial side of its proximal phalanx while you palpate the muscle belly in the first web space.","why":"Palpation confirms the muscle is contracting rather than the patient substituting with extensor indicis or tilting the hand.","cue":"You feel a firm belly hardening under your finger and can grade power using MRC."},{"do":"Test a median-innervated thenar muscle (abductor pollicis brevis) and an ulnar forearm muscle (flexor digitorum profundus to the little finger) for comparison.","why":"APB normal with ulnar intrinsics weak points to ulnar nerve; weak APB too points to C8/T1 or a lower trunk lesion. Weak FDP to the little finger places an ulnar lesion at or above the elbow.","cue":"You can localise: wrist (Guyon), elbow, or root/plexus."}],"normal":"Both thumbs stay straight at the IP joint and do not hyperextend at the MCP while the paper is pulled. The card is held firmly by extended fingers on both sides, and the middle finger crosses over the index easily with the hand flat.","abnormal":[{"finding":"Thumb IP flexion during key pinch (positive Froment's)","means":"Adductor pollicis weakness: ulnar neuropathy (elbow or wrist) or C8/T1 lesion."},{"finding":"Thumb MCP hyperextension during pinch (Jeanne's sign)","means":"Adductor pollicis and deep FPB weakness; same localisation, often more advanced."},{"finding":"Weak card hold and inability to cross fingers with normal APB","means":"Ulnar motor lesion; check FDP to little finger and dorsal ulnar sensation to localise elbow versus Guyon's canal."},{"finding":"Ulnar intrinsic weakness plus weak APB and FPL","means":"C8/T1 root, lower brachial plexus (consider Pancoast tumour, thoracic outlet), or motor neurone disease."},{"finding":"Persistent abduction of the little finger (Wartenberg's sign)","means":"Weak third palmar interosseous with unopposed extensor digiti minimi; ulnar neuropathy."}],"errors":[{"error":"Letting the patient start with the thumb tip opposed to the index pulp (tip pinch)","result":"Tests FPL and median-innervated muscles rather than adductor pollicis; false negative","fix":"Paper between the thumb pulp and the radial side of the index proximal phalanx, thumb straight at the start."},{"error":"Testing one hand at a time","result":"Subtle IP flexion missed because there is no side-by-side comparison","fix":"Pull paper from both hands simultaneously, or alternate quickly with identical force."},{"error":"Allowing the fingers to flex during the card test","result":"Long flexors clamp the card; false negative","fix":"Keep the fingers fully extended at the MCP and IP joints and the hand flat."},{"error":"Pulling with a sudden jerk","result":"Normal subjects flex the thumb reflexively; false positive","fix":"Pull steadily and progressively over 2 to 3 seconds."},{"error":"Ignoring the long-standing posture of a stiff or arthritic thumb","result":"Fixed IP flexion misread as Froment's","fix":"Check passive IP extension first; Froment's is a dynamic change during pinch from a straight starting position."}],"trouble":[{"problem":"The patient's thumb flexes on both sides","fix":"Reduce your pulling force so the normal side is just holding, then compare. If still bilateral and symmetrical, consider bilateral ulnar neuropathy or a C8/T1 process and test individual muscles with MRC grading."},{"problem":"I can't tell whether they are crossing the fingers properly","fix":"Have the hand flat palm-down with the forearm on the table; watch the index MCP. A genuine cross keeps the fingers extended; a trick lifts and flexes them off the table."},{"problem":"The patient has rheumatoid arthritis with thumb deformity","fix":"Rely on the card test, crossed-finger test and direct palpation of the first dorsal interosseous, and note that Froment's could not be interpreted."}],"pearls":["Froment's is really a test of substitution: the median nerve (FPL) is rescuing a failing ulnar muscle (adductor pollicis), so a combined median and ulnar lesion may show weakness without the classic sign.","Weak FDP to the little and ring fingers plus intrinsic weakness places the ulnar lesion at or above the elbow; preserved FDP and preserved dorsal ulnar sensation suggest Guyon's canal.","The 'ulnar paradox': a lesion at the wrist produces worse clawing than one at the elbow because FDP is preserved and pulls the fingers into flexion.","Sensory loss splitting the ring finger suggests a peripheral nerve; loss extending into the medial forearm suggests C8 or the medial cutaneous nerve of the forearm (plexus), not the ulnar nerve."],"evidence":[{"stat":"Bedside intrinsic muscle tests (Froment's, card, crossed-finger) have limited published accuracy data; they are best used to localise in combination rather than as stand-alone diagnostic tests","src":"Macleod's Clinical Examination 15e"},{"stat":"Froment's sign reflects substitution of flexor pollicis longus (median, anterior interosseous) for weak adductor pollicis (ulnar); a combined median and ulnar lesion can abolish the sign","src":"Aids to the Examination of the Peripheral Nervous System (Guarantors of Brain)"}],"document":"Normal: 'Froment's negative bilaterally; card test and crossed-finger test normal; 1st DI and ADM 5/5.' Abnormal: 'L Froment's positive with Jeanne's sign; weak card hold L; unable to cross middle over index L. 1st DI 3/5, ADM 4/5, APB 5/5, FDP V 4/5 L. Sensory loss L little and ulnar ring finger including dorsum. Consistent with L ulnar neuropathy at elbow.'","hook":"When the ulnar adductor fails, the median FPL bends the thumb to cheat.","mcq":[{"q":"A doctor tests Froment's by asking the patient to hold paper between the thumb tip and the index fingertip. The thumbs stay rounded on both sides. What is wrong?","opts":["The paper should be pulled faster","Tip pinch uses FPL by design; the paper must be held between the thumb pulp and the side of the index with the thumb straight","The test should be done with the wrist flexed","Nothing, the test is negative"],"a":1,"why":"Froment's tests adductor pollicis in key (lateral) pinch from a straight thumb. Tip pinch already uses FPL and cannot reveal substitution."},{"q":"A patient has a positive Froment's sign on the right, weak first dorsal interosseous, normal APB and normal FDP to the little finger, with normal sensation on the dorsum of the hand. Where is the lesion most likely?","opts":["C8 root","Ulnar nerve at the elbow","Median nerve at the wrist","Ulnar nerve at the wrist (Guyon's canal)"],"a":3,"why":"Sparing of FDP and the dorsal ulnar cutaneous branch (which leaves the nerve proximal to the wrist) localises to Guyon's canal; APB being normal excludes C8/T1."},{"q":"During the card test, a doctor notes the patient flexes their fingers to grip the card and holds it well. What should the doctor do?","opts":["Repeat with fingers fully extended at the MCP and IP joints","Record normal","Switch to Phalen's test","Test with the forearm supinated"],"a":0,"why":"Flexion lets the long flexors (FDS and FDP) substitute for the palmar interossei, giving a false negative."}],"src":["Macleod's Clinical Examination 15e","Talley & O'Connor, Clinical Examination","Aids to the Examination of the Peripheral Nervous System (Guarantors of Brain)"]},{"id":"mrc-power","name":"Testing and grading muscle power (MRC scale)","group":"Peripheral nerve","signs":[],"purpose":"Quantifies weakness muscle by muscle so you can map it to a root, nerve, plexus, upper motor neurone pattern or functional disorder, and compare reliably over time. Use it whenever a patient reports weakness or you find abnormal tone, reflexes or wasting.","kit":["An examination couch the patient can lie supine and prone on, plus a chair","Your two hands: one to stabilise the proximal segment, one to resist","A chart of muscle, root and nerve (for example the Guarantors of Brain booklet) if you need to localise"],"setup":[{"do":"Expose the limbs to see the muscle bellies, inspect for wasting and fasciculation, and assess tone before power.","why":"Wasting and fasciculation point to lower motor neurone disease; tone changes alter how you interpret weakness; seeing the belly lets you confirm contraction."},{"do":"Position for each muscle so the movement is in a single plane and the proximal joint can be fixed. Use supine or prone positions for hip and knee testing, and sitting for upper limb.","why":"Isolation prevents substitution by other muscles; correct positioning also determines whether gravity is acting with or against the movement, which matters for grades 2 and 3."},{"do":"Stand on the side being tested and match your body position to the force: use your arm, not your whole body weight, for small muscles; brace your forearm for large ones.","why":"You need enough force to overcome a normal muscle of that size; examiners routinely underestimate quadriceps and gastrocnemius strength."}],"steps":[{"do":"Demonstrate the movement and ask the patient to adopt and hold the test position: for example elbow flexed to 90 degrees with forearm supinated for biceps.","why":"Most muscles generate peak force in mid-range, where actin and myosin overlap is optimal (length-tension relationship); testing at end-range makes normal muscles feel weak.","cue":"The joint is roughly mid-range and the patient understands what to resist."},{"do":"Stabilise the proximal segment with one hand: for biceps hold the arm against the trunk at the elbow; for hip abduction stabilise the pelvis; for wrist extension support the forearm.","why":"Fixing the proximal joint removes trick movements, so the force you feel comes from the target muscle alone.","cue":"The proximal segment does not move as you apply resistance."},{"do":"Apply resistance close to the distal end of the moving segment, in the opposite direction to the muscle's action, building force smoothly over 1 to 2 seconds ('break' test: the patient holds, you try to overcome).","why":"A distal lever arm magnifies your force; a smooth build lets the patient recruit fully. The break test is the convention for MRC grading of grade 4 and above.","cue":"A normal muscle holds firm; a weak one gives smoothly through range."},{"do":"Grade from the top down: if the muscle cannot hold against any resistance, test against gravity (3); if it cannot move against gravity, reposition so gravity is eliminated (2); if no movement, look and palpate for a flicker (1 versus 0).","why":"MRC grades are defined by what the muscle can overcome: 5 normal, 4 movement against gravity and resistance, 3 against gravity only through full range, 2 with gravity eliminated, 1 flicker or trace, 0 none.","cue":"You assign a single defined grade, not a guess."},{"do":"Compare each muscle immediately with the same muscle on the other side, using the same position and force.","why":"Mild weakness (4 or 4+) is detected mainly by asymmetry; normal strength varies widely between people.","cue":"You can state whether there is asymmetry independently of the absolute grade."},{"do":"Assess effort and the quality of any give: a smooth, consistent yield is organic; sudden collapse after initially normal force (give-way), or variable force with retry, suggests non-organic or pain-limited weakness.","why":"Give-way is common in functional weakness and also in pain or poor understanding; it should be recorded rather than converted into a low MRC grade.","cue":"You note 'give-way' explicitly and look for supporting signs such as Hoover's sign."},{"do":"Use functional tests for very strong muscles: rise from a squat or a low chair without hands, walk on heels and toes, single-leg heel raises (10 times each side).","why":"Quadriceps and calf muscles are stronger than an examiner's arm, so manual testing will miss mild weakness; body-weight tests are more sensitive.","cue":"Inability to do 10 single-leg heel raises on one side reveals S1 or gastrocnemius weakness that felt 5/5 by hand."},{"do":"Map the pattern: upper motor neurone (pyramidal) weakness affects upper limb extensors and abductors more than flexors (shoulder abduction, elbow and wrist extension, finger extension and abduction) and lower limb flexors more than extensors (hip flexion, knee flexion, ankle dorsiflexion and eversion).","why":"The pyramidal pattern reflects the corticospinal tract's preferential supply to these muscles and the preserved antigravity muscles; recognising it shifts you from a root or nerve search to a central one.","cue":"You can describe the weakness as pyramidal, myotomal, nerve-territory, proximal symmetrical (myopathic) or distal symmetrical (neuropathic)."}],"normal":"Every tested muscle holds firm against full examiner resistance at mid-range, symmetrically, with smooth effort and no give-way: MRC 5/5 throughout. The patient can rise from a squat and do 10 single-leg heel raises on each side.","abnormal":[{"finding":"Pyramidal pattern weakness with increased tone and brisk reflexes","means":"Upper motor neurone lesion (brain or cord)."},{"finding":"Weakness confined to one myotome (for example shoulder abduction and elbow flexion: C5) with a reduced reflex","means":"Radiculopathy at that level."},{"finding":"Weakness confined to one nerve territory (for example wrist and finger extension with preserved triceps: radial nerve at the spiral groove)","means":"Mononeuropathy; localise by which branches are spared."},{"finding":"Symmetrical proximal weakness with preserved reflexes and sensation","means":"Myopathy (consider statin, thyroid, steroid, inflammatory) or neuromuscular junction disorder if fatiguable."},{"finding":"Fatiguable weakness that worsens with repeated testing","means":"Myasthenia gravis; test sustained upgaze and repeated shoulder abduction."},{"finding":"Give-way weakness, inconsistent with function, with a positive Hoover's sign","means":"Functional neurological disorder; document positive signs rather than 'non-organic'."}],"errors":[{"error":"Testing at end-range, for example biceps with the elbow fully flexed","result":"Normal muscles appear weak (or the patient locks the joint and appears strong)","fix":"Test at mid-range (around 90 degrees for elbow flexion), where force generation is greatest."},{"error":"Not fixing the proximal joint","result":"Substitution by other muscles hides weakness, for example trunk lean for hip abduction","fix":"Stabilise the proximal segment with your other hand for every muscle."},{"error":"Grading 4 when the muscle only moves against gravity through part of the range","result":"Overestimating strength","fix":"Grade 3 requires full range against gravity; less than full range against gravity is 2 (some use 3-)."},{"error":"Calling quadriceps 5/5 after a manual test in a young adult","result":"Missed L3/L4 or myopathic weakness","fix":"Add a squat or chair rise without hands, and single-leg step-up."},{"error":"Converting give-way into a grade such as 3/5","result":"Mislabelled weakness and inappropriate investigations","fix":"Record the maximum briefly achieved and 'give-way', and examine for positive functional signs."},{"error":"Testing the two sides minutes apart or at different joint angles","result":"Mild asymmetry missed","fix":"Test left and right in immediate succession, same position and force."}],"trouble":[{"problem":"I can't tell 4+ from 5 in a big muscle","fix":"Change the lever arm (resist more distally), use your body weight with a braced forearm, and use functional tests (heel raises, squat, hopping). If asymmetry persists only on functional testing, trust it."},{"problem":"The patient is in pain and gives way","fix":"Ask whether pain limits them, test a pain-free part of the range, record 'pain-limited' rather than a grade, and reassess after analgesia."},{"problem":"I suspect functional weakness","fix":"Check Hoover's sign (involuntary hip extension when the other leg flexes against resistance) and hip abductor sign; describe the pattern positively and avoid labelling effort as poor in front of the patient."}],"pearls":["The 4-, 4, 4+ subdivisions are a widely used extension, not part of the original MRC scale; define them in your notes (for example 4- slight, 4 moderate, 4+ marked resistance) and be consistent.","Grade 4 covers a huge range of strength (from barely against resistance to almost normal), which is why inter-rater agreement is weakest there; use functional measures when tracking change.","Wrist extension and finger abduction are the most sensitive upper limb muscles for a mild pyramidal weakness; pair them with pronator drift.","Myasthenia, Lambert-Eaton and periodic paralysis can be normal at rest; repeat testing after exercise if the history suggests fatiguability.","When MRC grading is too coarse (for example in Guillain-Barre syndrome or myositis), add the MRC sum score or timed functional tests."],"evidence":[{"stat":"MRC grades 0 to 3 are reliable between examiners, but agreement for grade 4 is poor because it spans a wide range of strength","src":"Macleod's Clinical Examination 15e"},{"stat":"The original MRC grading scale (0 to 5) was published in 'Aids to the Investigation of Peripheral Nerve Injuries', the predecessor of the Guarantors of Brain booklet","src":"Medical Research Council, Aids to the Investigation of Peripheral Nerve Injuries (War Memorandum No. 7)"}],"document":"Normal: 'Tone normal. Power 5/5 throughout upper and lower limbs bilaterally, including heel and toe walk and squat.' Abnormal: 'R pyramidal pattern weakness: shoulder abduction 4/5, elbow extension 4/5, wrist extension 4-/5, finger abduction 4/5; hip flexion 4/5, knee flexion 4+/5, ankle dorsiflexion 4/5. L 5/5. Give-way not present.'","hook":"Mid-range, fix proximal, resist distal, compare at once: the grade is what it beats, not what it feels like.","mcq":[{"q":"A doctor tests elbow flexion with the patient's elbow fully flexed, hand at the shoulder, and records biceps 4/5. What is the main error?","opts":["The forearm should be pronated","Biceps cannot be graded on MRC","The elbow should be extended","End-range testing underestimates strength; test at about 90 degrees"],"a":3,"why":"Muscles generate maximum force in mid-range because of the length-tension relationship; end-range testing makes normal muscles feel weak."},{"q":"A patient can lift the leg off the bed through half the range of hip flexion but not fully against gravity. What MRC grade fits?","opts":["2 (some record 3-)","3","4-","1"],"a":0,"why":"Grade 3 requires full range against gravity. Partial range against gravity is below 3; strictly it is 2 if full range is possible with gravity eliminated, often recorded as 3- by convention."},{"q":"A young man with back pain has quadriceps 5/5 on manual testing but cannot rise from a low chair without his hands on the left. What should the doctor do?","opts":["Record 5/5 as the manual test is the standard","Trust the functional test, record left quadriceps weakness and look for an L3/L4 cause","Record give-way weakness","Retest with the knee fully extended"],"a":1,"why":"Quadriceps are stronger than an examiner's arm; functional tests detect mild weakness that manual testing misses."}],"src":["Aids to the Examination of the Peripheral Nervous System (Guarantors of Brain)","Macleod's Clinical Examination 15e","Talley & O'Connor, Clinical Examination"]},{"id":"vibration-proprioception","name":"Vibration sense and joint position sense","group":"Sensory","signs":[],"purpose":"Tests the large-fibre dorsal column (posterior column-medial lemniscus) pathway. Use it to detect length-dependent large-fibre neuropathy (diabetes, B12, alcohol), dorsal column disease (B12, copper, tabes, compression), and to explain sensory ataxia and a positive Romberg.","kit":["128 Hz tuning fork (not 256 or 512 Hz, which are for hearing); a Rydel-Seiffer graduated 128 Hz fork if you want a quantitative score","Watch with a seconds display for the timed method","A quiet room; patient's eyes closed or a card shielding their view"],"setup":[{"do":"Patient lying supine with feet bare, socks off, legs relaxed. Explain and demonstrate first: strike the fork, place it on the sternum or chin and say 'this is the buzzing; tell me when it stops'. Then touch with a still fork so they know the difference.","why":"Patients confuse pressure with vibration; demonstrating on a normal site calibrates them and lets you verify understanding before testing the feet."},{"do":"For joint position, demonstrate with eyes open: 'This is up, this is down', then ask them to close their eyes.","why":"Patients must know which direction counts as up for each digit; without a practice run you test comprehension, not proprioception."}],"steps":[{"do":"Strike the tuning fork prongs against your thenar eminence or knee (not a hard surface) so it vibrates without a metallic ring.","why":"Striking a hard surface produces overtones and an unpredictable amplitude; a soft strike gives a consistent, pure 128 Hz vibration.","cue":"The prongs vibrate visibly; there is little audible ring."},{"do":"Place the base of the fork firmly and perpendicularly on the dorsum of the interphalangeal joint of the hallux (just proximal to the nail), holding the fork by the stem only.","why":"Bone conducts vibration well and the most distal site detects length-dependent neuropathy earliest. Holding by the stem avoids damping the prongs.","cue":"The patient reports buzzing (not just touch)."},{"do":"Timed method: ask the patient to say 'stop' when the vibration fades away. Immediately transfer the fork to your own corresponding site (for example your distal finger joint or hallux if accessible) and count the seconds you still feel it.","why":"This compares the patient's threshold with a reference, removing variability in how hard the fork was struck. A patient who stops feeling it while you still feel it for many seconds has an elevated threshold.","cue":"You can state 'patient perceived x seconds less than examiner'."},{"do":"Do a sham: occasionally place a non-vibrating fork, or stop the prongs with your fingers while the base remains in place, and ask whether it is buzzing.","why":"Verifies that the patient is reporting vibration rather than pressure.","cue":"The patient correctly reports 'not buzzing'."},{"do":"If abnormal at the hallux, move proximally in order: medial malleolus, tibial tuberosity, anterior superior iliac spine; in the arms from the distal finger joint to the ulnar styloid, olecranon and acromion. Stop once sensation is normal.","why":"Defines the upper limit of loss, separating length-dependent neuropathy (gradient) from a dorsal column or spinal level (a level on the trunk or spinous processes).","cue":"You can describe 'lost to the knee' or 'reduced to the ankle'."},{"do":"Joint position: hold the patient's hallux by its sides (medial and lateral surfaces at the proximal phalanx), stabilising the proximal joint with your other hand. With eyes closed, move the distal phalanx up or down in small (a few degrees) irregular movements, returning to neutral between each.","why":"Holding the sides avoids giving pressure cues from the dorsum or plantar surface, which would let the patient guess direction from touch rather than proprioception. Stabilising the proximal joint isolates the joint being tested.","cue":"You touch only the sides; the movement is barely visible."},{"do":"Do 5 to 10 trials in a random sequence (not alternating up-down-up). If incorrect, increase the excursion, then move proximally: ankle, knee; in the hand from the distal finger joint to the wrist and elbow.","why":"Random sequences prevent guessing; normal people detect a few degrees at the hallux, so failing small movements but succeeding with large ones is mild loss.","cue":"A score such as 10 of 10 correct for small movements, or the level at which it becomes normal."},{"do":"Compare both sides and both the feet and hands.","why":"Length-dependent neuropathy is symmetrical and feet-first; asymmetry or hands-first suggests a mononeuritis, ganglionopathy or central lesion.","cue":"You can classify the distribution."}],"normal":"Vibration felt at the hallux for nearly as long as the examiner feels it (difference of a few seconds in young adults; elderly people may lose some vibration at the toes). Joint position: correct direction detected for small movements at the hallux in nearly all trials.","abnormal":[{"finding":"Vibration reduced at the hallux, normal at the knee, symmetrical","means":"Length-dependent large-fibre peripheral neuropathy (for example diabetes, B12, alcohol)."},{"finding":"Vibration and joint position lost in the legs with a level on the trunk, often with brisk reflexes and extensor plantars","means":"Dorsal column or cord lesion (subacute combined degeneration, compression, MS)."},{"finding":"Joint position loss in hands and feet with normal power and areflexia, sensory ataxia","means":"Sensory neuronopathy (ganglionopathy): Sjogren, paraneoplastic, cisplatin."},{"finding":"Joint position loss with a positive Romberg test","means":"Proprioceptive (sensory) ataxia rather than cerebellar ataxia."},{"finding":"Pseudoathetosis of outstretched fingers with eyes closed","means":"Severe proprioceptive loss in the arms (cervical dorsal column or large-fibre neuropathy)."}],"errors":[{"error":"Using a 256 or 512 Hz fork","result":"Higher frequency decays faster and is less suited to bony vibration; false positives for loss","fix":"Use a 128 Hz fork for vibration testing."},{"error":"Placing the fork on soft tissue rather than bone, or holding it by the prongs","result":"Damped vibration; false positive loss","fix":"Place the base on a bony prominence and hold the stem only."},{"error":"Asking 'Can you feel this?'","result":"Patient reports pressure, not vibration; false negative","fix":"Ask the patient to say when the buzzing stops, and use sham trials."},{"error":"Holding the toe on its top and bottom surfaces for joint position","result":"Pressure cues reveal direction; false negative","fix":"Hold the sides of the toe, and stabilise the proximal joint."},{"error":"Large, predictable up-down-up movements","result":"Patient guesses; mild loss missed","fix":"Use small, random movements and return to neutral each time."},{"error":"Starting at the knee or ankle","result":"Missing early length-dependent loss","fix":"Always start at the most distal site and move proximally only if abnormal."}],"trouble":[{"problem":"The patient says it is buzzing even when it is not","fix":"Re-demonstrate with the sternum, then interleave sham trials. Use the timed method, which depends on 'stop' rather than 'yes'."},{"problem":"I can't tell whether reduced vibration is just ageing","fix":"Use the timed method against your own threshold or a Rydel-Seiffer fork with age-adjusted norms; symmetrical mild reduction at the toes in a person over 70 may be normal, but loss at the ankle or asymmetry is not."},{"problem":"The patient has a toe amputation or deformity","fix":"Test vibration at the first metatarsal head or medial malleolus, and joint position at the ankle; document the site used."}],"pearls":["Vibration is usually the earliest modality lost in length-dependent large-fibre neuropathy; joint position is relatively preserved until later.","The Rydel-Seiffer fork has triangles on the dampers that move with decay; the reading (0 to 8) at the moment the patient stops feeling it is the score. Cut-offs are age-dependent and vary between sources.","The on-off method (can the patient feel vibration at all) and the timed method are both described; the timed method is more sensitive for mild loss.","A sensory level for vibration on the spinous processes or trunk points to the cord; a peripheral gradient points to nerve.","Romberg's test is a test of proprioception, not cerebellar function: if joint position at the toes is normal, a positive Romberg needs another explanation (vestibular, functional)."],"evidence":[{"stat":"In diabetic patients, abnormal vibration perception (timed or on-off tuning fork) is among the findings that most increase the likelihood of large-fibre neuropathy, whereas normal results lower it","src":"Kanji et al 2010, JAMA (Rational Clinical Examination)"},{"stat":"Vibration sense at the toes declines with normal ageing, so thresholds in older people must be interpreted against age-adjusted norms","src":"McGee, Evidence-Based Physical Diagnosis 5e"}],"document":"Normal: 'Vibration (128 Hz) intact at hallux bilaterally (within 3 s of examiner). JPS intact at hallux bilaterally (10/10).' Abnormal: 'Vibration absent at hallux and medial malleolus bilaterally, present at tibial tuberosity. JPS impaired at hallux (5/10 small movements), intact at ankle. Romberg positive.'","hook":"Bone, 128, distal first; hold the toe by its sides and move it small and random.","mcq":[{"q":"A doctor holds a patient's great toe by its upper and lower surfaces and moves it up and down in large alternating movements. The patient answers correctly every time. What is wrong?","opts":["Joint position must be tested at the ankle first","Eyes should be open","Holding the dorsal and plantar surfaces gives pressure cues and alternating movements allow guessing","The test should use a tuning fork"],"a":2,"why":"Hold the sides of the toe, use small random movements and return to neutral; otherwise touch cues and pattern guessing produce false negatives."},{"q":"A doctor strikes a 512 Hz tuning fork on the bedside table and places it on the patient's ankle. The patient feels nothing. What is the most appropriate next step?","opts":["Repeat with a 128 Hz fork struck on your hand, placed on the hallux distal joint first","Record vibration absent to the ankle","Move to the knee","Test light touch instead"],"a":0,"why":"Vibration testing uses a 128 Hz fork, softly struck, starting at the most distal bony site. A 512 Hz fork decays quickly and gives false-positive loss."},{"q":"A patient has absent joint position sense at the toes, a positive Romberg and normal finger-nose testing. What is the best interpretation?","opts":["Cerebellar ataxia","Sensory (proprioceptive) ataxia","Vestibular neuritis","Functional disorder"],"a":1,"why":"Romberg tests reliance on proprioception; with absent joint position sense and normal cerebellar tests, the ataxia is sensory."}],"src":["Macleod's Clinical Examination 15e","Kanji JN et al. JAMA 2010;303:1526-1532","McGee, Evidence-Based Physical Diagnosis 5e","Talley & O'Connor, Clinical Examination"]},{"id":"pinprick-sensory-level","name":"Pinprick and light touch mapping, sensory level and sacral sparing","group":"Sensory","signs":[],"purpose":"Maps the territory of sensory loss to distinguish dermatomal (root), peripheral nerve, length-dependent (glove and stocking) and spinal cord patterns, and finds a sensory level on the trunk in suspected cord compression or myelitis. Reach for it in any patient with numbness, back pain with red flags, or limb weakness.","kit":["Single-use neurological pin (Neurotip), never a hypodermic needle or a reused pin","Cotton wool wisp for light touch","Skin marker pen to mark boundaries","A dermatome chart if you need it"],"setup":[{"do":"Explain, then demonstrate pinprick on the sternum or a known normal area: 'This is sharp. This is blunt [touch with the other end].' Then ask them to close their eyes and answer 'sharp' or 'blunt' each time.","why":"Pinprick tests the spinothalamic pathway by asking for sharpness; calibrating on a normal area gives a personal reference and makes the test sharp-versus-blunt discrimination rather than 'can you feel this'."},{"do":"Expose the area needed: both limbs and, for a level, the whole trunk front and back with the patient supine and then sitting or rolled.","why":"A sensory level can be missed if you only test the anterior trunk or limbs; the back and saddle region must be accessible."}],"steps":[{"do":"Use a fresh Neurotip, applying just enough pressure to feel sharp without denting deeply or drawing blood, with the same pressure each time.","why":"Consistent pressure keeps the stimulus constant; needles pierce skin and transmit blood-borne infection; heavy pressure stimulates pressure receptors and blurs the boundary.","cue":"A slight indentation, no skin break; the patient reports 'sharp' on normal skin reliably."},{"do":"Screen in a pattern: test one point in each dermatome or nerve territory on both sides in turn (for example C5 lateral arm, C6 thumb, C7 middle finger, C8 little finger, T1 medial forearm; L2 anterior thigh, L3 medial knee, L4 medial malleolus, L5 dorsum of foot and first web space, S1 lateral foot and heel).","why":"Left-right comparison at each site detects asymmetry; testing in dermatomes and nerve territories gives you a map rather than random points.","cue":"You can state which areas are normal and which differ."},{"do":"When you find an abnormal area, map its edges by moving from the abnormal area towards normal skin in short steps (about 1 to 2 cm) and mark where sensation changes; repeat from several directions.","why":"Patients detect the onset of a normal sensation more precisely than its disappearance, so moving from abnormal to normal gives a sharper, reproducible boundary.","cue":"A clear marked border that matches a dermatome, a nerve territory or a glove and stocking pattern."},{"do":"Glove and stocking: start at the toes and move proximally up the leg in steps, asking when it becomes normal sharp; repeat in the hands from the fingertips.","why":"Length-dependent neuropathy affects the longest axons first, so loss starts distally and has a gradient rather than a sharp dermatomal border. Hands typically become affected when leg loss reaches the knee.","cue":"Symmetric distal loss with a graded upper limit (for example mid-shin)."},{"do":"Sensory level: with the patient supine, start in the legs or lower abdomen where sensation is reduced and move upward in the midline and in the mid-clavicular lines, 1 to 2 cm at a time, until it becomes normal. Repeat on the back (patient sitting or rolled) along the paraspinal line.","why":"A sensory level is the upper border of a band of loss on the trunk; moving upward from abnormal to normal defines it best, and testing front and back confirms it is horizontal.","cue":"A horizontal border at a consistent level front and back; use landmarks: nipples T4, xiphisternum T6, umbilicus T10, inguinal ligament T12/L1."},{"do":"Test the saddle region (S3 to S5, perianal skin) for pinprick and light touch in any patient with a spinal cord or cauda equina question.","why":"Saddle sensory loss with urinary retention suggests cauda equina syndrome; sacral sparing (preserved perianal sensation below a level) suggests an intramedullary (central cord) lesion because sacral fibres lie most laterally in the spinothalamic tract and are spared by a lesion expanding from the centre.","cue":"You have explicitly documented perianal sensation as present or absent."},{"do":"Light touch: dab (do not stroke) a cotton wool wisp at the same sites, asking the patient to say 'yes' each time, with variable timing.","why":"Stroking stimulates hair follicles and may cross territories; dabbing at irregular intervals prevents anticipation. Light touch is carried in both the dorsal columns and spinothalamic tracts, so it is less localising than pinprick or vibration.","cue":"Reliable 'yes' responses on normal skin with no false responses during pauses."},{"do":"Map pain and temperature separately from dorsal column modalities if a cord lesion is suspected, testing both sides.","why":"Dissociated sensory loss localises: Brown-Sequard gives ipsilateral dorsal column loss and contralateral spinothalamic loss starting one to two segments below; a central cord lesion (syrinx) gives suspended, cape-like spinothalamic loss with preserved dorsal columns.","cue":"You can describe which modality is lost on which side."}],"normal":"Pinprick felt as sharp and equally sharp on both sides at every dermatome and nerve territory, with no level on the trunk, intact perianal sensation, and light touch felt reliably everywhere.","abnormal":[{"finding":"Loss in a single dermatome strip (for example L5 over the dorsum of the foot and first web space)","means":"Radiculopathy at that root; borders are often blurred because dermatomes overlap."},{"finding":"Loss with a sharp border matching a single nerve territory (for example the lateral thigh in meralgia paraesthetica)","means":"Mononeuropathy of that nerve."},{"finding":"Symmetrical distal glove and stocking loss with a gradient","means":"Length-dependent peripheral neuropathy."},{"finding":"Horizontal sensory level on the trunk, front and back","means":"Spinal cord lesion at or above that level; urgent MRI of the whole spine."},{"finding":"Saddle anaesthesia","means":"Cauda equina or conus medullaris lesion; emergency MRI."},{"finding":"Sensory level with preserved perianal sensation (sacral sparing)","means":"Intramedullary or central cord lesion rather than extrinsic compression."}],"errors":[{"error":"Using a hypodermic needle or a reused safety pin","result":"Skin puncture, bleeding and blood-borne virus risk; inconsistent stimulus","fix":"Use a single-use Neurotip and dispose of it in a sharps bin after each patient."},{"error":"Asking 'Can you feel this?'","result":"Patients feel the touch even when spinothalamic function is lost; false negatives","fix":"Ask 'sharp or blunt?' and include blunt stimuli randomly."},{"error":"Moving from normal to abnormal","result":"Boundaries placed inaccurately, levels misjudged","fix":"Move from the abnormal area towards normal, and mark where sensation changes."},{"error":"Testing only the anterior trunk","result":"Missed or misread sensory level","fix":"Test front and back, and include the saddle area."},{"error":"Stroking with cotton wool","result":"Crossing territories and hair-follicle stimulation; poor localisation","fix":"Dab lightly at single points at irregular intervals."},{"error":"Assuming the sensory level equals the level of the lesion","result":"Imaging the wrong segment and missing a higher lesion","fix":"Image the whole spine or at least well above the sensory level; the lesion may be several segments higher."}],"trouble":[{"problem":"The patient's answers are inconsistent","fix":"Slow down, re-demonstrate on the sternum, use fewer points, and check whether they are tired or anxious. Record the best reproducible map; inconsistency alone is not evidence of functional disorder."},{"problem":"I can't decide between a dermatome and a nerve territory in the hand","fix":"Check whether the loss splits the ring finger (median or ulnar) or extends into the forearm (root or plexus). Then test power in muscles of the same root but different nerves."},{"problem":"The level is vague and hard to pin down on the trunk","fix":"Use pinprick and temperature (a cold tuning fork) and test down the midline anterior and posterior; mark both and look for consistency. If any level is suspected with weakness or bladder symptoms, image urgently anyway."}],"pearls":["Dermatome maps differ between atlases and there is substantial overlap; a single-root lesion often causes only a small autonomous zone of loss (for example the middle finger for C7).","In a cord compression, the sensory level can be several segments below the actual lesion, so image above it.","Midline splitting of sensation, especially including vibration over the sternum (which crosses the midline in bone), favours a functional cause, but interpret with caution.","Cold (the side of a tuning fork) is a quick second spinothalamic test and is useful when pinprick is equivocal.","Always document perianal sensation in any patient with back pain and leg symptoms; it is a defensible, key safety finding."],"evidence":[{"stat":"Sensory level in spinal cord lesions is frequently several segments below the anatomical level of the lesion, so imaging should include the spine above the clinical level","src":"Macleod's Clinical Examination 15e"},{"stat":"Neurological pins (single-use) are recommended over needles to avoid skin puncture and transmission of blood-borne infection","src":"Macleod's Clinical Examination 15e"}],"document":"Normal: 'Pinprick and light touch intact and symmetrical in all dermatomes upper and lower limbs; no sensory level; perianal sensation intact.' Abnormal: 'Reduced pinprick both legs and trunk to a level at T10 (umbilicus) front and back; light touch reduced to T10. Perianal pinprick preserved (sacral sparing). Urgent MRI whole spine requested.'","hook":"Sharp or blunt, from numb to normal, front and back, and never forget the saddle.","mcq":[{"q":"A doctor finds reduced pinprick in both legs and tests the trunk from the nipples downward, asking when sensation becomes dull. They record a level at T12. What should they change?","opts":["Test from the abnormal area upward towards normal and test the back too","Use a needle for sharper stimulus","Test only light touch","Nothing; the method is correct"],"a":0,"why":"Boundaries are more accurate moving from abnormal to normal, and a level should be confirmed on the back. Starting from normal shifts the apparent level."},{"q":"A patient has a sensory level at T6 with preserved perianal pinprick. What does this most suggest?","opts":["Cauda equina syndrome","An intramedullary (central) cord lesion","Peripheral neuropathy","Functional disorder"],"a":1,"why":"Sacral fibres lie most laterally in the spinothalamic tract and are spared by a lesion expanding from the cord centre."},{"q":"A patient with a T8 sensory level has an MRI of the thoracic spine from T7 to T10 only, which is normal. What is the problem?","opts":["MRI is not the right test","Imaging should be lumbar","The sensory level is always exactly at the lesion","The lesion may be several segments above the sensory level; image the whole spine"],"a":3,"why":"Sensory levels often lie below the lesion; a cervical or upper thoracic lesion can produce a lower thoracic level."}],"src":["Macleod's Clinical Examination 15e","Aids to the Examination of the Peripheral Nervous System (Guarantors of Brain)","Talley & O'Connor, Clinical Examination"]},{"id":"monofilament","name":"10 g monofilament diabetic foot screening","group":"Sensory","signs":["monofilament"],"purpose":"Detects loss of protective sensation in the feet of people with diabetes, which identifies those at increased risk of foot ulceration and amputation. Used at diagnosis and at least annually in every adult with diabetes, and at any foot check.","kit":["A 10 g (Semmes-Weinstein 5.07) nylon monofilament from a reputable supplier","A spare monofilament: replace after use on many patients or when it is bent or kinked (follow the manufacturer guidance on number of uses and rest periods)","Alcohol wipe to clean the filament between patients","A foot chart to record each site"],"setup":[{"do":"Patient lying or sitting with shoes and socks off, feet relaxed and supported, in a quiet room.","why":"A relaxed, supported foot gives consistent responses; socks and noise distract and alter perception."},{"do":"Demonstrate the monofilament on the patient's hand, sternum or inner forearm so they know what it feels like, then ask them to close their eyes and say 'yes' each time they feel it and which foot.","why":"Calibrates the patient to a 10 g stimulus and avoids visual cues during testing."}],"steps":[{"do":"Inspect the plantar surfaces and choose test sites that avoid callus, scars, ulcers and necrotic tissue.","why":"Callus and scar raise sensory thresholds locally, causing false positive loss; ulcers must not be touched.","cue":"Sites are on intact skin; if a site is covered with callus, test adjacent intact skin and note it."},{"do":"Apply the monofilament perpendicular to the skin surface with steady pressure until it buckles (bends into a C shape), hold for about 1 to 2 seconds, then remove. The whole approach, contact and removal should take about 2 seconds.","why":"The filament is calibrated to deliver 10 g of force when it buckles; continuing to press does not increase the force but sliding or jabbing changes the stimulus.","cue":"The filament visibly bends; it does not slide across the skin."},{"do":"IWGDF method: test three sites on each foot, commonly the plantar pulp of the hallux and the plantar first and fifth metatarsal heads. At each site apply the filament twice and once give a sham (approach without touching), in random order, so there are three questions per site.","why":"Random sham applications check for guessing; three responses per site give a reproducible result.","cue":"You have recorded correct or incorrect for each application at each site."},{"do":"Ask, after each application or sham, 'Do you feel the pressure? Which foot?' Do not ask leading questions, and vary the timing between applications.","why":"Variable timing prevents patients from responding by rhythm rather than sensation.","cue":"Patient responds 'no' to shams."},{"do":"Score: protective sensation is present at a site if at least 2 out of 3 answers are correct, and absent if 2 out of 3 are incorrect. If any site is insensate, the foot has loss of protective sensation.","why":"The rule balances sensitivity and reproducibility and is the IWGDF standard.","cue":"You can record each foot as protective sensation present or absent."},{"do":"Combine with a 128 Hz tuning fork at the hallux, ankle reflexes, inspection, pulses and footwear check, and stratify risk per local or national guidance (for example NICE NG19).","why":"Monofilament alone does not complete the foot risk assessment; combining tests improves detection of neuropathy and guides referral and follow-up.","cue":"A risk category (low, moderate, high, active problem) is documented."},{"do":"Clean the monofilament and store it straight; replace it if bent or after the manufacturer's recommended use.","why":"Monofilaments fatigue with repeated use and buckle at lower force, giving false normal results; heat and humidity (relevant in Saudi climates) also affect the filament.","cue":"The filament is straight and stored in its sleeve."}],"normal":"The patient correctly identifies the monofilament at least 2 out of 3 times at every site on both feet and does not report the sham applications. Protective sensation present bilaterally.","abnormal":[{"finding":"Failure at one or more sites (2 of 3 incorrect)","means":"Loss of protective sensation; at least moderate risk of ulceration; refer to foot protection service per local guidance."},{"finding":"Loss of protective sensation plus absent pulses or deformity","means":"High risk of ulceration; requires specialist foot care and close follow-up."},{"finding":"Loss of sensation with an existing ulcer or callus with haemorrhage","means":"Active diabetic foot problem; urgent referral."},{"finding":"Loss only under thick callus","means":"May be a false positive; retest on adjacent intact skin and debride callus before retesting."}],"errors":[{"error":"Applying the filament over callus","result":"False positive loss of sensation","fix":"Choose intact skin; note callus and test adjacent skin."},{"error":"Sliding or brushing the filament, or not making it buckle","result":"Inconsistent force; false negatives or positives","fix":"Apply perpendicular, press until it bends, hold 1 to 2 seconds, then remove."},{"error":"Applying in a predictable rhythm without shams","result":"Patients respond by timing; false negatives","fix":"Randomise timing and include sham applications at each site."},{"error":"Using a worn or bent monofilament","result":"Lower buckling force; false normal results","fix":"Replace according to manufacturer guidance and store it straight."},{"error":"Stopping at monofilament alone","result":"Incomplete risk assessment","fix":"Add vibration, reflexes, inspection, pulses and footwear review, then stratify risk."}],"trouble":[{"problem":"The patient says yes to everything, even shams","fix":"Re-demonstrate on the hand, explain that some touches are not real, and repeat. If still unreliable, use a 128 Hz tuning fork and the Ipswich Touch Test as alternatives, and document."},{"problem":"The whole sole is calloused","fix":"Test the dorsal aspect of the toes or adjacent intact skin, record the limitation, and arrange podiatry for callus debridement before retesting."},{"problem":"I don't have a monofilament","fix":"Use the Ipswich Touch Test (light touch with the index fingertip for 1 to 2 seconds on the tips of the first, third and fifth toes) and a 128 Hz tuning fork; IWGDF accepts these as alternatives when no monofilament is available."}],"pearls":["Site protocols vary: IWGDF uses three plantar sites per foot (hallux and first and fifth metatarsal heads); some services test four or ten sites. Use your local protocol consistently and record which.","The 10 g monofilament tests protective sensation, not neuropathy in general; patients can have small-fibre neuropathy (pain, temperature) with a normal monofilament.","Monofilaments lose accuracy with repeated use; heat and humidity accelerate this, so keep a spare and a recovery rest.","In patients who cannot report reliably (for example cognitive impairment), the Ipswich Touch Test and observation of a withdrawal response are useful backups."],"evidence":[{"stat":"In diabetes, an abnormal monofilament examination is one of the most useful bedside findings for increasing the likelihood of large-fibre neuropathy, and a normal examination lowers it","src":"Kanji et al 2010, JAMA (Rational Clinical Examination)"},{"stat":"IWGDF recommends testing loss of protective sensation with a 10 g monofilament, with the Ipswich Touch Test or a 128 Hz tuning fork as alternatives","src":"IWGDF Practical Guidelines on the prevention and management of diabetic foot disease (2019, updated 2023)"},{"stat":"NICE recommends assessing the feet of adults with diabetes at diagnosis and at least annually, including testing for neuropathy with a 10 g monofilament","src":"NICE NG19, Diabetic foot problems: prevention and management"}],"document":"Normal: 'Monofilament (10 g) sensation present at 3/3 sites both feet (3/3 correct responses each site, shams negative). Vibration present at hallux bilaterally. Pulses palpable. Low risk.' Abnormal: 'Monofilament absent at L hallux and L 1st MTH (1/3 correct each), present elsewhere. Vibration absent at L hallux. Callus over L 1st MTH. Loss of protective sensation L foot: moderate risk; refer foot protection service.'","hook":"Bend it, hold two seconds, randomise with shams, and never test on callus.","mcq":[{"q":"A nurse presses the monofilament on a patient's sole repeatedly in a regular rhythm every 2 seconds, and the patient says 'yes' each time. What is the main problem?","opts":["The filament should be applied for 10 seconds","The test should be done with eyes open","Rhythmic application without shams allows the patient to respond by timing","The heel should be tested first"],"a":2,"why":"Random timing and sham applications prevent guessing; IWGDF includes one sham at each site."},{"q":"A doctor tests the monofilament over thick callus at the first metatarsal head and the patient cannot feel it. What should they do?","opts":["Record loss of protective sensation and refer urgently","Test on adjacent intact skin and arrange callus care","Use a 512 Hz tuning fork","Press harder until the patient feels it"],"a":1,"why":"Callus raises the threshold locally and causes false positive results; test intact skin nearby and address callus."},{"q":"Using the IWGDF method, a patient gives 2 correct out of 3 responses at the hallux. How is this site recorded?","opts":["Protective sensation present","Protective sensation absent","Indeterminate; repeat 10 times","Abnormal if the other foot is normal"],"a":0,"why":"IWGDF: present if at least 2 of 3 answers are correct, absent if 2 of 3 are incorrect."}],"src":["IWGDF Practical Guidelines on the prevention and management of diabetic foot disease","NICE NG19, Diabetic foot problems: prevention and management","Kanji JN et al. JAMA 2010;303:1526-1532","Macleod's Clinical Examination 15e"]},{"id":"two-point","name":"Two-point discrimination","group":"Sensory","signs":[],"purpose":"Measures innervation density in the fingertips, used mainly in hand trauma to detect a digital nerve injury after a laceration and to track recovery after nerve repair, and occasionally to grade median or ulnar neuropathy.","kit":["A two-point discriminator (Disk-Criminator or calibrated aesthesiometer), or a paper clip bent so the two ends can be set to measured distances with a ruler","A ruler in millimetres","Good light, and the patient's hand resting palm-up on a firm surface"],"setup":[{"do":"Rest the patient's hand palm-up on a firm surface, fingers supported and still, ideally with the dorsum resting on putty or a folded towel.","why":"Finger movement adds proprioceptive and movement cues that invalidate static testing; a supported hand lets the patient relax."},{"do":"Demonstrate on an unaffected digit with eyes open, touching with one point and then two points well apart (for example 10 mm), then ask the patient to close their eyes and answer 'one' or 'two'.","why":"The patient must know what each sensation feels like; testing an unaffected digit first gives you a personal normal value."}],"steps":[{"do":"Static 2PD: start with the points about 10 mm apart. Apply the instrument to the pulp of the fingertip, with both points placed simultaneously along the long axis of the finger, with just enough pressure to blanch the skin, for 2 to 3 seconds.","why":"Placing the points longitudinally keeps both within the territory of one digital nerve (radial or ulnar side of the pulp); transverse placement would cross the midline and stimulate both nerves. Pressure just to blanch prevents deep pressure receptors cueing the patient.","cue":"The skin blanches slightly at both points; they touch at the same moment."},{"do":"Randomly alternate single-point and two-point applications, recording the response to each.","why":"Random interleaving with single points detects guessing.","cue":"The patient correctly reports 'one' for single-point touches."},{"do":"Reduce the distance in 1 mm steps until the patient can no longer reliably distinguish two points. Use a criterion such as 7 of 10 correct responses at a distance.","why":"A defined criterion (commonly 7 of 10) gives a reproducible threshold rather than a single lucky answer.","cue":"You can state the smallest distance at which the patient is reliably correct."},{"do":"Test the radial and ulnar sides of each affected digit pulp separately, and compare with the same side of the uninjured digit.","why":"Each side of the digit is supplied by a separate proper digital nerve; a laceration may cut only one.","cue":"A side-specific threshold for each digital nerve."},{"do":"Moving 2PD (Dellon): move the instrument from proximal to distal along the pulp with the points parallel to the long axis, with light pressure, alternating one and two points randomly.","why":"Moving 2PD tests rapidly adapting receptors (Meissner corpuscles) and usually recovers earlier than static 2PD after nerve repair; normal thresholds are lower than for static.","cue":"A moving 2PD threshold for each tested side."},{"do":"Record the threshold in millimetres for static and moving 2PD and compare with norms and the contralateral digit.","why":"Normal fingertip static 2PD is usually under 6 mm; moving 2PD is typically 2 to 3 mm; comparison with the contralateral side accounts for individual variation (for example callused manual workers).","cue":"A documented threshold per digit side, both static and moving."}],"normal":"Fingertip static 2PD under about 6 mm (often 3 to 5 mm) and moving 2PD about 2 to 3 mm, symmetrical with the corresponding digit on the other hand. Manual workers with callused fingertips may have slightly higher thresholds.","abnormal":[{"finding":"Static 2PD greater than 10 to 15 mm on one side of a digit after a laceration","means":"Likely divided or severely injured digital nerve; refer for exploration and repair."},{"finding":"Static 2PD 6 to 10 mm","means":"Fair or reduced discrimination; partial nerve injury, compression neuropathy or early recovery."},{"finding":"Unable to distinguish two points at any distance","means":"Protective sensation only or anaesthesia in that nerve's territory."},{"finding":"Moving 2PD recovering before static 2PD after repair","means":"Expected pattern of recovery; reinnervation of rapidly adapting receptors first."}],"errors":[{"error":"Placing the points transversely across the pulp","result":"Both digital nerves are stimulated, giving a false normal result in a single digital nerve injury","fix":"Place the points along the long axis, on the radial or ulnar half of the pulp."},{"error":"Pressing too hard","result":"Deep pressure receptors cue the patient; false normal results","fix":"Apply just enough pressure to blanch the skin."},{"error":"Applying the two points one after the other","result":"The patient detects two touches in time rather than space","fix":"Ensure both points touch simultaneously."},{"error":"Testing without random single-point trials","result":"Guessing goes undetected","fix":"Interleave single-point applications randomly."},{"error":"Testing an injured digit in a distressed or anaesthetised patient","result":"Unreliable answers; an injured nerve may be missed or over-called","fix":"Test before local anaesthetic, and supplement with other signs (sweating, pulp wrinkling in water, sharp-blunt)."}],"trouble":[{"problem":"A child or a distressed patient can't cooperate","fix":"Use objective signs of digital nerve injury: absence of sweating on the pulp (dry, smooth skin), absent skin wrinkling after 5 minutes in warm water, and a laceration in the line of the nerve. Low threshold for exploration."},{"problem":"I don't have a discriminator","fix":"Bend a paper clip into a U so the ends are level, set the distance with a ruler, and check it is unchanged between applications."},{"problem":"The values are borderline in a callused manual worker","fix":"Compare with the same side of the corresponding digit on the uninjured hand; a clear asymmetry is more meaningful than the absolute value."}],"pearls":["Two-point discrimination tests innervation density in the pulp and is one of the most functionally relevant sensory tests in the hand, because it correlates with fine manipulation.","Two-point discrimination is often normal in early compression neuropathy (such as carpal tunnel syndrome) because it requires substantial axonal loss before it changes; threshold tests (monofilaments, vibration) detect earlier change.","Thresholds vary between sources: ASSH grades static 2PD under 6 mm as normal, 6 to 10 mm fair, 11 to 15 mm poor, and only protective sensation beyond that.","In any finger laceration, test sensation before local anaesthetic is given; the result guides exploration."],"evidence":[{"stat":"Static 2PD categories: normal under 6 mm, fair 6 to 10 mm, poor 11 to 15 mm, protective only beyond 15 mm","src":"American Society for Surgery of the Hand, Clinical Assessment Recommendations"},{"stat":"Two-point discrimination is insensitive for carpal tunnel syndrome because it changes late, after significant axonal loss","src":"D'Arcy & McGee 2000, JAMA (Rational Clinical Examination)"}],"document":"Normal: 'Static 2PD 4 mm radial and ulnar sides of all digit pulps bilaterally; moving 2PD 3 mm.' Abnormal: 'L index finger laceration over radial side of middle phalanx: static 2PD >15 mm radial side, 4 mm ulnar side; R index 4 mm both sides. Suspected L radial digital nerve injury; refer for exploration.'","hook":"Along the finger, to blanching, both points together, randomised with one.","mcq":[{"q":"After a laceration over the radial side of the index finger, a doctor tests 2PD with the points placed transversely across the pulp and gets 4 mm. What is the error?","opts":["The points should be placed along the long axis on each side of the pulp","Static 2PD should be replaced by moving 2PD","The fingertip should be tested only after local anaesthetic","Nothing; the result is normal"],"a":0,"why":"Transverse placement stimulates both digital nerves, so an intact ulnar digital nerve can give a normal response despite a divided radial digital nerve."},{"q":"A doctor presses the two-point discriminator firmly until the patient winces. The patient correctly identifies two points at 3 mm. What should they do?","opts":["Record normal","Repeat with the moving method only","Repeat with pressure just to blanch the skin","Test the dorsum of the hand instead"],"a":2,"why":"Excess pressure recruits deep pressure receptors and gives false normal results; use pressure just to blanch."},{"q":"A patient with clear carpal tunnel symptoms has normal 2PD. What is the best interpretation?","opts":["Carpal tunnel syndrome is excluded","2PD is insensitive in early compression neuropathy; it changes late","The patient has an ulnar neuropathy","The test was done incorrectly"],"a":1,"why":"2PD requires significant axonal loss before it becomes abnormal, so a normal result does not exclude CTS."}],"src":["American Society for Surgery of the Hand, Clinical Assessment Recommendations","D'Arcy CA, McGee S. JAMA 2000;283:3110-3117","Macleod's Clinical Examination 15e"]},{"id":"tendon-reflexes","name":"Deep tendon reflexes","group":"Reflexes and tone","signs":["reflexes"],"purpose":"Tests the integrity of a monosynaptic stretch arc at a known root level and whether descending (upper motor neurone) inhibition is intact. Reach for it whenever weakness, numbness, radicular pain, a possible cord lesion or neuropathy is on the table.","kit":["Long-handled reflex hammer with a weighted head (Queen Square or Tromner style); a heavier head gives a more consistent stimulus than a light tomahawk","Exposed limbs: sleeves above the elbow, legs exposed from mid-thigh to the feet","Bed or couch you can reach from both sides; a chair high enough for the feet to hang free if testing seated"],"setup":[{"do":"Patient lying supine with the head on one pillow, or sitting with arms resting in the lap; limbs fully supported and relaxed. Stand on the patient's right and compare each reflex side to side immediately.","why":"Any active muscle contraction preloads the muscle spindle and alters the response, and a muscle the patient is holding up against gravity cannot relax. Immediate side to side comparison is what makes asymmetry, the most useful finding, detectable."},{"do":"Position each joint so the tested muscle is slightly stretched, roughly at mid-range: elbow at about 90 degrees for biceps and supinator, elbow at about 90 degrees with the forearm across the trunk for triceps, knee at about 60 to 90 degrees for the knee jerk, ankle passively dorsiflexed for the ankle jerk.","why":"Spindles fire best when the muscle is under slight stretch; a fully shortened or fully stretched muscle gives a smaller response. Mid-range positioning also lets you see and feel the contraction rather than only joint movement."},{"do":"Explain briefly ('I'm going to tap your tendons, just let your arm go floppy') and chat during testing.","why":"Anxiety increases muscle tone and reflex briskness, while attention on the limb makes patients brace. Conversation is a simple distraction that improves relaxation."}],"steps":[{"do":"Hold the hammer loosely near the end of the handle between thumb and index finger, other fingers guiding it. Let the head fall in an arc by flicking from the wrist, not driving from the shoulder or elbow, so the head swings through and its weight does the work.","why":"A pendulum swing delivers a brief, reproducible, high-velocity stretch, which is the adequate stimulus for the spindle. Pushing with the arm gives a slow, variable push that partly fails to excite the reflex and is less comparable side to side.","cue":"The hammer bounces off the tendon rather than sticking to it, and the strike force feels the same on each side."},{"do":"Biceps (C5, C6): with the elbow flexed to about 90 degrees and the forearm resting on the abdomen, slightly pronated, place your left thumb (supine) or index finger (seated) firmly on the biceps tendon in the antecubital fossa and strike your own finger.","why":"The biceps tendon is small and moves under the skin; your finger localises it, spreads the stimulus over the tendon, and lets you feel a contraction that may be too small to see.","cue":"You feel the tendon tighten under your finger and see the biceps belly contract, with or without elbow flexion."},{"do":"Supinator, brachioradialis (C5, C6): same arm position, forearm semi-pronated resting on the abdomen. Place two fingers on the radius about 5 to 8 cm (two to three fingerbreadths) above the radial styloid and strike them, or strike the bone directly.","why":"The brachioradialis inserts at the distal radius; a tap here stretches it. Striking through your fingers avoids a painful blow on the radius in thin patients.","cue":"Contraction of brachioradialis at the lateral forearm with elbow flexion; watch the fingers at the same time for the inverted response."},{"do":"Triceps (C7, with C6): supine, lift the arm and lay the forearm across the chest with the elbow at about 90 degrees, supporting the wrist with your left hand; or seated, hold the upper arm abducted so the forearm hangs at 90 degrees. Strike the triceps tendon 2 to 3 cm above the olecranon directly.","why":"The triceps tendon is broad and easy to hit directly. Supporting the limb removes any need for the patient to hold the arm, so the triceps is relaxed.","cue":"Contraction of triceps with a small extension of the elbow."},{"do":"Knee (L3, L4): supine, slide your left forearm under both knees and lift them to about 60 to 90 degrees of flexion, heels resting on the bed; strike the patellar tendon midway between patella and tibial tuberosity. Seated, the legs hang freely over the edge.","why":"Supporting both knees on one forearm lets you test and compare quickly with identical positioning, and the quadriceps does not need to hold the leg.","cue":"Visible quadriceps contraction (watch the muscle, not just the foot) with knee extension."},{"do":"Ankle (S1, S2): supine, flex the hip and knee, externally rotate the thigh so the lateral side of the leg rests on the bed, and dorsiflex the foot gently with your left hand on the sole. Strike the Achilles tendon directly. Alternatives: patient kneeling on a chair with feet hanging over the edge, or the plantar strike (hit your own fingers placed on the ball of the foot while dorsiflexing).","why":"Dorsiflexion pre-stretches gastrocnemius-soleus; the hand on the sole feels plantar flexion that is too small to see. The kneeling position fully relaxes the calf and is the best way to confirm a reflex you think is absent.","cue":"You feel the foot push into your hand and see the calf contract."},{"do":"If a reflex seems absent, repeat with reinforcement (Jendrassik): for leg reflexes ask the patient to hook the fingers of both hands together and pull hard; for arm reflexes ask them to clench the teeth or make a fist with the other hand. Say 'pull' and strike within the next second while they are pulling.","why":"Remote voluntary contraction increases excitability of the spinal motor neurone pool (mechanism debated: increased fusimotor drive and reduced presynaptic inhibition). The facilitation is maximal shortly after the contraction starts and fades within seconds, so timing matters more than strength of pull.","cue":"A reflex appearing only with reinforcement is graded 1 (trace), not 0."},{"do":"Grade each reflex 0 to 4 (the everyday clinical scale; the formal NINDS scale words grades 1 to 3 differently): 0 absent; 1 reduced or present only with reinforcement; 2 normal; 3 brisk, more than normal, possibly with spread to other muscles; 4 very brisk with clonus. Then look specifically for the inverted supinator, a pendular knee and a slow-relaxing ankle jerk.","why":"Grading is subjective; the useful clinical information is asymmetry, a reflex out of keeping with the others (for example brisk arms with absent ankles), and the special patterns, which point to a lesion level or a systemic cause.","cue":"You can state each reflex as a grade and say whether it is symmetric."}],"normal":"Present (grade 1 to 2) and symmetric at all five sites, with a single brief contraction that relaxes promptly. Absent ankle jerks bilaterally can be normal in older adults if reinforcement fails, but should still be interpreted with the rest of the examination.","abnormal":[{"finding":"Brisk reflexes (3 to 4) with spread, clonus, extensor plantars and increased tone","means":"Upper motor neurone lesion; the level is at or above the highest brisk reflex"},{"finding":"Absent or reduced reflex in one root distribution (for example a lost ankle jerk with S1 pain)","means":"Radiculopathy or focal neuropathy at that root or nerve"},{"finding":"Symmetrically reduced or absent reflexes, distal first (ankles then knees)","means":"Length-dependent peripheral neuropathy; diffuse areflexia suggests Guillain-Barre syndrome or a demyelinating neuropathy"},{"finding":"Inverted supinator: tapping brachioradialis produces finger flexion with little or no brachioradialis contraction, often with a brisk triceps","means":"Lesion at C5-C6: lower motor neurone loss of the C5-C6 arc plus cord compression causing hyperreflexia below (C8 finger flexors), typically cervical spondylotic myelopathy"},{"finding":"Pendular knee jerk: the leg swings back and forth several times after the tap","means":"Ipsilateral cerebellar hemisphere lesion (hypotonia with loss of damping)"},{"finding":"Slow-relaxing ankle jerk: normal contraction but visibly delayed relaxation phase (Woltman sign)","means":"Hypothyroidism; occasionally hypothermia or other causes of slowed muscle relaxation"}],"errors":[{"error":"Gripping the hammer near the head and pushing it onto the tendon","result":"A slow, weak, inconsistent stimulus that produces false 'absent' reflexes and unequal side to side force","fix":"Hold at the end of the handle, loosely, and let the head fall from the wrist so its weight delivers the strike"},{"error":"Testing a limb the patient is holding up or tensing","result":"Preloaded or co-contracting muscles give reduced or unpredictable responses","fix":"Support the limb fully with your hand or the bed, and distract the patient with conversation before striking"},{"error":"Calling a reflex absent without reinforcement or an alternative position","result":"False areflexia, especially at the ankle, leading to unnecessary neuropathy work-up","fix":"Repeat with Jendrassik timed just before the tap, then try the kneeling position or plantar strike for the ankle"},{"error":"Judging only by joint movement","result":"Small contractions are missed when the joint barely moves","fix":"Watch and feel the muscle belly or tendon under your finger; grade the contraction, not the swing"},{"error":"Testing all reflexes on one side then the other","result":"Subtle asymmetry is lost because comparison is from memory","fix":"Test each reflex right then left immediately, in identical position with identical force"},{"error":"Interpreting brisk but symmetric reflexes in an anxious young patient as pathological","result":"False upper motor neurone diagnosis","fix":"Look for the companions of a true UMN lesion: sustained clonus, extensor plantar, increased tone, weakness in a pyramidal pattern, asymmetry"}],"trouble":[{"problem":"I can't get the ankle jerks at all","fix":"Check the patient is relaxed and the foot is dorsiflexed slightly by your hand. Try reinforcement, saying 'pull' just as you swing. Then kneel the patient on a chair with feet over the edge and strike again. If still absent, try the plantar strike. Only then record absent."},{"problem":"I can't feel the biceps tendon","fix":"Ask the patient to flex the elbow briefly against your hand so the tendon stands out in the antecubital fossa, place your thumb on it, then ask them to relax and strike your thumb."},{"problem":"Every reflex looks brisk and I don't know if it means anything","fix":"Look for asymmetry, spread (for example adductor contraction with the knee jerk), sustained ankle clonus and the plantar response. Symmetric brisk reflexes with no other UMN signs are often normal; a jaw jerk that is also brisk places any true UMN process above the pons."}],"pearls":["Grading conventions vary: the NINDS scale runs 0 to 4, while many UK notes use 0, plus-minus (with reinforcement), +, ++, +++. Asymmetry matters more than the absolute grade in any system.","Remember the roots in order: ankle S1-2, knee L3-4, biceps and supinator C5-6, triceps C7-8 (the 1-2, 3-4, 5-6, 7-8 rhyme). The finger jerk tests C8.","The highest brisk reflex helps localise a cord lesion: a normal biceps with a brisk triceps and finger jerks points below C6.","Reinforcement is a timing trick, not a strength trick: tap within a second of the pull starting.","An inverted supinator is a level sign; finding one should prompt you to look carefully for long-tract signs in the legs."],"evidence":[{"stat":"Inter-observer agreement for reflex grading is only moderate, and better for detecting asymmetry and absent reflexes than for exact grades","src":"McGee, Evidence-Based Physical Diagnosis 5e"},{"stat":"Ankle reflexes are reduced or absent more often with increasing age, so bilateral absence in the elderly is weak evidence of neuropathy on its own","src":"McGee, Evidence-Based Physical Diagnosis 5e"}],"document":"Normal: 'Reflexes: biceps, supinator, triceps, knee and ankle 2 and symmetric; plantars flexor.' Abnormal: 'Reflexes: biceps and supinator 1 bilaterally with inverted supinator on the left; triceps, knee 3, ankle 3 with 3 beats of unsustained clonus bilaterally; plantars extensor bilaterally. Pattern suggests C5-6 myelopathy.'","hook":"Let the hammer fall, compare instantly, and reinforce before you call it absent.","mcq":[{"q":"A colleague cannot elicit the ankle jerks in a 50-year-old with diabetes. He asks the patient to clench the teeth, waits until the patient has relaxed, and taps again. Still nothing. What is the main flaw?","opts":["Teeth clenching cannot reinforce leg reflexes","Reinforcement is useful only for arm reflexes","The tap must come while the patient is actively pulling or clenching, within a second of starting","Reinforcement should be held for 30 seconds before tapping"],"a":2,"why":"The facilitation from a remote contraction is brief; tapping after the patient relaxes loses it. Hooked fingers pulling is the classic leg reinforcement, but any remote contraction works if timed correctly."},{"q":"Tapping a patient's brachioradialis produces finger flexion but no elbow flexion. Triceps and knee jerks are brisk. What is the most likely site of the lesion?","opts":["Cervical cord at C5-C6","Radial nerve at the spiral groove","Left cerebral hemisphere","Lumbar canal stenosis"],"a":0,"why":"This is the inverted supinator: the C5-6 arc is interrupted (lower motor neurone at that level) and the segments below are hyperreflexic from cord compression, classically cervical spondylotic myelopathy."},{"q":"A doctor tests the knee jerk with a light tomahawk hammer held close to the head, pressing it onto the tendon. The reflex is graded absent on the right and 1 on the left. What should he do first?","opts":["Order nerve conduction studies","Record a right L3-4 radiculopathy","Test the ankle jerk instead","Repeat with the hammer held at the end of the handle and swung from the wrist, with knees supported and reinforcement"],"a":3,"why":"A pushed, slow stimulus is an inadequate stretch and gives false asymmetry. Correct technique and reinforcement must come before interpreting an absent reflex."}],"src":["Macleod's Clinical Examination 15e","McGee, Evidence-Based Physical Diagnosis 5e","Hallett M 1993, Neurology (NINDS myotatic reflex scale)","Talley and O'Connor, Clinical Examination"]},{"id":"plantar-response","name":"Plantar response (Babinski sign)","group":"Reflexes and tone","signs":["babinski"],"purpose":"Detects corticospinal tract dysfunction by showing whether the primitive extensor withdrawal of the great toe has been released. Use it whenever an upper motor neurone lesion is possible: weakness, brisk reflexes, possible stroke, cord compression or reduced consciousness.","kit":["A blunt but firm object: orange stick, the handle end of a reflex hammer, or a key. Not a needle, not a sharp point, not your fingernail on a tender sole","Feet and lower legs exposed, socks off"],"setup":[{"do":"Patient supine with legs straight and relaxed, feet uncovered and warm if possible. Stand at the side of the foot being tested, at the level of the shin.","why":"A tense or flexed leg alters the response and a cold or anxious patient withdraws more. Standing beside rather than at the end of the bed lets you watch the hallux at eye level while holding the ankle."},{"do":"Hold the ankle gently with your other hand and warn the patient: 'I'm going to stroke the sole of your foot; it may tickle.'","why":"Steadying the foot reduces gross withdrawal of the whole limb; a warning reduces a startle that makes interpretation impossible."}],"steps":[{"do":"Start at the lateral border of the sole near the heel and draw the object forward along the lateral edge toward the base of the little toe, using a slow, steady stroke taking about 1 to 2 seconds.","why":"The receptive field for the reflex is the S1 dermatome along the lateral sole. A slow firm stroke is an adequate stimulus without being noxious; a fast or sharp stroke triggers a nociceptive withdrawal that can mimic an extensor response.","cue":"The patient feels pressure and mild discomfort, not pain; the skin blanches along the line but is not scratched."},{"do":"At the metatarsal heads, curve medially across the ball of the foot, stopping before the base of the hallux (the medial ball).","why":"Stimulating the ball of the great toe directly can elicit a plantar grasp (toe flexion) reflex that masks an extensor response; stopping short of it keeps the stimulus in the reflexogenic zone.","cue":"The stroke ends around the second or third metatarsal head."},{"do":"Watch the hallux, specifically its first movement, at the metatarsophalangeal joint. Ignore later movements.","why":"In a normal response the first movement is flexion; in a true Babinski the first movement is slow, tonic extension through contraction of extensor hallucis longus. Later movements are often voluntary or withdrawal and confuse the picture.","cue":"You can say whether the toe went down or up first."},{"do":"If the toe extends, look at the dorsum of the foot for the EHL tendon tightening and at the thigh for hamstring or tensor fasciae latae contraction.","why":"True extensor responses are part of a flexion synergy (toe extension, ankle dorsiflexion, knee and hip flexion) driven by the same spinal reflex. Visible EHL contraction confirms the toe is pulled up by the extensor, not passively displaced.","cue":"The EHL tendon stands out; the movement is slow and sustained rather than a quick flick."},{"do":"Distinguish withdrawal: a rapid, brisk movement of the whole leg, often with ankle dorsiflexion and toe movement, that the patient may be able to suppress. Repeat more gently or use an alternative site.","why":"Withdrawal is a protective response to a tickly or painful stimulus; it is fast and variable, while the Babinski response is slower, stereotyped and reproducible with gentle stimuli.","cue":"A true extensor response reappears consistently with a gentler stroke; withdrawal fades when the stimulus is reduced or the patient is reassured."},{"do":"If equivocal or the patient withdraws, use the Chaddock: stroke from behind the lateral malleolus forward along the lateral dorsum of the foot toward the little toe.","why":"The Chaddock stimulates the same S1 receptive field on the non-ticklish lateral foot, so it produces less withdrawal while eliciting the same reflex.","cue":"Same interpretation: a slow upgoing hallux as the first movement is extensor."},{"do":"Compare both sides and record each as flexor, extensor or equivocal (and if equivocal, say why).","why":"A unilateral extensor plantar in the context of other ipsilateral UMN signs is highly meaningful; bilateral equivocal responses are often uninterpretable and are better recorded honestly than forced.","cue":"Each side has a clear label."}],"normal":"In adults and children over about 1 to 2 years, the great toe flexes at the MTP joint as the first movement (flexor plantar). An absent response (no movement of the hallux) can occur, for example with sensory loss or a paralysed EHL, and is not the same as a flexor response.","abnormal":[{"finding":"Slow, tonic extension of the hallux as the first movement with visible EHL contraction, sometimes with fanning of the other toes and flexion at hip and knee","means":"Extensor plantar (Babinski sign): corticospinal tract dysfunction anywhere from cortex to cord"},{"finding":"Unilateral extensor plantar with ipsilateral hyperreflexia and pyramidal weakness","means":"Contralateral hemisphere or brainstem lesion, or ipsilateral cord lesion"},{"finding":"Bilateral extensor plantars","means":"Bilateral UMN involvement: cord compression, diffuse brain disease, post-ictal state, deep coma, hypoglycaemia or metabolic encephalopathy"},{"finding":"No movement of the hallux","means":"Mute response: possible severe sensory loss, peripheral neuropathy, or paralysis of the toe muscles (for example L5 lesion affecting EHL); it is not interpretable as flexor"},{"finding":"Extensor response in an infant under about 12 months","means":"Normal developmental finding before corticospinal myelination"}],"errors":[{"error":"Using a sharp object or a fast scratch","result":"Painful stimulus causes withdrawal, read as a false extensor response, and may break the skin","fix":"Use a blunt orange stick or key handle with slow, firm, steady pressure"},{"error":"Running the stroke up the middle of the sole or across the ball of the big toe","result":"Triggers the plantar grasp reflex, causing toe flexion that masks a true extensor response (false flexor)","fix":"Stay on the lateral border, curve across the metatarsal heads and stop before the hallux base"},{"error":"Watching the toes after the stroke ends or reading the final position","result":"Later voluntary or withdrawal movements are misread","fix":"Watch the first movement of the hallux only, from the start of the stroke"},{"error":"Calling toe fanning alone a Babinski","result":"False positive; fanning occurs normally and without hallux extension","fix":"Base the call on the hallux and EHL contraction; fanning is a supporting, optional feature"},{"error":"Recording 'mute' as 'flexor'","result":"Masks a possible UMN sign hidden by sensory loss or EHL weakness","fix":"Record mute separately and use Chaddock or other signs; check EHL power"}],"trouble":[{"problem":"The patient withdraws every time I touch the sole","fix":"Warm the foot, reassure, hold the ankle, and use a lighter but slower stroke. Then try the Chaddock on the lateral dorsum. If still uninterpretable, record 'equivocal due to withdrawal' and rely on reflexes, tone and power."},{"problem":"The toe goes up then down, or down then up","fix":"Take the first movement. If you genuinely cannot see the first movement, repeat once or twice with identical technique, watching only the hallux from the start; inconsistent results over three tries should be recorded as equivocal."},{"problem":"The toe doesn't move at all","fix":"Check sensation on the sole and EHL power. If EHL is paralysed the sign cannot be shown; look for other UMN signs. If sensation is absent, try the Chaddock or Oppenheim (pressing firmly down the anterior tibia)."}],"pearls":["The sign is the hallux going up through EHL contraction; Babinski himself described fanning separately and it is not required.","A true extensor response is a fragment of the flexion withdrawal synergy, so look at the hamstrings as well: they tighten with a genuine Babinski.","Conventions on the exact stroke path vary slightly between textbooks (some stop at the little toe base, others curve across), but all agree: lateral sole, slow, blunt, avoid the hallux ball.","In the drowsy post-ictal or hypoglycaemic patient, bilateral extensor plantars can be transient; recheck after correction."],"evidence":[{"stat":"Inter-observer agreement for the Babinski sign is only fair to moderate, and in one study it was less reliable than foot-tapping or weakness of toe extension for detecting UMN lesions","src":"Miller and Johnston 2005, Neurology"},{"stat":"Interpretation relies on the first hallux movement and visible EHL contraction; withdrawal is the main source of false positives","src":"van Gijn, The Babinski Sign: A Centenary (1996)"}],"document":"Normal: 'Plantars flexor bilaterally.' Abnormal: 'Right plantar extensor (slow upgoing hallux with EHL contraction, reproducible, confirmed on Chaddock); left flexor.' Equivocal: 'Left plantar equivocal due to withdrawal, Chaddock flexor.'","hook":"Blunt, slow, lateral, stop short of the big toe, and watch only the first move.","mcq":[{"q":"A junior doctor strokes the middle of the sole with a key, ending at the base of the big toe. The toes flex every time in a patient with a dense left hemiparesis. What is the likely explanation?","opts":["The patient has a peripheral nerve lesion","Stimulation over the hallux ball elicited a plantar grasp that masked an extensor response","Hemiparesis never produces a Babinski sign","The key was too blunt"],"a":1,"why":"The reflexogenic zone is the lateral sole. Stimulating the ball of the great toe provokes toe flexion (grasp), giving a false flexor response."},{"q":"With a firm scratch using a needle, a patient's whole leg jerks away rapidly, the ankle dorsiflexes and the toe briefly lifts. What should be done?","opts":["Record an extensor plantar","Record a flexor plantar","Ignore the plantar response in this patient","Repeat with a blunt object and slow stroke, or use the Chaddock, and read only the first hallux movement"],"a":3,"why":"A noxious stimulus triggers withdrawal. A blunt slow stimulus or the less ticklish Chaddock site separates withdrawal from a true extensor response."},{"q":"A patient with an L5 radiculopathy has no hallux movement to plantar stimulation on the affected side. How should this be recorded?","opts":["Mute response, uninterpretable; check EHL power and other UMN signs","Flexor plantar","Extensor plantar","Normal"],"a":0,"why":"EHL is L5-innervated; if it is weak the toe cannot extend. A mute response is not flexor and should be recorded separately."}],"src":["van Gijn J, The Babinski Sign: A Centenary (1996)","Macleod's Clinical Examination 15e","Miller TM, Johnston SC 2005, Neurology","McGee, Evidence-Based Physical Diagnosis 5e"]},{"id":"hoffmann","name":"Hoffmann's sign","group":"Reflexes and tone","signs":["hoffmann"],"purpose":"Shows hyperreflexia of the finger flexors (C8 arc), an upper limb marker of upper motor neurone dysfunction, most often used to look for cervical myelopathy. Reach for it in neck pain with hand clumsiness, gait change, brisk reflexes or when the plantars are uninterpretable.","kit":["No instruments needed","Patient's hand and forearm exposed and relaxed"],"setup":[{"do":"Patient seated or supine with the arm relaxed, forearm pronated and supported by you; wrist in slight dorsiflexion, fingers partly flexed and loose.","why":"Slight wrist extension puts the long finger flexors under mild stretch, which increases spindle sensitivity, and the limb must be passive for the reflex to be seen."},{"do":"Test both hands and compare, asking the patient to look away and let the hand go limp.","why":"A symmetric weak Hoffmann can be normal; asymmetry is more meaningful. Voluntary tension of the finger muscles suppresses or mimics the response."}],"steps":[{"do":"Support the patient's hand with your left hand under the palm, and hold the patient's middle finger at the middle phalanx between your right index and middle fingers (or thumb and index), so the distal phalanx projects free.","why":"Holding the middle phalanx immobilises the finger proximally so that only the distal phalanx is flicked, producing a sudden stretch of flexor digitorum profundus.","cue":"The patient's distal phalanx hangs loosely beyond your grip."},{"do":"With your right thumb, press down on the patient's nail and then flick off it sharply, so the distal phalanx is briskly flexed and suddenly released (some describe flicking the nail downward; both describe the same snap).","why":"The sudden release produces a quick stretch of the long finger flexors, which is the adequate stimulus. A slow push does not stretch fast enough to elicit the reflex.","cue":"A clean snapping movement of your thumb off the nail edge."},{"do":"Watch the thumb and index finger of the same hand during the flick.","why":"A positive response is reflex contraction of the stretched finger flexors spreading to the thumb (flexor pollicis longus) and index, seen as flexion and adduction of the thumb and flexion of the index finger.","cue":"You can say whether the thumb flexed and adducted or stayed still."},{"do":"Repeat three to five times on each side.","why":"The response may be small and variable; reproducibility guards against misreading a voluntary twitch.","cue":"A positive sign recurs with each flick."},{"do":"If negative but myelopathy is suspected, consider the dynamic Hoffmann: ask the patient to flex and extend the neck repeatedly, then test again in neck flexion. Alternatively perform the Tromner: hold the middle finger and tap the pulp of the distal phalanx upward with your finger.","why":"Neck movement in a narrowed canal may transiently increase cord compromise and unmask the sign. The Tromner gives the same stretch stimulus from the opposite direction.","cue":"Same reading: thumb and index flexion."}],"normal":"No movement, or a tiny symmetric flicker, of the thumb and index finger when the middle finger nail is flicked.","abnormal":[{"finding":"Clear thumb flexion-adduction and index flexion, reproducible, on one side","means":"Unilateral UMN lesion above C8: cervical cord compression on that side, or contralateral cerebral lesion"},{"finding":"Bilateral brisk Hoffmann with brisk reflexes, hand clumsiness, gait disturbance","means":"Cervical myelopathy (degenerative, disc, tumour), or other bilateral UMN process (for example motor neurone disease, multiple sclerosis)"},{"finding":"Bilateral mild symmetric Hoffmann in a young, anxious patient with brisk reflexes and no other signs","means":"Often a normal variant of physiological hyperreflexia; correlate clinically"},{"finding":"Positive Hoffmann with inverted supinator","means":"Lesion localised at C5-C6 level with cord compression"}],"errors":[{"error":"Pushing the nail slowly instead of flicking","result":"No quick stretch, false negative","fix":"Press then snap your thumb off the nail edge so the release is sudden"},{"error":"Holding the finger at the distal phalanx","result":"The flicked segment cannot move freely, false negative","fix":"Hold the middle phalanx so the distal phalanx projects free"},{"error":"Calling any symmetric small twitch a positive sign","result":"Over-diagnosis of myelopathy in anxious hyperreflexic people","fix":"Look for asymmetry, reproducibility and other UMN signs before attaching significance"},{"error":"Patient's hand tense or actively holding position","result":"Voluntary tension suppresses the reflex or causes a twitch mistaken for it","fix":"Support the hand fully, distract the patient, and check the fingers are loose before flicking"}],"trouble":[{"problem":"I never see anything, even in patients with brisk reflexes","fix":"Check the wrist is slightly extended and the hand supported, hold the middle phalanx, and make the flick genuinely sharp. Watch the thumb tip from the side at eye level. Try the Tromner as an alternative."},{"problem":"I see a positive sign and I'm not sure if it matters","fix":"Compare sides, test reflexes in arms and legs, look for an inverted supinator, check tone, plantars, gait and tandem walking, and ask about hand clumsiness and neck symptoms. Unilateral or with any other UMN finding, it needs imaging."}],"pearls":["Hoffmann's sign is a stretch reflex, not a pathological reflex in the Babinski sense; it shows hyperreflexia, so it can be present in healthy hyperreflexic people.","It is the upper limb equivalent of a brisk reflex at C8, so its presence places a UMN lesion above C8.","Asymmetry and context matter far more than presence or absence.","A negative Hoffmann does not exclude cervical myelopathy; many patients with cord compression have a negative sign."],"evidence":[{"stat":"A positive Hoffmann's is found in a small proportion of asymptomatic people, and a substantial number of those have cervical cord or root compression on imaging","src":"Sung and Wang 2001, Spine"},{"stat":"Sensitivity for cervical myelopathy is moderate at best, so a negative sign does not rule out myelopathy","src":"McGee, Evidence-Based Physical Diagnosis 5e"}],"document":"Normal: 'Hoffmann negative bilaterally.' Abnormal: 'Hoffmann positive on the right (reproducible thumb and index flexion), negative on left; brisk right triceps and finger jerk; right plantar extensor.'","hook":"Flick the middle finger free and watch the thumb tell you about the cord.","mcq":[{"q":"A doctor tests Hoffmann's sign by holding the patient's middle finger at the distal phalanx and slowly pressing down on the nail. No movement is seen. What is wrong?","opts":["The sign must be done with a hammer","The middle phalanx should be held so the distal phalanx is free, and the nail should be flicked off sharply","The index finger should be stimulated instead","Nothing; the test is negative"],"a":1,"why":"The reflex needs a sudden stretch of the finger flexors; holding the distal phalanx and pressing slowly gives no quick stretch."},{"q":"A 25-year-old anxious medical student has brisk symmetric reflexes and a very mild symmetric Hoffmann on both sides, with normal tone, power, plantars and gait. What is the best interpretation?","opts":["Urgent MRI cervical spine","Probable multiple sclerosis","Likely physiological hyperreflexia; no further action unless other signs or symptoms","Motor neurone disease"],"a":2,"why":"Hoffmann's is a stretch reflex and can be present symmetrically in healthy hyperreflexic people; isolated and symmetric with no other UMN signs, it is usually normal."},{"q":"What does a positive Hoffmann's sign primarily indicate?","opts":["Hyperreflexia of the finger flexors, pointing to a UMN lesion above C8","Median nerve compression at the wrist","Lower motor neurone lesion at C8","Cerebellar dysfunction"],"a":0,"why":"The sign shows exaggerated finger flexor stretch reflexes, a marker of upper motor neurone dysfunction above the C8 segment."}],"src":["Macleod's Clinical Examination 15e","McGee, Evidence-Based Physical Diagnosis 5e","Sung RD, Wang JC 2001, Spine"]},{"id":"clonus-tone","name":"Tone and clonus","group":"Reflexes and tone","signs":["clonus"],"purpose":"Detects increased tone and characterises it as spasticity (pyramidal) or rigidity (extrapyramidal), or reduced tone (cerebellar, LMN). Ankle clonus is the most objective sign of upper motor neurone hyperexcitability in the legs.","kit":["No instruments","Patient lying supine on a couch with legs exposed; a quiet room"],"setup":[{"do":"Patient supine and as relaxed as possible, arms by the sides, legs straight. Ask them to let you move their limbs and to go floppy like a rag doll.","why":"Tone is resistance to passive movement; any voluntary help or resistance invalidates the test. Supine position removes the need for antigravity postural tone."},{"do":"Distract by chatting; if the patient keeps helping, ask them to count backwards from 20.","why":"Patients often unconsciously assist or resist. A cognitive task diverts attention and improves relaxation."}],"steps":[{"do":"Upper limb: shake hands with the patient (your right hand to their right hand) and support their elbow with your other hand. Pronate and supinate the forearm, then flex and extend the wrist and elbow, varying speed from slow to fast and unpredictable.","why":"Spasticity is velocity-dependent; a fast movement provokes a stretch-reflex catch that a slow movement misses. Varying speed and rhythm prevents the patient anticipating and helping.","cue":"Normal: smooth, little resistance at any speed."},{"do":"Feel specifically for a supinator catch: during fast supination, a sudden resistance that then gives way.","why":"UMN spasticity predominantly affects the antigravity flexors and pronators in the arm, so the catch appears on rapid supination (stretching the pronators). It is often the earliest sign of upper limb spasticity.","cue":"A sudden halt, then release, felt in the first part of the movement."},{"do":"Lower limb, knee roll: with legs straight, place your hands on the thigh and roll the leg briskly from side to side.","why":"With normal tone the foot lags behind and flops; with increased tone the foot moves rigidly with the leg.","cue":"Normal: foot flops loosely after the thigh moves."},{"do":"Knee lift: place your hand under the knee and lift it briskly upward.","why":"With normal tone the heel stays on the bed or slides up; with spasticity the quadriceps catches and the heel lifts off the bed.","cue":"Normal: heel stays in contact with the couch."},{"do":"Ankle clonus: flex the hip and knee to about 30 to 45 degrees with the thigh slightly externally rotated, supporting the knee or calf with your left hand. Hold the forefoot with your right hand and briskly dorsiflex the foot, then maintain gentle upward pressure.","why":"Knee flexion relaxes gastrocnemius so the stretch is delivered mainly to soleus; brisk dorsiflexion is a strong stretch, and maintaining pressure allows the stretch reflex to cycle repeatedly if the arc is hyperexcitable.","cue":"You feel rhythmic beats of plantar flexion against your hand."},{"do":"Count the beats. Sustained clonus (more than 3 to 5 beats, or continuing while pressure is maintained) is abnormal. Repeat on the other side.","why":"A few unsustained beats can occur normally in anxious people with brisk reflexes. Sustained clonus represents a stretch reflex loop no longer damped by descending inhibition.","cue":"You can state the number of beats and whether it was sustained."},{"do":"If you suspect rigidity, test at slow and fast speeds; then add synkinesis (activation): ask the patient to open and close the opposite hand, or draw circles with it, while you move the wrist.","why":"Rigidity is velocity-independent and present throughout the range in both flexors and extensors. Contralateral activation increases rigidity and makes subtle cogwheeling detectable (Froment manoeuvre).","cue":"Resistance increases with activation; cogwheeling is a ratchety, jerky resistance."}],"normal":"Smooth, slight resistance throughout the range at all speeds, the foot flops on knee roll, the heel stays down on knee lift, and no more than a few unsustained beats of ankle clonus.","abnormal":[{"finding":"Velocity-dependent catch, mainly in arm pronators and flexors and leg extensors, giving way (clasp-knife)","means":"Spasticity: UMN lesion (stroke, cord lesion, MS)"},{"finding":"Sustained ankle clonus (more than 3 to 5 beats)","means":"UMN lesion affecting the leg; can also occur transiently with marked hyperreflexia (for example serotonin syndrome, where it is often inducible and spontaneous)"},{"finding":"Uniform resistance throughout the range, both directions, independent of speed (lead pipe)","means":"Extrapyramidal rigidity: Parkinson's disease, antipsychotic drugs, neuroleptic malignant syndrome"},{"finding":"Rigidity with superimposed ratcheting (cogwheel), increased by contralateral activation","means":"Parkinsonism (rigidity plus tremor)"},{"finding":"Variable resistance that increases with speed and the patient's apparent opposition (gegenhalten, paratonia)","means":"Frontal lobe dysfunction, dementia"},{"finding":"Reduced tone, floppy limb","means":"LMN lesion, acute UMN lesion in the spinal shock phase, cerebellar lesion"}],"errors":[{"error":"Moving the limb only slowly","result":"Missed spasticity because it is velocity-dependent","fix":"Vary speed, include brisk unpredictable movements"},{"error":"Testing ankle clonus with a straight knee","result":"Gastrocnemius tension and patient discomfort, poorer response","fix":"Flex the knee 30 to 45 degrees and support it, then dorsiflex briskly"},{"error":"Dorsiflexing the ankle and then releasing immediately","result":"Clonus cannot sustain, false negative","fix":"Maintain gentle upward pressure after the brisk dorsiflexion"},{"error":"Calling a few beats of clonus pathological","result":"Over-diagnosis in anxious hyperreflexic patients","fix":"Record the number of beats; consider pathological when sustained or asymmetric"},{"error":"Not distracting the patient during tone testing","result":"Voluntary resistance mimics increased tone","fix":"Chat, ask them to count backwards, and test when they are relaxed"}],"trouble":[{"problem":"The patient keeps helping me move the arm","fix":"Take their full weight, tell them 'let me do all the work', and change direction unpredictably. If they still assist, try counting backwards or have them look away."},{"problem":"I can't tell whether the resistance is spasticity or rigidity","fix":"Move slowly then quickly: spasticity increases with speed, rigidity does not. Spasticity affects certain groups and gives way; rigidity is uniform in both directions. Add contralateral activation; rigidity increases."}],"pearls":["Spasticity is velocity-dependent and directional; rigidity is velocity-independent and in all directions.","Supinator catch is often the earliest sign of upper limb spasticity.","In the acute phase of a stroke or cord injury, tone may be reduced (shock) and develop to spasticity over days to weeks.","Inducible and spontaneous clonus, especially with ocular clonus, should prompt you to consider serotonin syndrome.","Conventions differ on how many beats define pathological clonus (often more than 3 or more than 5); sustained clonus is unequivocally abnormal."],"evidence":[{"stat":"Agreement on tone assessment is variable and better for marked than subtle change; inducible clonus with hyperreflexia is a key criterion for serotonin toxicity","src":"Dunkley 2003, QJM (Hunter criteria)"}],"document":"Normal: 'Tone normal in all four limbs, no clonus.' Abnormal: 'Tone increased in left arm (supinator catch) and leg (knee lift positive); sustained ankle clonus on the left, 2 unsustained beats on the right.'","hook":"Fast for spasticity, slow for rigidity, and hold the ankle up to count the clonus.","mcq":[{"q":"A doctor tests ankle clonus with the leg straight, dorsiflexes the foot sharply and immediately lets go. No clonus. The patient has a known cord lesion. What should he change?","opts":["Use a reflex hammer on the heel","Flex the knee to 30 to 45 degrees, support the calf, dorsiflex briskly and maintain pressure","Test only seated","Push the foot into plantar flexion"],"a":1,"why":"Knee flexion relaxes gastrocnemius and maintained dorsiflexion pressure allows the loop to cycle; letting go stops clonus before it can be seen."},{"q":"On moving a patient's wrist, the resistance is the same at slow and fast speeds and in flexion and extension, and increases when they open and close the other hand. What is this?","opts":["Spasticity","Paratonia","Clasp-knife phenomenon","Rigidity"],"a":3,"why":"Velocity-independent, bidirectional resistance increased by synkinesis is extrapyramidal rigidity."},{"q":"An anxious patient has 3 beats of unsustained clonus at both ankles, symmetric brisk reflexes, flexor plantars and normal tone. What is the best interpretation?","opts":["Likely normal; record the beats and look for other UMN signs","Cord compression requiring urgent MRI","Motor neurone disease","Serotonin toxicity"],"a":0,"why":"A few unsustained beats can be normal with anxiety and physiological hyperreflexia; sustained or asymmetric clonus, or other UMN signs, would change this."}],"src":["Macleod's Clinical Examination 15e","Talley and O'Connor, Clinical Examination","Dunkley EJ et al 2003, QJM"]},{"id":"pronator-drift","name":"Pronator drift","group":"Coordination and gait","signs":["pronator-drift"],"purpose":"A quick, sensitive screen for subtle upper motor neurone weakness of the arm before formal power testing reveals it. Use it in suspected stroke, TIA or any possible hemispheric lesion.","kit":["No instruments","Patient seated or standing, arms exposed"],"setup":[{"do":"Patient sitting upright or standing, facing you, with enough space for both arms to be extended forward.","why":"A stable trunk lets the arms be held against gravity; facing the patient lets you watch both arms together."},{"do":"Demonstrate the position yourself.","why":"Patients with mild dysphasia or anxiety follow a demonstration more accurately than verbal instructions."}],"steps":[{"do":"Ask the patient to hold both arms straight out in front at shoulder height (90 degrees of shoulder flexion), elbows extended, palms facing upward (fully supinated), fingers together and extended.","why":"Full supination requires the supinators and is the position most sensitive to the subtle weakness pattern of UMN lesions, in which supinators and arm extensors are weaker than pronators and flexors.","cue":"Both palms face the ceiling, arms level and parallel."},{"do":"Ask them to close their eyes and hold the position for 20 to 30 seconds.","why":"Closing the eyes removes visual correction, so a subtle drift caused by weakness or proprioceptive loss becomes visible. Sustained posture is needed because drift develops gradually.","cue":"Eyes closed, arms steady at the start."},{"do":"Watch both hands from the front and side, looking first for pronation of the forearm, then downward drift and elbow flexion.","why":"UMN weakness affects supinators and extensors more, so the forearm pronates first, followed by downward drift and slight elbow and finger flexion.","cue":"You can say whether any forearm pronated, and whether any arm drifted down."},{"do":"Optionally, tap each arm briskly downward while the eyes remain closed and watch the return.","why":"This tests cerebellar rebound; in cerebellar lesions the arm overshoots on return, and the tap can also increase a subtle drift.","cue":"Normal: the arm returns promptly to position without overshoot."},{"do":"Note any other drift pattern: upward or outward drift, or searching finger movements (pseudoathetosis).","why":"Upward and outward drift suggests a cerebellar lesion; searching finger movements and drift in any direction suggest loss of proprioception.","cue":"You can classify the drift pattern."}],"normal":"Both arms stay level with palms up for 20 to 30 seconds with eyes closed; slight symmetrical sinking without pronation can be normal.","abnormal":[{"finding":"Forearm pronation followed by downward drift and slight flexion of elbow and fingers","means":"Pyramidal (UMN) weakness, contralateral hemisphere or brainstem lesion"},{"finding":"Upward or outward drift, overshoot on tapping","means":"Ipsilateral cerebellar lesion"},{"finding":"Searching finger movements, drift in any direction only with eyes closed","means":"Proprioceptive loss (pseudoathetosis)"},{"finding":"Downward drift without pronation, or the arm drops abruptly","means":"Suggests functional weakness, especially if inconsistent; consider other functional signs"}],"errors":[{"error":"Palms facing down","result":"Pronation cannot be seen, losing the earliest sign; lower sensitivity","fix":"Ensure full supination, palms facing the ceiling"},{"error":"Eyes left open","result":"Visual correction masks drift, false negative","fix":"Ask the patient to close the eyes and check they remain closed"},{"error":"Watching for only 5 seconds","result":"Subtle drift not yet developed, false negative","fix":"Watch for 20 to 30 seconds"},{"error":"Calling slight symmetric sinking pathological","result":"False positive","fix":"Look for pronation and asymmetry; symmetric slight sinking without pronation is usually normal fatigue"}],"trouble":[{"problem":"The patient can't keep their eyes closed or follow instructions","fix":"Demonstrate, place their arms in position yourself, and ask them to keep them there; in dysphasic patients you can still observe drift with eyes open, though it is less sensitive."},{"problem":"I see drift but power testing is normal","fix":"Pronator drift can detect weakness before formal power testing. Check other subtle UMN signs: finger tapping speed, forearm rolling, reflex asymmetry and plantar response."}],"pearls":["Pronation comes before drift; watch the palms first.","Pronator drift can be present when formal power testing is normal, so it earns its place in a stroke or TIA screen.","The pattern of drift points to the system: pronation (pyramidal), upward or outward (cerebellar), searching (proprioceptive), drop without pronation (consider functional).","Pair it with finger tapping and forearm rolling for a rapid screen of subtle hemiparesis."],"evidence":[{"stat":"Pronator drift is useful for subtle unilateral pyramidal weakness, and is complemented by finger tapping and forearm rolling","src":"McGee, Evidence-Based Physical Diagnosis 5e"}],"document":"Normal: 'No pronator drift.' Abnormal: 'Left pronator drift (pronation then downward drift within 15 seconds, eyes closed).'","hook":"Palms up, eyes shut, and the weak arm turns over before it sinks.","mcq":[{"q":"A doctor asks a patient with suspected TIA to hold the arms out, palms down, eyes open, for 5 seconds. No drift is seen. What is wrong?","opts":["Nothing","Arms should be at the sides","Palms should face up, eyes closed, and hold for 20 to 30 seconds","The test should be done lying down"],"a":2,"why":"Supination exposes pronation, eye closure removes visual correction, and drift needs time to develop."},{"q":"In a patient holding the arms out, one arm drifts upward and outward and overshoots when tapped. What does this suggest?","opts":["Pyramidal weakness","Ipsilateral cerebellar lesion","Proprioceptive loss","Functional weakness"],"a":1,"why":"Upward and outward drift with rebound overshoot is a cerebellar pattern."},{"q":"With eyes closed, a patient's fingers make slow searching movements and the arm wanders, but power is normal. What is the most likely cause?","opts":["Loss of joint position sense","Basal ganglia lesion","UMN weakness","Myasthenia"],"a":0,"why":"Pseudoathetosis with eyes closed reflects loss of proprioceptive input."}],"src":["Macleod's Clinical Examination 15e","McGee, Evidence-Based Physical Diagnosis 5e","Talley and O'Connor, Clinical Examination"]},{"id":"romberg","name":"Romberg's test","group":"Coordination and gait","signs":["romberg"],"purpose":"Tests whether balance depends on vision because proprioception (dorsal column or large fibre sensory input) is impaired. Reach for it in unsteadiness, falls, numbness of the feet or suspected B12 deficiency, tabes or sensory neuropathy.","kit":["No instruments","Clear floor space, a wall or bed nearby, and the patient in socks or barefoot"],"setup":[{"do":"Stand close beside or behind the patient with your arms ready (not touching) to catch them, and clear obstacles.","why":"A positive Romberg means the patient may fall; guarding prevents injury and lets the patient relax enough for a valid test."},{"do":"Explain: 'Stand with your feet together, arms by your side. I'll be right here to catch you.'","why":"Reassurance reduces anxiety-related sway and stops patients stepping out prematurely."}],"steps":[{"do":"Ask the patient to stand with feet together (heels and toes touching), arms by the sides or crossed over the chest, eyes open, for about 30 seconds.","why":"Feet together narrows the base of support so that balance needs all three inputs (vision, vestibular, proprioception). Eyes open first establishes whether they can stand at all; if not, the test cannot be interpreted.","cue":"The patient stands steady with eyes open."},{"do":"Ask them to close their eyes and hold for up to 60 seconds, while you guard.","why":"Removing vision leaves balance to proprioception and vestibular input; if proprioception is impaired, the patient sways and loses balance.","cue":"Normal: slight sway, no stepping out."},{"do":"Record positive if the patient loses balance with eyes closed (steps out, needs to be caught, or sways so markedly they would fall) but was steady with eyes open.","why":"The sign is defined by the difference between eyes open and eyes closed, not by unsteadiness itself.","cue":"You can state clearly: stable eyes open, unstable eyes closed."},{"do":"If normal and you suspect subtle impairment, perform the sharpened (tandem) Romberg: one foot directly in front of the other, heel to toe, arms crossed, eyes closed, up to 60 seconds.","why":"The narrower base increases difficulty and sensitivity for subtle proprioceptive or vestibular deficits.","cue":"Healthy young adults can usually hold it; performance declines with age."},{"do":"If the patient cannot stand steady even with eyes open, do not proceed; record 'unable to stand feet together eyes open' and move to gait and cerebellar testing.","why":"Instability with eyes open points to a cerebellar or severe vestibular or motor problem; Romberg is then uninterpretable.","cue":"Clear documentation of why the test was not completed."}],"normal":"The patient stands with feet together, eyes open and eyes closed, with only slight sway and no stepping out for 60 seconds.","abnormal":[{"finding":"Stable eyes open, loses balance eyes closed","means":"Positive Romberg: proprioceptive loss (dorsal column disease, sensory neuropathy, B12 deficiency, tabes dorsalis) or, less commonly, bilateral vestibular failure"},{"finding":"Unsteady with eyes open and eyes closed","means":"Not a Romberg sign: suggests cerebellar ataxia, severe vestibular dysfunction or weakness"},{"finding":"Falls consistently to one side with eyes closed","means":"Acute unilateral vestibular lesion (falls toward the side of the lesion)"},{"finding":"Large sway from the hips without falling, improving with distraction","means":"Suggests functional imbalance"}],"errors":[{"error":"Calling unsteadiness with eyes open a positive Romberg","result":"Misattribution of cerebellar ataxia to proprioceptive loss","fix":"Only call positive when stable eyes open and unstable eyes closed"},{"error":"Using the Romberg as a cerebellar test","result":"Wrong localisation","fix":"Remember it tests proprioception; use finger-nose, heel-shin and gait for the cerebellum"},{"error":"Not guarding the patient","result":"Falls and injury; anxious patient steps out early","fix":"Stand close with arms ready and reassure the patient"},{"error":"Calling slight sway positive","result":"False positive","fix":"Look for loss of balance requiring a step or catch"}],"trouble":[{"problem":"The patient sways a lot but never falls","fix":"Distract them (ask them to count backwards or touch their nose); functional sway often improves with distraction. Assess sensation, reflexes and gait."},{"problem":"The patient is too frail to stand with feet together","fix":"Record it, assess gait with support, test joint position sense and vibration at the toes, and consider a seated sitting balance test."}],"pearls":["Romberg is a sensory test: a positive Romberg localises to proprioceptive pathways, not the cerebellum.","Moritz Romberg described it in tabes dorsalis; the classic modern causes are B12 deficiency and large-fibre neuropathy.","Arms position varies (by sides or crossed); conventions differ, but arms must not be used for balance.","Test joint position sense and vibration at the toes to corroborate a positive Romberg."],"evidence":[{"stat":"Romberg sign is associated with large-fibre sensory loss; its accuracy as a single test is limited and it should be combined with vibration and joint position sense","src":"McGee, Evidence-Based Physical Diagnosis 5e"}],"document":"Normal: 'Romberg negative; sharpened Romberg held 30 seconds.' Abnormal: 'Romberg positive: stable eyes open, stepped out at 8 seconds eyes closed; absent vibration to the knees.'","hook":"Romberg asks one question: can you balance without your eyes?","mcq":[{"q":"A patient sways with eyes open and with eyes closed and cannot stand feet together. A doctor records 'Romberg positive, cerebellar'. What is wrong?","opts":["Romberg positive means proprioceptive loss and requires stability eyes open; this is not a Romberg sign","Nothing","Romberg should be done sitting","Romberg is always positive in cerebellar disease"],"a":0,"why":"Romberg compares eyes open and closed; instability with eyes open makes it uninterpretable and points to cerebellar or other causes."},{"q":"A vegan patient with tingling feet stands steady eyes open but falls when eyes are closed. What is the most likely mechanism?","opts":["Cerebellar vermis lesion","Basal ganglia disease","Proprioceptive loss from dorsal column dysfunction, consider B12 deficiency","Functional imbalance"],"a":2,"why":"A positive Romberg reflects proprioceptive loss; B12 deficiency causes dorsal column dysfunction."},{"q":"What is the best way to increase the sensitivity of Romberg's test in a patient with a normal standard test?","opts":["Ask them to stand on one leg eyes open","Perform the sharpened (tandem) Romberg with eyes closed","Push them firmly","Test with eyes open only"],"a":1,"why":"Heel-to-toe stance with eyes closed narrows the base and reveals subtle deficits."}],"src":["Macleod's Clinical Examination 15e","McGee, Evidence-Based Physical Diagnosis 5e","Talley and O'Connor, Clinical Examination"]},{"id":"cerebellar-tests","name":"Cerebellar limb tests","group":"Coordination and gait","signs":["finger-nose"],"purpose":"Detects limb ataxia (dysmetria, intention tremor, dysdiadochokinesia) and localises a cerebellar hemisphere lesion to the ipsilateral side. Use in vertigo, unsteadiness, slurred speech, suspected posterior circulation stroke or toxicity.","kit":["No instruments","Patient seated for upper limb tests, supine for heel-shin"],"setup":[{"do":"Patient seated upright facing you at arm's length, then supine for heel-shin. Check power first in the limbs to be tested.","why":"Weakness causes clumsiness that mimics ataxia; confirming adequate power makes ataxia interpretable."},{"do":"Explain and demonstrate each test.","why":"Demonstration reduces error from misunderstanding, particularly in dysphasia or language barriers."}],"steps":[{"do":"Finger-nose: hold your index finger at the full stretch of the patient's arm in front of them. Ask them to touch your finger with their index finger, then their nose, then back, repeatedly, as accurately as they can.","why":"Full stretch makes the arm reach the end of range, where intention tremor and dysmetria are maximal. A target at less than arm's length hides end-point errors.","cue":"The patient's arm is fully extended when touching your finger."},{"do":"Move your finger to a new position after a few repetitions and watch for overshoot or undershoot.","why":"A moving target requires recalibration and amplifies dysmetria; it also prevents a learned movement that may mask subtle ataxia.","cue":"You see whether the patient lands on the target or overshoots."},{"do":"Watch for intention tremor (oscillation increasing as the finger approaches the target) and past-pointing (overshooting the target).","why":"Cerebellar lesions impair the predictive control of movement termination, giving oscillation and overshoot near the target.","cue":"Tremor that worsens toward the target is intention tremor."},{"do":"Dysdiadochokinesia: ask the patient to tap the palm and then the back of one hand on the other palm (or thigh) as quickly and regularly as possible. Test each side.","why":"Rapid alternating movements require precise timing between agonists and antagonists, which the cerebellum coordinates; in cerebellar lesions the movements are slow, irregular in rhythm and amplitude.","cue":"Normal: rapid, regular, even taps."},{"do":"Heel-shin: patient supine. Ask them to place one heel on the opposite knee and slide it down the front of the shin to the ankle, lift it and return to the knee, repeating several times.","why":"This tests lower limb coordination, which corresponds to the cerebellar control of leg movements; ataxia shows as the heel falling off the shin or wobbling.","cue":"Normal: the heel travels smoothly in a straight line."},{"do":"Rebound: with the patient's arms outstretched, push one arm briskly down and release, or ask them to flex the elbow against your resistance and release suddenly while you shield their face with your other arm.","why":"Cerebellar lesions impair the ability to check a movement; the limb overshoots on release.","cue":"Normal: the limb stops promptly."},{"do":"Compare sides and consider repeating finger-nose with eyes closed (from the outstretched arm to the nose).","why":"Cerebellar ataxia is present with eyes open; sensory ataxia worsens markedly with eyes closed.","cue":"You can state whether the ataxia is worse with eyes closed."}],"normal":"Smooth, accurate finger-nose movements without tremor or overshoot, rapid regular alternating movements, smooth heel-shin, and prompt check on rebound.","abnormal":[{"finding":"Intention tremor and past-pointing on finger-nose","means":"Ipsilateral cerebellar hemisphere lesion"},{"finding":"Slow, irregular alternating movements (dysdiadochokinesia)","means":"Ipsilateral cerebellar lesion; can also occur in pyramidal weakness or parkinsonism (slow but regular)"},{"finding":"Heel falls off the shin or wobbles","means":"Ipsilateral cerebellar dysfunction affecting the leg"},{"finding":"Normal limb tests but wide-based unsteady gait and truncal ataxia","means":"Midline (vermis) cerebellar lesion, alcohol-related cerebellar degeneration"},{"finding":"Ataxia much worse with eyes closed","means":"Sensory ataxia from proprioceptive loss"}],"errors":[{"error":"Holding the target too close","result":"End-point dysmetria and intention tremor missed","fix":"Hold your finger at the full stretch of the patient's arm"},{"error":"Testing ataxia without checking power","result":"Weakness misinterpreted as ataxia","fix":"Test power first; interpret ataxia only with adequate strength"},{"error":"Keeping the target still throughout","result":"Learned movement masks mild dysmetria","fix":"Move your finger to new positions"},{"error":"Not protecting the face on rebound testing","result":"Patient hits their own face","fix":"Shield the face with your other arm"}],"trouble":[{"problem":"The patient has a tremor at rest and I can't tell if it's cerebellar","fix":"Look at when the tremor occurs: resting tremor that improves with movement suggests parkinsonism; tremor that worsens toward the target is intention tremor."},{"problem":"The finger-nose test looks normal but the patient is very unsteady","fix":"Assess gait and tandem walking, and sitting balance; midline cerebellar lesions spare the limbs."}],"pearls":["Cerebellar hemisphere lesions cause ipsilateral limb ataxia; vermis lesions cause truncal and gait ataxia with relatively normal limbs.","In acute vertigo, inability to walk or sit unaided is a red flag for central causes, even if limb tests are normal.","Ataxia with weakness on the same side can occur in ataxic hemiparesis (lacunar syndrome).","Look for nystagmus and dysarthria (scanning speech) as part of the cerebellar picture."],"evidence":[{"stat":"In acute vertigo, severe truncal ataxia (inability to walk or sit unaided) strongly suggests a central cause","src":"Edlow et al 2023, Academic Emergency Medicine (ACEP GRACE-3 guideline)"}],"document":"Normal: 'No cerebellar signs: finger-nose, dysdiadochokinesia and heel-shin normal bilaterally.' Abnormal: 'Right-sided intention tremor and past-pointing, dysdiadochokinesia on the right, right heel-shin ataxia.'","hook":"Full stretch, moving target, and the cerebellum shows its overshoot on the same side.","mcq":[{"q":"A doctor holds his finger 20 cm from the patient's face during finger-nose testing and the test appears normal. What is the main flaw?","opts":["The target should be at full arm's length and moved","The test should be done supine","The patient should keep their eyes closed","Nothing"],"a":0,"why":"Intention tremor and dysmetria are maximal at the end of reach; a close, fixed target hides them."},{"q":"A patient with chronic alcohol use has a wide-based unsteady gait but normal finger-nose and heel-shin. What is the likely localisation?","opts":["Cerebellar hemisphere","Dorsal columns","Basal ganglia","Cerebellar vermis"],"a":3,"why":"Vermis lesions produce truncal and gait ataxia with relatively spared limb tests."},{"q":"A patient's finger-nose accuracy is good with eyes open but very poor with eyes closed. What does this suggest?","opts":["Cerebellar hemisphere lesion","Sensory ataxia from proprioceptive loss","Pyramidal weakness","Functional disorder"],"a":1,"why":"Visual compensation masks sensory ataxia; with eyes closed, proprioceptive loss is exposed."}],"src":["Macleod's Clinical Examination 15e","Talley and O'Connor, Clinical Examination","Edlow JA et al 2023, Academic Emergency Medicine (GRACE-3)"]},{"id":"tandem-gait","name":"Gait and tandem walking","group":"Coordination and gait","signs":["tandem"],"purpose":"Gait is the most integrative test of the motor system, showing weakness, ataxia, sensory loss, extrapyramidal disease and functional disorders. Tandem walking is a sensitive test of midline (vermis) cerebellar and vestibular function.","kit":["A corridor or space at least 6 to 10 metres long","Patient's legs visible from mid-thigh (shorts or rolled trousers), shoes off for heel and toe walking"],"setup":[{"do":"Clear a straight path and stand where you can see the patient from front, back and side; walk alongside to guard if needed.","why":"Different gait features are visible from different angles (base and arm swing from the front, stride and posture from the side). Guarding protects unsteady patients."},{"do":"Ask the patient to walk normally to the end, turn and walk back.","why":"Natural walking shows the true gait pattern; turning stresses balance and reveals en bloc turning or instability."}],"steps":[{"do":"Observe normal walking for at least 10 metres: posture, base width, stride length, arm swing, foot clearance, speed, symmetry and the turn.","why":"Each component is controlled by different systems; for example reduced arm swing is an early extrapyramidal sign, while wide base suggests ataxia.","cue":"You can describe the gait in terms of these components, not just 'abnormal'."},{"do":"Ask the patient to walk heel to toe in a straight line (tandem), arms by sides, for about 10 steps.","why":"The narrow base requires precise midline balance control; it is very sensitive for vermis and vestibular dysfunction.","cue":"Normal adults can complete 10 steps without stepping off the line, though performance declines in the elderly."},{"do":"Ask the patient to walk on the heels, then on the toes.","why":"Heel walking requires ankle dorsiflexion (L4-5, peroneal nerve); toe walking requires plantar flexion (S1). These detect mild foot drop or calf weakness.","cue":"Normal: heels and toes clear the ground."},{"do":"Observe the patient standing up from a chair without using their arms, and consider a squat.","why":"Proximal muscle weakness (myopathy) shows as difficulty rising from a chair.","cue":"Normal: rises without arm support."},{"do":"Identify the named gait by its features, and correlate with the rest of the examination.","why":"Named gaits are shorthand for patterns; correlation with tone, reflexes and sensation confirms the localisation.","cue":"You can name a pattern and its likely cause."}],"normal":"Upright posture, narrow base, symmetrical stride and arm swing, smooth turn, able to walk tandem for 10 steps and walk on heels and toes.","abnormal":[{"finding":"Hemiplegic gait: stiff extended leg circumducting, arm held flexed and adducted","means":"UMN lesion, contralateral hemisphere stroke"},{"finding":"Parkinsonian gait: stooped posture, short shuffling steps, reduced arm swing, festination, en bloc turning","means":"Parkinsonism"},{"finding":"Cerebellar ataxic gait: wide-based, staggering, unable to tandem walk; falls to the side of a hemispheric lesion","means":"Cerebellar lesion (vermis or hemisphere), alcohol, drugs"},{"finding":"Sensory ataxic gait: wide-based, high stepping, stamping, watching the feet, worse in the dark","means":"Proprioceptive loss: dorsal column disease, sensory neuropathy"},{"finding":"Steppage gait: high stepping with foot slap","means":"Foot drop: common peroneal palsy, L5 radiculopathy, peripheral neuropathy"},{"finding":"Waddling gait: trunk sways side to side, pelvis drops on the swing side","means":"Proximal myopathy or bilateral hip girdle weakness"}],"errors":[{"error":"Watching only a few steps in a small room","result":"Subtle gait abnormalities missed","fix":"Use a corridor of 6 to 10 metres and include turning"},{"error":"Not watching arm swing","result":"Early parkinsonism missed","fix":"Look for reduced or asymmetric arm swing"},{"error":"Omitting tandem walking","result":"Midline cerebellar or vestibular dysfunction missed","fix":"Always ask for 10 steps heel to toe"},{"error":"Labelling gait abnormal without describing it","result":"Unhelpful notes, poor localisation","fix":"Describe posture, base, stride, arm swing, turn"}],"trouble":[{"problem":"The gait looks odd and doesn't fit any pattern","fix":"Look for inconsistency, variability, improvement with distraction, knee buckling without falling or excessive slowness, which suggest functional gait disorder; also consider mixed patterns (for example neuropathy with parkinsonism)."},{"problem":"The patient is too unsteady to walk safely","fix":"Walk with them, or assess sitting balance and standing with support. Inability to walk unaided in acute vertigo is a red flag."}],"pearls":["Tandem walking ability declines with age; many people over 70 cannot complete 10 steps without stepping off.","Falling to one side in acute vertigo can occur in vestibular neuritis, but severe truncal ataxia suggests a central cause.","A magnetic or apraxic gait (feet seem stuck to the floor) suggests normal pressure hydrocephalus or frontal disease.","Functional gait often shows dramatic sway without falling, and improves with distraction."],"evidence":[{"stat":"Gait assessment is central to the bedside diagnosis of the acutely dizzy patient; inability to walk unaided is a strong indicator of a central cause","src":"Edlow et al 2023, Academic Emergency Medicine (GRACE-3)"}],"document":"Normal: 'Gait normal; tandem walking 10 steps; heel and toe walking normal.' Abnormal: 'Wide-based ataxic gait, unable to tandem walk (falls to the right after 2 steps), heel walking normal.'","hook":"Watch the walk, name the pattern, then make them walk the line.","mcq":[{"q":"A patient walks with short shuffling steps, reduced arm swing on the right and turns en bloc. What is the most likely pattern?","opts":["Hemiplegic","Steppage","Parkinsonian","Cerebellar"],"a":2,"why":"Shuffling steps, reduced arm swing and en bloc turning are parkinsonian features."},{"q":"A patient has a high stepping gait with foot slap on the left. What is the most likely cause?","opts":["Left foot drop from a common peroneal palsy or L5 radiculopathy","Left cerebellar lesion","Right hemisphere stroke","Proximal myopathy"],"a":0,"why":"High stepping with foot slap compensates for foot drop."},{"q":"A doctor examines gait by watching a patient take three steps in a small cubicle. What should he add?","opts":["Romberg only","Nothing","Neck examination","A longer walk with turning, tandem walking, and heel and toe walking"],"a":3,"why":"A longer walk, turn and tandem walking are needed to reveal subtle and midline abnormalities."}],"src":["Macleod's Clinical Examination 15e","Talley and O'Connor, Clinical Examination","Edlow JA et al 2023, Academic Emergency Medicine (GRACE-3)"]},{"id":"eye-movements","name":"Eye movements, saccades, pursuit and nystagmus","group":"Vestibular and eye movements","signs":[],"purpose":"Answers whether there is an ocular motor palsy, a gaze palsy, an internuclear ophthalmoplegia, abnormal saccades or pursuit, or pathological nystagmus. Reach for it in diplopia, vertigo, suspected stroke or brainstem lesion, head injury, and when examining cranial nerves III, IV and VI.","kit":["A target: pen tip, finger or small toy for children","A second target for saccades (your other finger or a pen)","Occluder or the patient's hand for the cover test","Frenzel goggles or a direct ophthalmoscope to remove fixation for nystagmus, if available"],"setup":[{"do":"Sit facing the patient about 50 to 60 cm away, at the same eye level, and hold the target in the midline.","why":"At 50 to 60 cm the eyes do not need to converge much, so you see true horizontal and vertical movements; closer targets trigger convergence and miosis and may mimic medial rectus weakness."},{"do":"Ask the patient to keep their head still; if needed, place your hand gently on their forehead or chin.","why":"Head movement substitutes for eye movement and hides limitation."},{"do":"Ask about double vision before you start and ask them to tell you if it appears or worsens during the test.","why":"Diplopia reported at specific positions localises the weak muscle more sensitively than visible restriction."}],"steps":[{"do":"Observe the eyes in primary position for any deviation, ptosis, head tilt or spontaneous nystagmus.","why":"A head tilt suggests a fourth nerve palsy; ptosis and a down-and-out eye suggest a third nerve palsy; nystagmus in primary position is always abnormal.","cue":"The eyes are aligned and still, with no ptosis or head tilt."},{"do":"Pursuit: ask the patient to follow the target with their eyes only as you trace an H pattern slowly: horizontally to each side, then up and down at the far left and far right positions.","why":"The H pattern tests each extraocular muscle in its main field of action: lateral rectus (abduction), medial rectus (adduction), superior and inferior recti (up and down in abduction), inferior and superior obliques (up and down in adduction). Slow movement tests smooth pursuit.","cue":"Both eyes move smoothly and fully together in all directions."},{"do":"Stop about 30 degrees from primary position, not at the extreme of gaze.","why":"At extreme gaze, physiological end-point nystagmus (a few fine beats, symmetrical and fatigable) appears in normal people and can be misread as pathological.","cue":"No nystagmus is seen until the extreme position."},{"do":"Ask at each position: 'Is there any double vision?' If yes, ask whether the images are side by side or one above the other, and where they are furthest apart.","why":"Diplopia is maximal in the direction of action of the weak muscle. Horizontal separation points to medial or lateral rectus; vertical or tilted to the vertical muscles.","cue":"The patient can describe where diplopia is worst and its orientation."},{"do":"In the position of maximal diplopia, cover each eye in turn and ask which image disappears.","why":"The outer (more peripheral) image always belongs to the affected eye, because the weak muscle fails to move the eye fully, so the image falls on peripheral retina and is projected further out.","cue":"Covering one eye removes the outer image, identifying the affected eye."},{"do":"Saccades: hold two targets (your finger and a pen) about 30 degrees apart horizontally, then vertically, and ask the patient to look quickly from one to the other.","why":"Saccades test the fast eye movement system: speed, accuracy and conjugacy. Slow adduction in one eye with abducting nystagmus in the other is internuclear ophthalmoplegia (MLF lesion, e.g. MS). Overshoot or undershoot suggests cerebellar disease.","cue":"Both eyes move quickly and accurately to the target without overshoot or slow adduction."},{"do":"Nystagmus: hold the target in primary position and then 30 degrees to each side and up and down for a few seconds, looking for nystagmus.","why":"Pathological nystagmus has slow and fast phases; describe it by the direction of the fast phase. Peripheral vestibular nystagmus is unidirectional, horizontal-torsional, suppressed by fixation, and increases when looking in the direction of the fast phase (Alexander's law). Central nystagmus may change direction with gaze, be purely vertical or torsional, and is not suppressed by fixation.","cue":"You can describe the nystagmus: direction, plane, gaze dependence and whether it is suppressed by fixation."},{"do":"Remove fixation if available: use Frenzel goggles or examine with an ophthalmoscope while covering the other eye.","why":"Fixation suppresses peripheral vestibular nystagmus, so removing it reveals hidden nystagmus. With an ophthalmoscope, the disc moves in the opposite direction to the front of the eye.","cue":"Nystagmus increases without fixation in peripheral causes; central nystagmus is unchanged."},{"do":"Cover test: ask the patient to fix on a target at distance (6 m) and near (33 cm); cover one eye and watch the other uncovered eye for movement; then uncover and watch the covered eye.","why":"The cover test detects manifest squint (tropia) when the uncovered eye moves to take up fixation, and latent squint (phoria) when the covered eye moves back on uncovering.","cue":"No movement of either eye on cover or uncover."}],"normal":"Eyes aligned in primary position, no ptosis or head tilt. Full, smooth, conjugate pursuit in all directions without diplopia. Fast, accurate saccades. No nystagmus except a few symmetrical, fatigable beats at extreme gaze. Cover test shows no movement.","abnormal":[{"finding":"Failure of abduction with horizontal diplopia worse on looking to the affected side","means":"Sixth nerve palsy: raised ICP (false localising sign), microvascular, trauma, tumour."},{"finding":"Ptosis, eye down and out, failure of elevation, depression and adduction, with or without a dilated pupil","means":"Third nerve palsy: a dilated pupil suggests compression (posterior communicating artery aneurysm), an emergency."},{"finding":"Vertical diplopia worse looking down and in, head tilt to opposite side","means":"Fourth nerve palsy: trauma, microvascular, congenital."},{"finding":"Slow or absent adduction in one eye with nystagmus in the abducting eye","means":"Internuclear ophthalmoplegia: MLF lesion, MS in young, stroke in older."},{"finding":"Direction-changing gaze-evoked nystagmus, vertical or torsional nystagmus","means":"Central lesion: brainstem or cerebellar stroke, MS, drugs."},{"finding":"Unidirectional horizontal nystagmus, suppressed by fixation, with acute vertigo","means":"Peripheral vestibular lesion (vestibular neuritis); use HINTS to exclude stroke."}],"errors":[{"error":"Moving the target too close to the patient.","result":"Convergence and miosis mimic medial rectus weakness or hide abduction weakness.","fix":"Hold the target at 50 to 60 cm."},{"error":"Moving the target to the extreme of gaze.","result":"Physiological end-point nystagmus is misread as pathological.","fix":"Stop at about 30 degrees from primary position."},{"error":"Not asking about diplopia.","result":"Subtle muscle weakness is missed.","fix":"Ask about diplopia in each position and use the outer image rule."},{"error":"Letting the head move.","result":"Head movement hides limitation.","fix":"Hold the patient's chin or forehead gently."},{"error":"Moving the target too fast in pursuit.","result":"Normal patients show saccadic (broken) pursuit, misread as pathological.","fix":"Move the target slowly and smoothly, about 2 seconds to cross each half of the field."},{"error":"Not removing fixation in suspected vestibular nystagmus.","result":"Peripheral nystagmus is missed.","fix":"Use Frenzel goggles or the ophthalmoscope trick."}],"trouble":[{"problem":"The patient can't keep their head still.","fix":"Hold their chin gently, or ask them to place their chin on their hand; if they still move, use a smaller target and repeat slowly."},{"problem":"I see nystagmus but can't tell if it's peripheral or central.","fix":"Describe it by fast phase direction, plane, gaze dependence and effect of fixation; if direction-changing, vertical or torsional, or not suppressed by fixation, treat as central. Use HINTS in acute vestibular syndrome."},{"problem":"The patient reports diplopia but I can't see a restriction.","fix":"Use the cover test in the position of maximal diplopia and ask which image disappears; the outer image belongs to the affected eye."}],"pearls":["The outer image always belongs to the affected eye.","A painful third nerve palsy with pupil involvement is an aneurysm until proved otherwise.","HINTS (head impulse, nystagmus, test of skew) is more sensitive than early MRI for stroke in acute vestibular syndrome when performed by trained examiners.","Physiological end-point nystagmus is symmetrical and fatigable; pathological nystagmus persists.","Textbooks vary on the distance for testing (30 cm to 1 m); 50 to 60 cm is a practical compromise to avoid convergence."],"evidence":[{"stat":"In acute vestibular syndrome, HINTS performed by trained examiners had sensitivity 100% and specificity 96% for stroke, better than early MRI.","src":"Kattah et al 2009, Stroke"},{"stat":"The outer image rule and cover test reliably identify the affected eye in paralytic strabismus.","src":"Macleod's Clinical Examination 15e"}],"document":"Normal: Eye movements full and smooth in all directions, no diplopia, no nystagmus, saccades normal, cover test no movement. Abnormal: L abduction limited, horizontal diplopia worse on L gaze, outer image L eye. Impression: L sixth nerve palsy.","hook":"H at 60 cm, stop short of extreme, ask about diplopia, cover to find the outer image, and describe nystagmus by its fast phase.","mcq":[{"q":"A doctor tests eye movements with the target at 20 cm and finds both eyes fail to abduct fully. What is the likely error?","opts":["The target is too close, triggering convergence","The doctor should use a red target","The patient has bilateral sixth nerve palsy","There is no error"],"a":0,"why":"At 20 cm, convergence pulls the eyes inwards, mimicking abduction weakness. Hold the target at 50 to 60 cm."},{"q":"A patient reports horizontal diplopia worst on looking left. Covering the left eye removes the outer image. Which muscle is weak?","opts":["Right lateral rectus","Right medial rectus","Left medial rectus","Left lateral rectus"],"a":3,"why":"Diplopia is maximal in the direction of action of the weak muscle (left gaze: left lateral rectus or right medial rectus). The outer image belongs to the affected eye (left), so the left lateral rectus is weak."},{"q":"A doctor sees a few beats of nystagmus at extreme lateral gaze in both directions, symmetrical and fatiguing, and diagnoses cerebellar disease. What is wrong?","opts":["Nothing, this is cerebellar nystagmus","This is physiological end-point nystagmus; stop at about 30 degrees from primary position","The doctor should use Frenzel goggles","This is peripheral vestibular nystagmus"],"a":1,"why":"Physiological end-point nystagmus occurs at extreme gaze, is symmetrical and fatigable, and is normal. Testing at about 30 degrees avoids this pitfall."}],"src":["Macleod's Clinical Examination 15e","Kattah et al 2009, Stroke: HINTS to diagnose stroke in the acute vestibular syndrome","Talley and O'Connor's Clinical Examination 9e"]},{"id":"dix-hallpike","name":"Dix-Hallpike and supine roll test","group":"Vestibular and eye movements","signs":["dix-hallpike"],"purpose":"Diagnoses benign paroxysmal positional vertigo (BPPV) and identifies the affected ear and canal. Use in brief, recurrent, positional vertigo; not in continuous vertigo.","kit":["Examination couch long enough for the head to extend beyond the end, or a pillow under the shoulders","Frenzel goggles or video goggles if available to remove visual fixation","Patient's glasses removed"],"setup":[{"do":"Screen for contraindications: cervical spine instability or recent neck injury, severe cervical spondylosis or rheumatoid neck disease, known vertebrobasilar insufficiency or carotid dissection, and severe cardiovascular instability. In these, consider a side-lying modification or avoid.","why":"The manoeuvre involves rapid neck rotation and extension, which can injure an unstable neck or compromise vertebral flow."},{"do":"Sit the patient on the couch so that when lying back the head will extend beyond the end. Explain that the test may provoke vertigo and ask them to keep their eyes open and look straight ahead throughout.","why":"Head extension is needed to align the posterior canal; open eyes are required to see the nystagmus. Warning prevents the patient from closing their eyes or sitting up in alarm."}],"steps":[{"do":"Stand beside or behind the patient. Rotate the head 45 degrees toward the side being tested.","why":"This aligns the posterior semicircular canal of the downmost ear with the sagittal plane of the movement, so the lie-back moves the canal in its plane and debris (otoconia) moves by gravity.","cue":"The patient's nose points 45 degrees toward the tested ear."},{"do":"Holding the head in your hands, rapidly lay the patient back (within about 1 to 2 seconds) so the head is extended 20 to 30 degrees below horizontal, keeping the 45 degree rotation.","why":"Rapid movement and extension move the otoconia to the most dependent part of the canal, causing endolymph flow and cupular deflection.","cue":"The head is below the horizontal and supported by your hands."},{"do":"Hold the position for at least 30 seconds (up to 45 to 60 if no nystagmus) and watch the eyes closely.","why":"Nystagmus in BPPV has a latency of a few seconds and lasts less than a minute; leaving too soon misses it.","cue":"You are watching the eyes continuously, ideally with goggles."},{"do":"Look for the posterior canal BPPV pattern: after a latency of a few seconds, torsional and upbeating nystagmus with the upper pole of the eye beating toward the lowermost (affected) ear, building and then fading within about 30 seconds, accompanied by vertigo.","why":"Stimulation of the posterior canal produces eye movement in its plane, giving torsional and vertical (upbeating) components. Latency and fatigue reflect the time for otoconia to move and settle.","cue":"You can describe direction, latency and duration."},{"do":"Sit the patient up slowly and watch for reversal of the nystagmus.","why":"As otoconia move back, endolymph flows in the opposite direction, so the nystagmus reverses.","cue":"Brief reversed nystagmus on sitting up supports BPPV."},{"do":"Repeat on the other side. If both are negative and horizontal canal BPPV is suspected, perform the supine roll test: patient supine with the head flexed 30 degrees, rotate the head quickly 90 degrees to one side, observe, return to midline, then rotate to the other side.","why":"Head flexion places the horizontal canals in the vertical plane so rotation moves debris; horizontal nystagmus results, either geotropic (toward the ground, canalolithiasis) or apogeotropic (away, cupulolithiasis).","cue":"Horizontal nystagmus on rotation to each side; in geotropic type the stronger side is affected, in apogeotropic type the weaker side is affected."},{"do":"If findings are atypical, treat as possible central positional nystagmus.","why":"Central lesions (cerebellar, brainstem) can cause positional nystagmus without latency or fatigue, or with pure vertical or pure torsional direction.","cue":"Recognise warning features and arrange neuroimaging or specialist review."}],"normal":"No nystagmus and no vertigo in either head-hanging position.","abnormal":[{"finding":"Latent, transient (less than 1 minute), torsional upbeating nystagmus with the upper pole beating toward the lower ear, with vertigo, reversing on sitting up and fatiguing on repetition","means":"Posterior canal BPPV of the lower ear"},{"finding":"Horizontal geotropic nystagmus on supine roll","means":"Horizontal canal canalolithiasis; affected side is the side with stronger nystagmus"},{"finding":"Horizontal apogeotropic nystagmus on supine roll","means":"Horizontal canal cupulolithiasis; affected side is the side with weaker nystagmus"},{"finding":"Positional nystagmus without latency, without fatigue, pure downbeating or pure vertical, persistent or without vertigo","means":"Central positional nystagmus; consider cerebellar or brainstem lesion"},{"finding":"Downbeating torsional nystagmus","means":"Possible anterior canal BPPV (rare), but consider central causes"}],"errors":[{"error":"Not rotating the head 45 degrees","result":"Posterior canal not aligned; false negative","fix":"Rotate the head 45 degrees toward the tested ear before lying back"},{"error":"Lying back slowly","result":"Otoconia move gradually; nystagmus may not appear","fix":"Lie back quickly within 1 to 2 seconds"},{"error":"Not extending the head below the horizontal","result":"Canal not fully dependent; false negative","fix":"Extend 20 to 30 degrees below horizontal, beyond the end of the couch"},{"error":"Sitting the patient up too early","result":"Missed nystagmus with latency","fix":"Hold for at least 30 seconds"},{"error":"Performing in continuous vertigo","result":"Misdiagnosis; BPPV causes brief episodes only","fix":"Use HINTS for acute continuous vertigo with nystagmus"}],"trouble":[{"problem":"The patient closes their eyes when the vertigo starts","fix":"Warn beforehand, encourage them to keep eyes open on your forehead, or gently hold the eyelids open. Frenzel goggles help."},{"problem":"I can't tell the direction of torsional nystagmus","fix":"Look at the top of the iris (the upper pole) and watch which ear it beats toward. Use goggles or a phone video to slow it down."},{"problem":"The patient has neck problems","fix":"Use the side-lying test: sit the patient on the side of the couch, turn the head 45 degrees away from the side tested, then lie them quickly onto the tested side."}],"pearls":["Posterior canal BPPV is the most common type; the nystagmus is upbeating and torsional toward the lower ear.","Latency, short duration, fatigue and reversal on sitting are hallmarks of peripheral BPPV; their absence is a warning sign.","Treat a positive posterior canal test with the Epley manoeuvre for the same side.","Conventions on hold time vary (30 to 60 seconds); longer hold reduces false negatives in long-latency cases."],"evidence":[{"stat":"Dix-Hallpike sensitivity 79%, specificity 75% for posterior canal BPPV (LR+ about 3.2, LR- about 0.27)","src":"Halker et al 2008, The Neurologist"},{"stat":"AAO-HNS recommends diagnosing posterior canal BPPV with the Dix-Hallpike and horizontal canal BPPV with the supine roll test","src":"Bhattacharyya et al 2017, Otolaryngology-Head and Neck Surgery (AAO-HNS guideline)"}],"document":"Normal: 'Dix-Hallpike negative bilaterally.' Abnormal: 'Right Dix-Hallpike positive: latency 3 seconds, torsional upbeating nystagmus beating toward the right ear, 20 seconds, with vertigo, reversed on sitting. Left negative. Right posterior canal BPPV; Epley performed.'","hook":"Turn 45, drop fast, hang below, wait 30 and read the twist.","mcq":[{"q":"A doctor performs Dix-Hallpike with the head straight, lies the patient slowly onto the couch with the head on a pillow, and sits them up after 10 seconds. No nystagmus. What is wrong?","opts":["The head should be turned 45 degrees, the movement rapid, the head extended below horizontal, and the position held at least 30 seconds","Dix-Hallpike should be done only with eyes closed","The patient should be standing","Nothing"],"a":0,"why":"Each element aligns and moves the posterior canal; omitting them leads to false negatives."},{"q":"After a Dix-Hallpike to the left, there is immediate pure downbeating nystagmus without vertigo that persists without fatigue. What should be done?","opts":["Perform an Epley manoeuvre on the left","Treat as possible central positional nystagmus and arrange neuroimaging","Reassure","Repeat on the right"],"a":1,"why":"Lack of latency and fatigue, pure downbeating and absence of vertigo suggest a central lesion."},{"q":"In supine roll testing, horizontal geotropic nystagmus is stronger on turning to the right. Which ear is affected?","opts":["Left ear","Both","Cannot be determined","Right ear"],"a":3,"why":"In geotropic horizontal canal BPPV, the side with stronger nystagmus is affected."}],"src":["Bhattacharyya N et al 2017, Otolaryngology-Head and Neck Surgery (AAO-HNS BPPV guideline)","Halker RB et al 2008, The Neurologist","Macleod's Clinical Examination 15e"]},{"id":"hints","name":"HINTS examination","group":"Vestibular and eye movements","signs":["hints"],"purpose":"Distinguishes vestibular neuritis from posterior circulation stroke in acute vestibular syndrome: acute continuous vertigo lasting hours to days with spontaneous nystagmus, nausea, head-motion intolerance and unsteadiness. It is not valid in episodic or positional vertigo, or without nystagmus.","kit":["No instruments","Good light, the patient seated upright facing you, glasses removed","Optional Frenzel or video goggles"],"setup":[{"do":"Confirm that the patient has acute vestibular syndrome: continuous vertigo of recent onset, nystagmus present at rest or on gaze, nausea, head motion intolerance. If not, HINTS does not apply.","why":"HINTS was validated only in patients with this syndrome; an abnormal head impulse is reassuring only if there is spontaneous nystagmus from an acute unilateral vestibular loss."},{"do":"Sit the patient upright, facing you, with eyes on your nose; explain that you will move their head quickly and they should keep looking at your nose.","why":"Continuous fixation is required for the head impulse to reveal the corrective saccade; relaxation of the neck allows rapid passive movement."}],"steps":[{"do":"Head impulse test: hold the patient's head with both hands, gently rotate it side to side to relax the neck, then apply a small (10 to 20 degrees), rapid, unpredictable thrust toward one side, stopping abruptly. Repeat to each side several times.","why":"Rapid head rotation stimulates the horizontal canal on the side the head turns toward. The vestibulo-ocular reflex normally keeps the eyes fixed. If the canal or nerve on that side is damaged, the eyes move with the head and a corrective saccade back to target follows.","cue":"You watch the eyes, not the head, after the thrust stops."},{"do":"Look for a corrective saccade back to your nose immediately after the head stops.","why":"A corrective (catch-up) saccade indicates a peripheral vestibular deficit on the side the head was turned toward. In AVS, a normal head impulse (no saccade) suggests the vestibular nerve is intact and points to a central cause.","cue":"Abnormal: a visible flick of the eyes back to the target after the head stops."},{"do":"Nystagmus: observe the eyes in primary gaze, then with gaze 30 degrees to the right and left (do not go to extreme gaze).","why":"Peripheral nystagmus is unidirectional, horizontal-torsional, beating away from the lesion and increasing when looking in the direction of the fast phase (Alexander's law). Central nystagmus can change direction with gaze or be pure vertical or torsional. Extreme gaze can produce physiological end-point nystagmus.","cue":"You can state whether the fast phase direction changes with gaze."},{"do":"Test of skew: ask the patient to look at your nose, cover one eye with your hand, then rapidly switch to cover the other eye; repeat several times and watch the uncovered eye for vertical refixation.","why":"Skew deviation is a vertical misalignment caused by imbalance of otolith-ocular pathways in the brainstem; alternate cover reveals the vertical correction.","cue":"Abnormal: the uncovered eye moves up or down to refixate."},{"do":"Interpret using INFARCT: Impulse Normal, Fast-phase Alternating, Refixation on Cover Test. Any one of these suggests a central cause.","why":"A peripheral pattern requires all three components to be peripheral: abnormal head impulse, unidirectional nystagmus, no skew. Any central feature points to stroke.","cue":"You can classify the HINTS as peripheral or central."},{"do":"Add HINTS plus: check hearing at the bedside (finger rub or whisper) for new unilateral hearing loss.","why":"Anterior inferior cerebellar artery infarcts can involve the labyrinth, giving an abnormal head impulse that falsely appears peripheral; new hearing loss in acute vertigo should be treated as central until proven otherwise.","cue":"You can state whether hearing is equal."},{"do":"Assess gait and sitting balance.","why":"Severe truncal ataxia, inability to sit or stand unaided, is a strong central feature that HINTS does not capture.","cue":"You can state whether the patient can walk unaided."}],"normal":"In AVS, a peripheral pattern: abnormal head impulse to one side (with corrective saccade), unidirectional horizontal-torsional nystagmus beating away from the lesion and increasing on gaze toward the fast phase, no skew deviation, no new hearing loss, and able to walk with some unsteadiness.","abnormal":[{"finding":"Normal head impulse in a patient with AVS","means":"Suggests central cause (stroke) despite vertigo"},{"finding":"Direction-changing nystagmus on lateral gaze","means":"Central cause"},{"finding":"Vertical refixation on alternate cover (skew deviation)","means":"Central cause, brainstem lesion"},{"finding":"New unilateral hearing loss with acute vertigo","means":"Possible AICA stroke (HINTS plus) or labyrinthine infarct"},{"finding":"Pure vertical or pure torsional nystagmus","means":"Central cause"}],"errors":[{"error":"Using HINTS in patients without nystagmus or with episodic vertigo","result":"A normal head impulse is expected and is wrongly interpreted as central","fix":"Use HINTS only in acute continuous vertigo with nystagmus"},{"error":"Making large slow head movements","result":"Smooth pursuit compensates; corrective saccade missed","fix":"Use small, rapid, unpredictable thrusts of 10 to 20 degrees"},{"error":"Watching the head instead of the eyes","result":"Missed corrective saccade","fix":"Watch the eyes during and immediately after the thrust"},{"error":"Testing nystagmus at extreme gaze","result":"False positive from physiological end-point nystagmus","fix":"Test at about 30 degrees of gaze"},{"error":"Ignoring new hearing loss and gait","result":"Missed AICA stroke or central cause","fix":"Add hearing test and gait assessment"}],"trouble":[{"problem":"I can't see a corrective saccade","fix":"Ensure the thrust is rapid and small, and the patient fixates on your nose. Watch from slightly in front. Use a phone video to replay in slow motion."},{"problem":"The patient's neck is stiff or painful","fix":"Relax the neck first with gentle side to side movement. If not possible, rely on nystagmus, skew, hearing and gait, and have a lower threshold for imaging."},{"problem":"I'm not confident in my HINTS interpretation","fix":"Treat as possibly central, especially with vascular risk factors, and arrange stroke assessment and MRI; GRACE-3 recommends HINTS only by clinicians trained in it."}],"pearls":["An abnormal head impulse is reassuring; a normal head impulse in AVS is worrying.","HINTS applies only to acute continuous vertigo with nystagmus.","Early MRI can miss posterior circulation strokes in the first 24 to 48 hours; HINTS performed by experts was more sensitive than early MRI in Kattah's study.","Patients unable to walk unaided need neuroimaging regardless of HINTS."],"evidence":[{"stat":"HINTS sensitivity 100%, specificity 96% for stroke in AVS when performed by neuro-ophthalmologists; early MRI-DWI was falsely negative in 12%","src":"Kattah et al 2009, Stroke"},{"stat":"Meta-analysis: in neurologists’ and neuro-ophthalmologists’ hands HINTS had sensitivity 96.7% and specificity 94.8% for stroke; in the one study of emergency physicians, sensitivity was 83.3% and specificity 43.8%. Technique and patient selection matter.","src":"Ohle et al 2020, Academic Emergency Medicine"},{"stat":"GRACE-3 guideline recommends HINTS for AVS only by clinicians with appropriate training","src":"Edlow et al 2023, Academic Emergency Medicine (GRACE-3)"}],"document":"Normal (peripheral): 'AVS: head impulse abnormal to the left (corrective saccade), unidirectional right-beating horizontal-torsional nystagmus, no skew, hearing equal, walks unaided. HINTS peripheral, consistent with left vestibular neuritis.' Abnormal: 'AVS: head impulse normal bilaterally, direction-changing nystagmus, skew on alternate cover. HINTS central, stroke pathway.'","hook":"In acute continuous vertigo, a normal head impulse is the dangerous one.","mcq":[{"q":"A patient with brief positional vertigo, no spontaneous nystagmus, has a normal head impulse. The doctor concludes this suggests stroke. What is wrong?","opts":["HINTS is valid only in acute continuous vertigo with nystagmus","Head impulse is not part of HINTS","Nothing","Head impulse should be done lying down"],"a":0,"why":"In patients without AVS, a normal head impulse is expected and does not suggest stroke."},{"q":"In AVS, the head impulse shows a corrective saccade to the left, nystagmus beats right in all gaze directions, but the patient reports new left hearing loss. What should be done?","opts":["Discharge with vestibular neuritis","Perform an Epley manoeuvre","Treat as possible AICA stroke and arrange urgent imaging","Repeat HINTS tomorrow"],"a":2,"why":"New hearing loss in AVS raises concern for AICA stroke (HINTS plus), even with an otherwise peripheral HINTS."},{"q":"How should the head impulse be performed?","opts":["Large slow head rotations","Rotate the head 90 degrees each side","Ask the patient to turn their head voluntarily","Small, rapid, unpredictable thrusts of 10 to 20 degrees while the patient fixates on your nose"],"a":3,"why":"Rapid small thrusts stimulate the canal and expose a corrective saccade; slow movements allow compensation."}],"src":["Kattah JC et al 2009, Stroke","Ohle R et al 2020, Academic Emergency Medicine","Edlow JA et al 2023, Academic Emergency Medicine (GRACE-3)","Newman-Toker DE et al 2013, Academic Emergency Medicine"]},{"id":"meningism","name":"Meningism: neck stiffness, Kernig, Brudzinski, jolt","group":"Meninges and functional","signs":["kernig","neck-stiffness"],"purpose":"Detects meningeal irritation from meningitis or subarachnoid haemorrhage. Use in fever with headache, altered mental state or suspected SAH, remembering that these signs are insensitive and cannot rule out meningitis.","kit":["No instruments","Patient lying supine on a flat couch with one pillow or none"],"setup":[{"do":"Patient supine, relaxed, with no pillow or one thin pillow, neck exposed.","why":"A thick pillow already flexes the neck; relaxation is needed to distinguish meningeal resistance from voluntary guarding."},{"do":"Explain each manoeuvre and stop if severe pain.","why":"These tests provoke pain in meningitis; consent and gentle technique maintain trust."}],"steps":[{"do":"Neck stiffness: place your hands under the occiput and gently lift the head to flex the neck, bringing the chin toward the chest.","why":"Inflamed meninges resist stretch, causing reflex spasm of the neck extensors.","cue":"Normal: chin touches or nearly touches the chest without resistance."},{"do":"Then gently rotate the head side to side.","why":"Meningeal irritation mainly restricts flexion, while rotation is relatively preserved; restriction in all directions suggests cervical spondylosis or parkinsonism.","cue":"You can state whether rotation is free."},{"do":"Brudzinski neck sign: during passive neck flexion, watch the hips and knees.","why":"Stretching inflamed meninges triggers involuntary hip and knee flexion to relieve tension on the nerve roots.","cue":"Positive: involuntary flexion of hips and knees."},{"do":"Kernig sign: flex the hip to 90 degrees and the knee to 90 degrees, then extend the knee slowly. Repeat on the other side.","why":"Stretching the lumbosacral nerve roots through inflamed meninges causes pain and hamstring spasm.","cue":"Positive: resistance or pain in the back or posterior thigh that prevents extension beyond about 135 degrees."},{"do":"Jolt accentuation: ask the patient to rotate the head horizontally (side to side as if saying no) two to three times per second.","why":"The rapid motion jolts the inflamed meninges and worsens headache.","cue":"Positive: worsening of an existing headache."},{"do":"Interpret signs in context of the whole clinical picture.","why":"These signs are insensitive; absence does not exclude meningitis, so lumbar puncture and antibiotics should not be delayed on the basis of negative signs.","cue":"You record findings but base management on overall risk."}],"normal":"Neck flexes fully without resistance, rotation is free, Kernig and Brudzinski negative, and jolt accentuation does not worsen headache.","abnormal":[{"finding":"Resistance to neck flexion with free rotation","means":"Meningeal irritation: meningitis or SAH"},{"finding":"Resistance to flexion and rotation","means":"Cervical spondylosis, parkinsonism, or local neck pain"},{"finding":"Positive Kernig or Brudzinski","means":"Meningeal irritation; highly specific but very insensitive"},{"finding":"Jolt accentuation positive","means":"Supports meningitis, but low sensitivity"}],"errors":[{"error":"Using thick pillows","result":"Neck flexion limited before testing; misleading","fix":"Use one thin pillow or none"},{"error":"Forcing neck flexion","result":"Pain and voluntary guarding","fix":"Gently lift the head and stop at resistance"},{"error":"Relying on negative signs to exclude meningitis","result":"Missed meningitis and delayed treatment","fix":"Recognise low sensitivity and act on clinical suspicion"},{"error":"Testing jolt in patients without headache or with reduced consciousness","result":"Invalid test","fix":"Use jolt accentuation only in alert patients with headache"}],"trouble":[{"problem":"The patient has neck pain from arthritis","fix":"Check rotation; if rotation is restricted too, it is likely mechanical. Use other signs and clinical judgement."},{"problem":"The patient is confused or agitated","fix":"Signs are less reliable; base decisions on risk, and do not delay antibiotics or LP."}],"pearls":["Absence of fever, neck stiffness and altered mental state makes meningitis unlikely in adults, but a single negative sign is not enough.","Kernig and Brudzinski are specific but very insensitive.","Neck stiffness in meningitis mainly restricts flexion; free rotation helps distinguish it from mechanical causes.","Jolt accentuation was initially reported as sensitive, but later studies found lower sensitivity."],"evidence":[{"stat":"Kernig sensitivity 5%, specificity 95%; Brudzinski sensitivity 5%, specificity 95%; nuchal rigidity sensitivity 30%, specificity 68% for CSF pleocytosis","src":"Thomas et al 2002, Clinical Infectious Diseases"},{"stat":"Jolt accentuation: pooled sensitivity 21%, specificity 82% for meningitis","src":"Nakao et al 2014, American Journal of Emergency Medicine"},{"stat":"Absence of fever, neck stiffness and altered mental status effectively excludes meningitis in adults (very high sensitivity of the combination)","src":"Attia et al 1999, JAMA Rational Clinical Examination"}],"document":"Normal: 'No neck stiffness; Kernig and Brudzinski negative; jolt accentuation negative.' Abnormal: 'Neck stiffness on flexion with free rotation; Kernig positive bilaterally; jolt accentuation positive.'","hook":"Meningeal signs are true when present and silent when absent; never let them overrule suspicion.","mcq":[{"q":"A febrile patient with headache has negative Kernig and Brudzinski signs. The doctor decides meningitis is excluded. What is the problem?","opts":["These signs are highly sensitive","These signs are insensitive; negative signs do not exclude meningitis","Kernig is only positive in SAH","Nothing"],"a":1,"why":"Kernig and Brudzinski have very low sensitivity; they cannot rule out meningitis."},{"q":"A patient has restricted neck flexion and rotation with normal temperature. What is most likely?","opts":["Mechanical neck pain or cervical spondylosis","Bacterial meningitis","Subarachnoid haemorrhage","Encephalitis"],"a":0,"why":"Restriction in all directions suggests mechanical causes; meningism mainly restricts flexion."},{"q":"How is the jolt accentuation test performed?","opts":["Flex the neck passively","Extend the knee with hip flexed","Ask the patient to bend forward","Ask the patient to rotate the head horizontally 2 to 3 times per second and see if headache worsens"],"a":3,"why":"Jolt accentuation involves rapid horizontal head rotation and is positive if headache worsens."}],"src":["Thomas KE et al 2002, Clinical Infectious Diseases","Nakao JH et al 2014, American Journal of Emergency Medicine","Attia J et al 1999, JAMA","Macleod's Clinical Examination 15e"]},{"id":"hoover","name":"Hoover's sign and hip abductor sign","group":"Meninges and functional","signs":["hoover"],"purpose":"Positive signs of functional (non-organic) leg weakness: they demonstrate that hip extension or abduction is normal when the patient's attention is diverted. Use in leg weakness that is inconsistent with organic patterns.","kit":["No instruments","Patient lying supine on a couch"],"setup":[{"do":"Patient supine, legs straight, relaxed. Stand at the foot of the couch.","why":"Supine position allows you to place hands under both heels and feel hip extension."},{"do":"Explain neutrally: 'I'm going to test the strength of your legs in different ways.'","why":"A non-judgemental approach avoids implying deception and allows you to show the sign later as a positive explanation."}],"steps":[{"do":"Place your hand under the heel of the weak leg and ask the patient to push down into your hand (voluntary hip extension).","why":"This measures voluntary effort in hip extension of the weak leg.","cue":"Weak or absent push."},{"do":"Keeping your hand under the weak heel, place your other hand on the thigh of the good leg and ask the patient to lift the good leg against your resistance (hip flexion).","why":"Hip flexion of one leg automatically recruits hip extension of the other leg for stability; this is an involuntary synergy.","cue":"You feel the weak heel push down into your hand."},{"do":"Compare voluntary hip extension with involuntary hip extension.","why":"In functional weakness, involuntary extension is normal while voluntary extension is weak. In organic weakness, both are weak.","cue":"Positive Hoover's sign: involuntary extension stronger than voluntary."},{"do":"Hip abductor sign: place your hands on the lateral side of both legs. Ask the patient to abduct both legs against resistance, then abduct one leg at a time while feeling the opposite leg.","why":"Abduction of one leg involuntarily recruits abduction of the other leg for pelvic stability.","cue":"In functional weakness, when abducting the healthy leg, the weak leg abducts strongly; when trying to abduct the weak leg, the healthy leg does not fix."},{"do":"Present the result respectfully: show the patient how the leg pushes down when the attention is elsewhere, explaining that this shows the connection is intact and the problem is with how the brain sends signals, which can improve.","why":"Demonstrating the sign provides positive evidence of a functional disorder and can be therapeutic when explained as a real, treatable problem.","cue":"The patient understands that the weakness is real but potentially reversible."}],"normal":"Voluntary and involuntary hip extension are equal; abduction tests show consistent strength.","abnormal":[{"finding":"Voluntary hip extension weak but involuntary extension normal","means":"Positive Hoover's sign: functional weakness"},{"finding":"Weak leg abducts strongly when abducting the healthy leg but does not move voluntarily","means":"Positive hip abductor sign: functional weakness"},{"finding":"Both voluntary and involuntary hip extension weak","means":"Organic weakness"}],"errors":[{"error":"Presenting Hoover's sign as proof of faking","result":"Damaged trust and poor engagement","fix":"Explain that functional weakness is real and treatable"},{"error":"Not comparing voluntary and involuntary strength carefully","result":"Missed or false Hoover's sign","fix":"Keep the hand under the heel throughout and compare"},{"error":"Relying on Hoover's sign alone","result":"Missed coexisting organic disease","fix":"Perform a full neurological examination and use other functional signs"},{"error":"Testing in patients with hip pain or neglect","result":"False positives","fix":"Consider pain, neglect and cortical lesions as confounders"}],"trouble":[{"problem":"I'm not sure if the involuntary push is strong","fix":"Repeat with the patient lifting the good leg harder; feel for a clear downward push. Compare to the good leg's involuntary push when lifting the weak leg."},{"problem":"The patient becomes upset","fix":"Explain that the test shows the leg can work and that this is a common, treatable condition. Offer information and follow-up."}],"pearls":["Hoover's sign is a positive sign of functional weakness, not a diagnosis of exclusion.","Demonstrating the sign to the patient can help them understand the diagnosis.","False positives can occur with pain, cortical lesions and neglect.","Functional and organic disorders can coexist."],"evidence":[{"stat":"Hoover's sign has high specificity but moderate sensitivity for functional weakness across studies, with occasional false positives","src":"Daum, Hubschmid and Aybek 2014, Journal of Neurology, Neurosurgery and Psychiatry"},{"stat":"The hip abductor sign was described as a reliable sign distinguishing functional from organic paresis","src":"Sonoo 2004, Journal of Neurology, Neurosurgery and Psychiatry"}],"document":"Normal: 'Hoover's sign negative; hip abductor sign negative.' Abnormal: 'Hoover's sign positive on the left: weak voluntary hip extension, normal involuntary extension on contralateral hip flexion. Hip abductor sign positive. Findings explained to patient.'","hook":"Feel the heel push when the mind is elsewhere and show the patient the leg can work.","mcq":[{"q":"A doctor finds weak voluntary hip extension on the left. When the patient lifts the right leg against resistance, the left heel pushes down strongly. What is this?","opts":["Organic weakness","Positive Hoover's sign, suggesting functional weakness","Cerebellar ataxia","Hip arthritis"],"a":1,"why":"Involuntary hip extension stronger than voluntary extension is a positive Hoover's sign."},{"q":"What is the best way to present a positive Hoover's sign to the patient?","opts":["Tell them they are faking","Avoid mentioning it","Show them the leg can work when their attention is elsewhere, explaining it is a real, treatable problem","Refer them to psychiatry immediately"],"a":2,"why":"Showing the sign respectfully explains functional weakness and supports engagement with treatment."},{"q":"In the hip abductor sign, what suggests functional weakness?","opts":["The weak leg abducts strongly when the healthy leg abducts, but does not move voluntarily","Both legs weak to abduction","The healthy leg cannot abduct","Pain on abduction"],"a":0,"why":"Involuntary abduction of the weak leg during healthy leg abduction indicates functional weakness."}],"src":["Stone J, Carson A, Sharpe M 2005, Journal of Neurology, Neurosurgery and Psychiatry","Daum C, Hubschmid M, Aybek S 2014, Journal of Neurology, Neurosurgery and Psychiatry","Sonoo M 2004, Journal of Neurology, Neurosurgery and Psychiatry"]}];
